(10896, 'black hearted', 'Having a wicked malignant disposition morally bad'),
(10897, 'black hole', 'A dungeon or dark cell in a prison a military lock up or guardroom    now commonly with allusion to the cell the Black Hole in a fort at Calcutta called the Black Hole of Calcutta into which 146 English prisoners were thrust by the nabob Suraja Dowla on the night of June 20 1765 and in which 123 of the prisoners died before morning from lack of air'),
(10898, 'blacking', 'Any preparation for making things black esp one for giving a black luster to boots and shoes or to stoves'),
