(15138, 'Carpologist', 'One who describes fruits one versed in carpology'),
(15139, 'Carpology', 'That branch of botany which relates to the structure of seeds and fruit'),
(15140, 'Carpophagous', 'Living on fruits fruit consuming'),
(15141, 'Carpophore', 'A slender prolongation of the receptacle as an axis between the carpels as in Geranium and many umbelliferous plants'),
(15142, 'Carpophyll', 'A leaf converted into a fruit or a constituent portion of a fruit a carpel See Illust of Gymnospermous'),
(15143, 'Carpophyte', 'A flowerless plant which forms a true fruit as the result of fertilization as the red seaweeds the Ascomycetes etc'),
(15144, 'Carpospore', 'A kind of spore formed in the conceptacles of red alg'),
(15145, 'carposporic', 'of or relating to a carpospore'),
(15146, 'Carpus', 'The wrist the bones or cartilages between the forearm or antibrachium and the hand or forefoot in man consisting of eight short bones disposed in two rows'),
(15147, 'Carrack', 'See Carack'),
(15148, 'Carrageen', 'A small purplish branching cartilaginous seaweed Chondrus crispus which when bleached is the Irish moss of commerce'),
(15149, 'Carrageenan', 'a colloidal material obtained from seaweed or Irish moss used as an thickening or emulsifying agent and for stabilizing foods cosmetics and pharmaceuticals'),
(15150, 'Carrancha', 'The Brazilian kite Polyborus Brasiliensis    so called in imitation of its notes'),
(15151, 'Carraway', 'See Caraway'),
(15152, 'Carrel', 'See Quarrel an arrow'),
(15153, 'Carriable', 'Capable of being carried'),
(15154, 'Carriage', 'That which is carried burden baggage'),
(15155, 'Carriageable', 'Passable by carriages that can be conveyed in carriages'),
(15156, 'carriageway', 'one of the two sides of a motorway where traffic travels in one direction only usually in two or three lanes'),
(15157, 'carriage bolt', 'a type of bolt threaded only at the end opposite the head used mostly for fastening pieces of timber together and inserted into pre drilled holes'),
(15158, 'carriage house', 'a small building usually near a large residence or part of an estate used for keeping coaches carriages or other vehicles    also called coach house  It is now 1998 obsolescent and its function has been taken over by the garage which is usually attached to a residence or main building  Carriage houses are still found on older estates though not usually used for their original purpose'),
(15159, 'Carriboo', 'See Caribou'),
(15160, 'Carrick', 'A carack See Carack'),
(15161, 'Carrier', 'One who or that which carries or conveys a messenger'),
(15162, 'Carrion', 'The dead and putrefying body or flesh of an animal flesh so corrupted as to be unfit for food'),
(15163, 'Carrol', 'See 4th Carol'),
(15164, 'Carrom', 'See Carom'),
(15165, 'Carromata', 'In the Philippines a light two wheeled boxlike vehicle usually drawn by a single native pony and used to convey passengers within city limits or for traveling It is the common public carriage'),
(15166, 'Carronade', 'A kind of short cannon formerly in use designed to throw a large projectile with small velocity used for the purpose of breaking or smashing in rather than piercing the object aimed at as the side of a ship It has no trunnions but is supported on its carriage by a bolt passing through a loop on its under side'),
(15167, 'Carron oil', 'A lotion of linseed oil and lime water used as an application to burns and scalds    first used at the Carron iron works in Scotland'),
(15168, 'Carrot', 'An umbelliferous biennial plant Daucus Carota of many varieties'),
(15169, 'Carroty', 'Like a carrot in color or in taste    an epithet given to reddish yellow hair etc'),
(15170, 'Carrow', 'A strolling gamester'),
(15171, 'Carry', 'To convey or transport in any manner from one place to another to bear    often with away or off'),
(15172, 'Carryall', 'A light covered carriage having four wheels and seats for four or more persons usually drawn by one horse'),
(15173, 'Carrying', 'The act or business of transporting from one place to another'),
(15174, 'Carryk', 'A carack'),
(15175, 'Carrytale', 'A talebearer'),
(15176, 'Carse', 'Low fertile land a river valley'),
(15177, 'carsick', 'feeling nauseous due to the movement of a car or other land vehicle    similar to airsick and seasick'),
(15178, 'Cart', 'A common name for various kinds of vehicles as a Scythian dwelling on wheels or a chariot'),
(15179, 'Cartage', 'The act of carrying in a cart'),
(15180, 'Cartbote', 'Wood to which a tenant is entitled for making and repairing carts and other instruments of husbandry'),
(15181, 'Carte', 'Bill of fare'),
(15182, 'Carte blanche', 'A blank paper with a persons signature etc at the bottom given to another person with permission to superscribe what conditions he pleases Hence Unconditional terms unlimited authority'),
(15183, 'Carte de visite', 'A visiting card'),
(15184, 'Cartel', 'To defy or challenge'),
(15185, 'Carter', 'A charioteer'),
(15186, 'Cartesian', 'Of or pertaining to the French philosopher Ren'),
(15187, 'Cartesianism', 'The philosophy of Descartes'),
(15188, 'Carthaginian', 'Of a pertaining to ancient Carthage a city of northern Africa'),
(15189, 'Carthamin', 'A red coloring matter obtained from the safflower or Carthamus tinctorius'),
(15190, 'carthorse', 'a horse kept for pulling carts a horse bred or used for drawing heavy loads'),
(15191, 'Carthusian', 'A member of an exceeding austere religious order founded at Chartreuse in France by St Bruno in the year 1086'),
(15192, 'Cartilage', 'A translucent elastic tissue gristle'),
(15193, 'Cartilagineous', 'See Cartilaginous'),
(15194, 'Cartilaginification', 'The act or process of forming cartilage'),
(15195, 'Cartilaginous', 'Of or pertaining to cartilage gristly firm and tough like cartilage'),
(15196, 'Cartist', 'In Spain and Portugal one who supports the constitution'),
(15197, 'cartload', 'as much as will fill or load a cart the quantity that a cart holds In excavating and carting sand gravel earth etc one third of a cubic yard of the material before it is loosened is estimated to be a cart load'),
(15198, 'Cartman', 'One who drives or uses a cart a teamster a carter'),
(15199, 'Cartogram', 'A map showing geographically by shades or curves statistics of various kinds a statistical map'),
(15200, 'Cartographer', 'One who makes charts or maps'),
(15201, 'Cartographic', 'Of or pertaining to cartography'),
(15202, 'Cartographically', 'By cartography'),
(15203, 'Cartography', 'The art or business of forming charts or maps'),
(15204, 'Cartomancy', 'The art of telling fortunes with cards'),
(15205, 'Carton', 'Pasteboard for paper boxes also a pasteboard box'),
(15206, 'cartonful', 'the quantity contained in a carton'),
(15207, 'Cartoonist', 'One skilled in drawing cartoons'),
(15208, 'Cartridge', 'A complete charge for a firearm contained in or held together by a case capsule or shell of metal pasteboard or other material'),
(15209, 'cartroad', 'any road or path affording passage especially a rough one'),
(15210, 'Cartway', 'A way or road for carts'),
(15211, 'cartwheel', 'the type of wheel used on a cart it typically has wooden spokes and a metal rim'),
(15212, 'Cartwright', 'An artificer who makes carts a cart maker'),
(15213, 'Carucate', 'A plowland as much land as one team can plow in a year and a day    by some said to be about 100 acres'),
(15214, 'Caruncle', 'A small fleshy prominence or excrescence especially the small reddish body the caruncula lacrymalis in the inner angle of the eye'),
(15215, 'Caruncular', 'Of pertaining to or like a caruncle furnished with caruncles'),
(15216, 'Carunculate', 'Having a caruncle or caruncles caruncular'),
(15217, 'Carus', 'Coma with complete insensibility deep lethargy'),
(15218, 'Carvacrol', 'A thick oily liquid C10H13OH of a strong taste and disagreeable odor obtained from oil of caraway Carum carui'),
(15219, 'Carve', 'To cut'),
(15220, 'carved', 'formed by carving or having a design carved into the surface'),
(15221, 'Carvel', 'Same as Caravel'),
(15222, 'Carvelbuilt', 'Having the planks meet flush at the seams instead of lapping as in a clinker built vessel'),
(15223, 'Carven', 'Wrought by carving ornamented by carvings carved'),
(15224, 'Carvene', 'An oily substance C10H16 extracted from oil caraway'),
(15225, 'Carver', 'One who carves one who shapes or fashions by carving or as by carving esp one who carves decorative forms architectural adornments etc'),
(15226, 'Carving', 'The act or art of one who carves'),
(15227, 'Carvist', 'A hawk which is of proper age and training to be carried on the hand a hawk in its first year'),
(15228, 'Carvol', 'One of a species of aromatic oils resembling carvacrol'),
(15229, 'Car wheel', 'A flanged wheel of a railway car or truck'),
(15230, 'Caryatic', 'Of or pertaining to a caryatid'),
(15231, 'Caryatid', 'A draped female figure supporting an entablature in the place of a column or pilaster'),
(15232, 'Caryatides', 'Caryatids'),
(15233, 'Caryophyllaceous', 'Having corollas of five petals with long claws inclosed in a tubular calyx as the pink'),
(15234, 'Caryophyllales', 'an order of plants which corresponds approximately to the older group Centrospermae'),
(15235, 'Caryophyllidae', 'a group of families of mostly flowers having basal or free central placentation and trinucleate pollen binucleate pollen is commoner in flowering plants it contains 14 families including Caryophyllaceae carnations and pinks Aizoaceae Amaranthaceae Batidaceae Chenopodiaceae Cactaceae order Opuntiales Nyctaginaceae Phytolaccaceae it corresponds approximately to order Caryophyllales it is sometimes classified as a superorder'),
(15236, 'Caryophyllin', 'A tasteless and odorless crystalline substance extracted from cloves polymeric with common camphor'),
(15237, 'Caryophyllous', 'Caryophyllaceous'),
(15238, 'Caryopsis', 'A one celled dry indehiscent fruit with a thin membranous pericarp adhering closely to the seed so that fruit and seed are incorporated in one body forming a single grain as of wheat barley etc'),
(15239, 'Casa', 'A house or mansion'),
(15240, 'casaba', 'a winter melon having a yellowish rind    called also casaba melon'),
(15241, 'Casal', 'Of or pertaining to case as a casal ending'),
(15242, 'Casanova', 'an Italian adventurer Giovanni Giacomo Casanova b 1725 d 1798 who wrote vivid accounts of his sexual encounters'),
(15243, 'casava', 'any of several plants of the genus Manihot having fleshy roots yielding a nutritious starch'),
(15244, 'Cascabel', 'The projection in rear of the breech of a cannon usually a knob or breeching loop connected with the gun by a neck In old writers it included all in rear of the base ring See Illust of Cannon'),
(15245, 'Cascade', 'A fall of water over a precipice as in a river or brook a waterfall less than a cataract'),
(15246, 'Cascade method', 'A method of attaining successively lower temperatures by utilizing the cooling effect of the expansion of one gas in condensing another less easily liquefiable and so on'),
(15247, 'Cascade system', 'A system or method of connecting and operating two induction motors so that the primary circuit of one is connected to the secondary circuit of the other the primary circuit of the latter being connected to the source of supply also a system of electric traction in which motors so connected are employed The cascade system is also called tandem system or concatenated system the connection a cascade connection tandem connection or concatenated connection or a concatenation and the control of the motors so obtained a tandem control or concatenation control'),
(15248, 'Cascalho', 'A deposit of pebbles gravel and ferruginous sand in which the Brazilian diamond is usually found'),
(15249, 'cascara', 'the dried bark of the cascara buckthorn used as a laxative    called also cascara sagrada'),
(15250, 'Cascara buckthorn', 'The buckthorn Rhamnus Purshiana of the Pacific coast of the United States which yields cascara sagrada'),
(15251, 'Cascara sagrada', 'Holy bark the bark of the California buckthorn Rhamnus Purshianus used as a mild cathartic or laxative    called also cascara'),
(15252, 'Cascarilla', 'A euphorbiaceous West Indian shrub Croton Eleutheria also its aromatic bark'),
(15253, 'Cascarillin', 'A white crystallizable bitter substance extracted from oil of cascarilla'),
(15254, 'Cascaron', 'Lit an eggshell hence an eggshell filled with confetti to be thrown during balls carnivals etc'),
(15255, 'Case', 'To cover or protect with or as with a case to inclose'),
(15256, 'caseate', 'to turn into cheese    of milk'),
(15257, 'Caseation', 'A degeneration of animal tissue into a cheesy or curdy mass'),
(15258, 'Case bay', 'The space between two principals or girders'),
(15259, 'casebook', 'a book in which detailed written records of cases are kept and which are a source of information for subsequent work  Such books are often used as supplements to texts in law schools'),
(15260, 'cased', 'covered or protected with or as if with a case as knights cased in steel'),
(15261, 'caseful', 'the quantity contained in a case'),
(15262, 'Caseharden', 'To subject to a process which converts the surface of iron into steel'),
(15263, 'Casehardened', 'Having the surface hardened as iron tools'),
(15264, 'Casehardening', 'The act or process of converting the surface of iron into steel'),
(15265, 'Caseic', 'Of or pertaining to cheese as caseic acid');
INSERT INTO `dictionary` (`id`, `word`, `definition`) VALUES
(15266, 'Casein', 'A proteid substance present in both the animal and the vegetable kingdom In the animal kingdom it is chiefly found in milk and constitutes the main part of the curd separated by rennet in the vegetable kingdom it is found more or less abundantly in the seeds of leguminous plants Its reactions resemble those of alkali albumin'),
(15267, 'Case knife', 'A knife carried in a sheath or case'),
(15268, 'Casemated', 'Furnished with protected by or built like a casemate'),
(15269, 'Casement', 'A window sash opening on hinges affixed to the upright side of the frame into which it is fitted Poetically A window'),
(15270, 'Casemented', 'Having a casement or casements'),
(15271, 'Caseose', 'A soluble product proteose formed in the gastric and pancreatic digestion of casein and caseinogen'),
(15272, 'Caseous', 'Of pertaining to or resembling cheese having the qualities of cheese cheesy'),
(15273, 'Casern', 'A lodging for soldiers in garrison towns usually near the rampart barracks'),
(15274, 'Case shot', 'A collection of small projectiles inclosed in a case or canister'),
(15275, 'Case system', 'The system of teaching law in which the instruction is primarily a historical and inductive study of leading or selected cases with or without the use of textbooks for reference and collateral reading'),
(15276, 'Caseum', 'Same as Casein'),
(15277, 'Caseworm', 'A worm or grub that makes for itself a case See Caddice'),
(15278, 'Cash', 'A place where money is kept or where it is deposited and paid out a money box'),
(15279, 'cashable', 'able to be converted into ready money as a cashable check cashable gambling chips'),
(15280, 'Cashbook', 'A book in which is kept a register of money received or paid out'),
(15281, 'cashbox', 'a box for holding cash'),
(15282, 'cashed', 'converted into currency    of financial instruments as a cashed check'),
(15283, 'Cashew', 'A tree Anacardium occidentale of the same family which the sumac It is native in tropical America but is now naturalized in all tropical countries Its fruit a kidney shaped nut grows at the extremity of an edible pear shaped hypocarp about three inches long'),
(15284, 'Cashier', 'One who has charge of money a cash keeper the officer who has charge of the payments and receipts moneys checks notes of a bank or a mercantile company'),
(15285, 'Cashierer', 'One who rejects discards or dismisses as a cashierer of monarchs'),
(15286, 'Cashiers check', 'A check drawn by a bank upon its own funds signed by the cashier'),
(15287, 'Cashmere', 'A rich stuff for shawls scarfs etc originally made in Cashmere from the soft wool found beneath the hair of the goats of Cashmere Tibet and the Himalayas Some cashmere of fine quality is richly embroidered for sale to Europeans'),
(15288, 'Cashmerette', 'A kind of dress goods made with a soft and glossy surface like cashmere'),
(15289, 'Cashoo', 'See Catechu'),
(15290, 'Cash railway', 'A form of cash carrier in which a small carrier or car travels upon a kind of track'),
(15291, 'Cash register', 'A device for recording the amount of cash received usually having an automatic adding machine and a money drawer and exhibiting the amount of the sale'),
(15292, 'Casing', 'The act or process of inclosing in or covering with a case or thin substance as plaster boards etc'),
(15293, 'Casings', 'Dried dung of cattle used as fuel'),
(15294, 'Casino', 'A small country house'),
(15295, 'casino hotel', 'a business establishment that combines a casino and a hotel'),
(15296, 'Cask', 'Same as Casque'),
(15297, 'Casket', 'A small chest or box esp of rich material or ornamental character as for jewels etc'),
(15298, 'caskful', 'the quantity a cask will hold'),
(15299, 'Casmerodius', 'a genus of white egrets'),
(15300, 'Casque', 'A piece of defensive or ornamental armor with or without a vizor for the head and neck a helmet'),
(15301, 'Cass', 'To render useless or void to quash to annul to reject to send away'),
(15302, 'Cassada', 'See Cassava'),
(15303, 'Cassareep', 'A condiment made from the sap of the bitter cassava Manihot utilissima deprived of its poisonous qualities concentrated by boiling and flavored with aromatics See Pepper pot'),
(15304, 'Cassate', 'To render void or useless to vacate or annul'),
(15305, 'Cassation', 'The act of annulling'),
(15306, 'Cassava', 'A shrubby euphorbiaceous plant of the genus Manihot with fleshy rootstocks yielding an edible starch    called also manioc'),
(15307, 'Cassava wood', 'A West Indian tree Turpinia occidentalis of the family Staphyleace'),
(15308, 'Cassel brown', 'A brown pigment of varying permanence consisting of impure lignite It was found originally near Cassel now Kassel Germany'),
(15309, 'Casse Paper', 'Broken paper the outside quires of a ream'),
(15310, 'Casserole', 'A small round dish with a handle usually of porcelain'),
(15311, 'Casse t', 'A small war club esp of savages    so called because of its supposed use in crushing the skull'),
(15312, 'Cassette', 'Same as Seggar'),
(15313, 'Cassia', 'A genus of leguminous plants herbs shrubs or trees of many species most of which have purgative qualities The leaves of several species furnish the senna used in medicine'),
(15314, 'Cassican', 'An American bird of the genus Cassicus allied to the starlings and orioles remarkable for its skillfully constructed and suspended nest the crested oriole The name is also sometimes given to the piping crow an Australian bird'),
(15315, 'Cassideous', 'Helmet shaped    applied to a corolla having a broad helmet shaped upper petal as in aconite'),
(15316, 'cassie', 'a type of tropical American thorny shrub or small tree Acacia farnesiana it bears fragrant yellow flowers used in making perfumery'),
(15317, 'Cassidony', 'The French lavender Lavandula St'),
(15318, 'Cassimere', 'A thin twilled woolen cloth used for mens garments'),
(15319, 'Cassinette', 'A cloth with a cotton warp and a woof of very fine wool or wool and silk'),
(15320, 'Cassinian ovals', 'See under Oval'),
(15321, 'Cassino', 'A game at cards played by two or more persons usually for twenty one points'),
(15322, 'Cassioberry', 'The fruit of the Viburnum obovatum a shrub which grows from Virginia to Florida'),
(15323, 'Cassiopeia', 'A constellation of the northern hemisphere situated between Cepheus and Perseus    so called in honor of the wife of Cepheus a fabulous king of Ethiopia'),
(15324, 'Cassiterite', 'Native tin dioxide tin stone a mineral occurring in tetragonal crystals of reddish brown color and brilliant adamantine luster also massive sometimes in compact forms with concentric fibrous structure resembling wood wood tin also in rolled fragments or pebbly Stream tin It is the chief source of metallic tin See Black tin under Black'),
(15325, 'Cassius', 'A brownish purple pigment obtained by the action of some compounds of tin upon certain salts of gold It is used in painting and staining porcelain and glass to give a beautiful purple color Commonly called Purple of Cassius'),
(15326, 'Cassocked', 'Clothed with a cassock'),
(15327, 'Cassolette', 'a box or vase with a perforated cover to emit perfumes'),
(15328, 'Cassonade', 'Raw sugar sugar not refined'),
(15329, 'Cassowary', 'A large bird of the genus Casuarius found in the east Indies It is smaller and stouter than the ostrich Its head is armed with a kind of helmet of horny substance consisting of plates overlapping each other and it has a group of long sharp spines on each wing which are used as defensive organs It is a shy bird and runs with great rapidity Other species inhabit New Guinea Australia etc'),
(15330, 'Cassumunar', 'A pungent bitter aromatic gingerlike root obtained from the East Indies'),
(15331, 'Cast', 'To send or drive by force to throw to fling to hurl to impel'),
(15332, 'Castalian', 'Of or pertaining to Castalia a mythical fountain of inspiration on Mt Parnassus sacred to the Muses'),
(15333, 'Castanea', 'A genus of nut bearing trees or shrubs including the chestnut and chinquapin'),
(15334, 'Castanet', 'See Castanets'),
(15335, 'Castanets', 'Two small concave shells of ivory or hard wood shaped like spoons fastened to the thumb and beaten together with the middle finger    used by the Spaniards and Moors as an accompaniment to their dance and guitars'),
(15336, 'Castanopsis', 'a genus of evergreen trees and shrubs of warm regions valued for their foliage found in southeastern US and eastern Australia and northern New Zealand'),
(15337, 'Castanospermum', 'a genus consisting of one species the Moreton Bay chestnut'),
(15338, 'Castaway', 'One who or that which is cast away or shipwrecked'),
(15339, 'Caste', 'One of the hereditary classes into which the Hindu are divided according to the laws of Brahmanism'),
(15340, 'Castellan', 'A governor or warden of a castle'),
(15341, 'Castellany', 'The lordship of a castle the extent of land and jurisdiction appertaining to a castle'),
(15342, 'Castellated', 'Inclosed within a building as a fountain or cistern castellated'),
(15343, 'Castellation', 'The act of making into a castle'),
(15344, 'Caster', 'One who casts as caster of stones etc  a caster of cannon a caster of accounts'),
(15345, 'Castigate', 'To punish by stripes to chastise by blows to chasten also to chastise verbally to reprove to criticise severely'),
(15346, 'Castigator', 'One who castigates or corrects'),
(15347, 'Castigatory', 'Punitive in order to amendment corrective'),
(15348, 'Castile soap', 'A kind of fine hard white or mottled soap made with olive oil and soda also a soap made in imitation of the above described soap'),
(15349, 'Castilian', 'An inhabitant or native of Castile in Spain'),
(15350, 'Castillan', 'Of or pertaining to Castile in Spain'),
(15351, 'Casting', 'The act of one who casts or throws as in fishing'),
(15352, 'Cast iron', 'Highly carbonized iron the direct product of the blast furnace    used for making castings and for conversion into wrought iron and steel It can not be welded or forged is brittle and sometimes very hard Besides carbon it contains sulphur phosphorus silica etc'),
(15353, 'Castle', 'A fortified residence especially that of a prince or nobleman a fortress'),
(15354, 'Castlebuilder', 'Fig one who builds castles in the air or forms visionary schemes'),
(15355, 'Castled', 'Having a castle or castles supporting a castle as a castled height or crag'),
(15356, 'Castle guard', 'The guard or defense of a castle'),
(15357, 'Castlery', 'The government of a castle'),
(15358, 'Castlet', 'A small castle'),
(15359, 'Castleward', 'Same as Castleguard'),
(15360, 'Castling', 'That which is cast or brought forth prematurely an abortion'),
(15361, 'Cast off', 'Cast or laid aside thrown away discarded as cast off clothes'),
(15362, 'Castor', 'A genus of rodents including the beaver See Beaver'),
(15363, 'Castor and Pollux', 'See Saint Elmos fire under Saint'),
(15364, 'Castor bean', 'The bean or seed of the castor oil plant Ricinus communis or Palma Christi'),
(15365, 'Castoreum', 'A peculiar bitter orange brown substance with strong penetrating odor found in two sacs between the anus and external genitals of the beaver castor    used in medicine as an antispasmodic and by perfumers'),
(15366, 'Castoridae', 'a natural family comprising the beavers'),
(15367, 'Castoroides', 'a genus of extinct beavers of the Pleistocene of eastern and southern US'),
(15368, 'Castorin', 'A white crystalline substance obtained from castoreum'),
(15369, 'Castor oil', 'A mild cathartic oil expressed or extracted from the seeds of the Ricinus communis or Palma Christi When fresh the oil is inodorous and insipid'),
(15370, 'Castrametation', 'The art or act of encamping the making or laying out of a camp'),
(15371, 'Castrate', 'To deprive of the testicles to emasculate to geld to alter'),
(15372, 'castrated', 'deprived of reproductive organs or sexual attributes'),
(15373, 'Castration', 'The act of castrating'),
(15374, 'Castrato', 'A male person castrated for the purpose of improving his voice for singing an artificial or male soprano'),
(15375, 'Castrel', 'See Kestrel'),
(15376, 'Castrensial', 'Belonging to a camp'),
(15377, 'Castrensian', 'Castrensial'),
(15378, 'Castries', 'The capital city of St Lucia  Population 2000  13600'),
(15379, 'Cast steel', 'See Cast steel under Steel'),
(15380, 'Casual', 'Happening or coming to pass without design and without being foreseen or expected accidental fortuitous coming by chance'),
(15381, 'Casualism', 'The doctrine that all things exist or are controlled by chance'),
(15382, 'Casualist', 'One who believes in casualism'),
(15383, 'Casually', 'Without design accidentally fortuitously by chance occasionally'),
(15384, 'Casualness', 'The quality of being casual'),
(15385, 'Casualty', 'That which comes without design or without being foreseen contingency'),
(15386, 'Casuaridae', 'a natural family of large ostrichlike birds the cassowaries'),
(15387, 'Casuariiformes', 'an order of ratite birds comprising the cassowaries and emus'),
(15388, 'Casuarina', 'A genus of leafless trees or shrubs with drooping branchlets of a rushlike appearance mostly natives of Australia Some of them are large producing hard and heavy timber of excellent quality called beefwood from its color'),
(15389, 'Casuarinaceae', 'a natural family of plants having only one genus Casuarina'),
(15390, 'Casuarinales', 'an order of chiefly Australian trees and shrubs comprising the casuarinas having only one family Casuarinaceae'),
(15391, 'Casuarius', 'the type and sole genus of the Casuaridae the cassowaries'),
(15392, 'Casuist', 'One who is skilled in or given to casuistry'),
(15393, 'Casuistic', 'Of or pertaining to casuists or casuistry as casuistic thinking'),
(15394, 'Casuistry', 'The science or doctrine of dealing with cases of conscience of resolving questions of right or wrong in conduct or determining the lawfulness or unlawfulness of what a man may do by rules and principles drawn from the Scriptures from the laws of society or the church or from equity and natural reason the application of general moral rules to particular cases'),
(15395, 'Casus', 'An event an occurrence an occasion a combination of circumstances a case an act of God See the Note under Accident'),
(15396, 'cat', 'Any animal belonging to the natural family Felidae and in particular to the various species of the genera Felis Panthera and Lynx  The domestic cat is Felis domestica  The European wild cat Felis catus is much larger than the domestic cat  In the United States the name wild cat is commonly applied to the bay lynx Lynx rufus  The larger felines such as the lion tiger leopard and cougar are often referred to as cats and sometimes as big cats  See Wild cat and Tiger cat'),
(15397, 'Cata', 'The Latin and English form of a Greek preposition used as a prefix to signify down downward under against contrary or opposed to wholly completely as in cataclysm catarrh It sometimes drops the final vowel as in catoptric and is sometimes changed to cath as in cathartic catholic'),
(15398, 'Catabaptist', 'One who opposes baptism especially of infants'),
(15399, 'Catabasion', 'A vault under altar of a Greek church'),
(15400, 'Catabiotic', 'See under Force'),
(15401, 'catabolic', 'Of or pertaining to catabolism as catabolic processes which give rise to substances katastates of decreasing complexity and increasing stability'),
(15402, 'catabolism', 'the breakdown of more complex substances into simpler ones with release of energy in living organisms destructive or downward metabolism    a form of metabolism opposed to anabolism See also Disassimilation'),
(15403, 'Catacala', 'a genus of moths whose larvae are cutworms underwings'),
