(51778, 'Jasey', 'A wig    so called perhaps from being made of or resembling Jersey yarn'),
(51779, 'Jashawk', 'A young hawk'),
(51780, 'Jasmine', 'A shrubby plant of the genus Jasminum bearing flowers of a peculiarly fragrant odor The Jasminum officinale common in the south of Europe bears white flowers The Arabian jasmine is Jasminum Sambac and with Jasminum angustifolia comes from the East Indies  The yellow false jasmine in the Gelseminum sempervirens see Gelsemium Several other plants are called jasmine in the West Indies as species of Calotropis and Faramea'),
(51781, 'Jason', 'the husband of Medea and leader of the Argonauts who sailed in quest of the Golden Fleece'),
(51782, 'Jasp', 'Jasper'),
(51783, 'Jaspachate', 'Agate jasper'),
(51784, 'Jasper', 'An opaque impure variety of quartz of red yellow and other dull colors breaking with a smooth surface It admits of a high polish and is used for vases seals snuff boxes etc  When the colors are in stripes or bands it is called striped jasper or banded jasper The Egyptian pebble is a brownish yellow jasper'),
(51785, 'Jasperated', 'mixed with jasper containing particles of jasper as jasperated agate'),
(51786, 'Jasperize', 'To convert into or make to resemble jasper'),
(51787, 'Jaspery', 'Of the nature of jasper mixed with jasper'),
(51788, 'Jaspidean', 'Consisting of jasper or containing jasper jaspery jasperlike'),
(51789, 'Jaspilite', 'A compact siliceous rock resembling jasper'),
(51790, 'Jaspoid', 'Resembling jasper'),
(51791, 'Jasponyx', 'An onyx part or all of whose layers consist of jasper'),
(51792, 'Jatrophic', 'Of or pertaining to physic nuts the seeds of plants of the genus Jatropha'),
(51793, 'Jaunce', 'To ride hard to jounce'),
(51794, 'Jaundice', 'A morbid condition characterized by yellowness of the eyes skin and urine whiteness of the feces constipation uneasiness in the region of the stomach loss of appetite and general languor and lassitude  It is caused usually by obstruction of the biliary passages and consequent damming up in the liver of the bile which is then absorbed into the blood'),
(51795, 'Jaundiced', 'Affected with jaundice'),
(51796, 'Jaunt', 'To jolt to jounce'),
(51797, 'Jauntily', 'In a jaunty manner'),
(51798, 'Jauntiness', 'The quality of being jaunty'),
(51799, 'Jaunty', 'Airy showy finical hence characterized by an affected or fantastical manner'),
(51800, 'Java', 'One of the islands of the Malay Archipelago belonging to the Netherlands'),
(51801, 'Javanese', 'Of or pertaining to Java or to the people of Java'),
(51802, 'Javel', 'A vagabond'),
(51803, 'Javelin', 'A sort of light spear to be thrown or cast by the hand anciently a weapon of war used by horsemen and foot soldiers now used chiefly in hunting the wild boar and other fierce game'),
(51804, 'Javelinier', 'A soldier armed with a javelin'),
(51805, 'Jaw', 'To scold to clamor'),
(51806, 'Jawbone', 'The bone of either jaw a maxilla or a mandible'),
(51807, 'Jawed', 'Having jaws    chiefly in composition as lantern jawed'),
(51808, 'Jaw fall', 'Depression of the jaw hence depression of spirits'),
(51809, 'Jaw fallen', 'Dejected chopfallen'),
(51810, 'Jawfoot', 'See Maxilliped'),
(51811, 'Jawing', 'Scolding clamorous or abusive talk'),
(51812, 'Jawn', 'See Yawn'),
(51813, 'Jawy', 'Relating to the jaws'),
(51814, 'Jay', 'Any one of the numerous species of birds belonging to Garrulus Cyanocitta and allied genera of the family Corvidae They are allied to the crows but are smaller more graceful in form often handsomely colored and usually have a crest'),
(51815, 'Jayet', 'See Jet'),
(51816, 'Jayhawker', 'A name given to a free booting unenlisted armed man or guerrilla'),
(51817, 'Jazel', 'A gem of an azure color'),
(51818, 'Jazerant', 'A coat of defense made of small plates of metal sewed upon linen or the like also this kind of armor taken generally as a coat of jazerant'),
(51819, 'jazz', 'A type of music that originated in New Orleans around 1900 and developed through increasingly complex styles but generally featuring intricate rhythms improvisation prominent solo segments and great freedom in harmonic idiom played frequently in a polyphonic style on various instruments including horn saxophone piano and percussion but rarely stringed instruments'),
