(53024, 'Knew', 'imp of Know'),
(53025, 'Knicker', 'A small ball of clay baked hard and oiled used as a marble by boys in playing'),
(53026, 'Knickerbocker', 'A linsey woolsey fabric having a rough knotted surface on the right side used for womens dresses'),
(53027, 'Knickerbockers', 'The name for a style of short breeches smallclothes called also knickers'),
(53028, 'knickers', 'The name for a style of loose fitting short trousers gathered in and ending at the knees smallclothes called also knickerbockers'),
(53029, 'Knickknack', 'A trifle or toy a bawble a gewgaw a tchotchke'),
(53030, 'Knickknackatory', 'A collection of knickknacks'),
(53031, 'Knickknackery', 'Knickknacks'),
(53032, 'Knife', 'An instrument consisting of a thin blade usually of steel and having a sharp edge for cutting fastened to a handle but of many different forms and names for different uses as table knife drawing knife putty knife pallet knife pocketknife penknife chopping knife etc'),
(53033, 'Knifeboard', 'A board on which knives are cleaned or polished'),
(53034, 'Knife edge', 'A piece of steel sharpened to an acute edge or angle and resting on a smooth surface serving as the axis of motion of a pendulum scale beam or other piece required to oscillate with the least possible friction'),
(53035, 'Knife switch', 'A switch consisting of one or more knifelike pieces hinged at one end and making contact near the other with flat gripping springs'),
(53036, 'Knight', 'A young servant or follower a military attendant'),
(53037, 'Knightage', 'The body of knights taken collectively'),
(53038, 'Knight bachelor', 'A knight of the most ancient but lowest order of English knights and not a member of any order of chivalry See Bachelor 4'),
(53039, 'Knight banneret', 'A knight who carried a banner who possessed fiefs to a greater amount than the knight bachelor and who was obliged to serve in war with a greater number of attendants The dignity was sometimes conferred by the sovereign in person on the field of battle'),
(53040, 'Knight baronet', 'See Baronet'),
(53041, 'Knight errant', 'A wandering knight a knight who traveled in search of adventures for the purpose of exhibiting military skill prowess and generosity'),
(53042, 'Knight errantry', 'The character or actions of wandering knights the practice of wandering in quest of adventures chivalry a quixotic or romantic adventure or scheme'),
(53043, 'Knight er ratic', 'Pertaining to a knight errant or to knight errantry'),
(53044, 'Knighthead', 'A bollard timber See under Bollard'),
(53045, 'Knighthood', 'The character dignity or condition of a knight or of knights as a class hence chivalry'),
(53046, 'Knightless', 'Unbecoming a knight'),
(53047, 'Knightliness', 'The character or bearing suitable for a knight chivalry'),
(53048, 'Knightly', 'Of or pertaining to a knight becoming a knight chivalrous as a knightly combat a knightly spirit'),
(53049, 'Knight marshal', 'An officer in the household of the British sovereign who has cognizance of transgressions within the royal household and verge and of contracts made there a member of the household being one of the parties'),
(53050, 'Knight service', 'The military service by rendering which a knight held his lands'),
(53051, 'Knights fee', 'The fee of a knight specif the amount of land the holding of which imposed the obligation of knight service being sometimes a hide1b or less sometimes six or more hides'),
(53052, 'Knight Templar', 'See Commandery n 3 and also Templar n 1 and 3'),
(53053, 'knish', 'A fried or sometimes baked turnover made from a round or square sheet of dough containing a filling usually of meat or potatoes'),
(53054, 'Knit', 'To form into a knot or into knots to tie together as cord to fasten by tying'),
(53055, 'Knitback', 'The plant comfrey    so called from its use as a restorative'),
(53056, 'Knitch', 'A number of things tied or knit together a bundle a fagot'),
(53057, 'Knits', 'Small particles of ore'),
(53058, 'Knitster', 'A woman who knits'),
(53059, 'Knitter', 'One who or that which knits joins or unites a knitting machine'),
(53060, 'Knitting', 'The work of a knitter the network formed by knitting knitwork'),
(53061, 'Knittle', 'A string that draws together a purse or bag'),
(53062, 'knitwear', 'knitted clothing'),
(53063, 'knitwork', 'A network of yarn created by interlacing threads of yarn in a series of connected loops using straight eyeless needles or by machine knitting'),
(53064, 'Knives', 'n pl of Knife See Knife'),
(53065, 'Knob', 'A hard protuberance a hard swelling or rising a bunch a lump as a knob in the flesh or on a bone'),
(53066, 'Knobbed', 'Containing knobs full of knobs ending in a nob See Illust of Antenna'),
(53067, 'Knobber', 'See Knobbler'),
(53068, 'Knobbing', 'Rough dressing by knocking off knobs or projections'),
(53069, 'Knobbler', 'The hart in its second year a young deer'),
(53070, 'Knobbling fire', 'A bloomery fire See Bloomery'),
(53071, 'Knobby', 'Full of or covered with knobs or hard protuberances'),
(53072, 'knobkerrie', 'A short wooden club with a knobbed end used as a missile weapon by Kafir and other native tribes of South Africa'),
(53073, 'Knobstick', 'One who refuses to join or withdraws from a trade union'),
(53074, 'Knock', 'To drive or be driven against something to strike against something to clash as one heavy body knocks against another'),
(53075, 'Knockabout', 'A small yacht generally from fifteen to twenty five feet in length having a mainsail and a jib a sloop with a simplified rig and no bowsprit  All knockabouts have ballast and either a keel or centerboard  The original type was twenty one feet in length  The next larger type is called a raceabout'),
(53076, 'Knock down', 'To strike down to fell to prostrate by a blow or by blows as to knock down an assailant'),
(53077, 'Knockdown', 'That which knocks one down something that overpowers or overwhelms as strong liquor specif a kind of ale or beer that is very strong'),
(53078, 'knock down and drag out', 'marked by extreme violence    of fights also used metaphorically of fierce contests as a knock down and drag out fight a knockdown dragout competition for the browser market'),
(53079, 'knocked out', 'damaged    not used of persons as the gym has some of the most knocked out equipment since Vic Tanny  Opposite of undamaged'),
(53080, 'knocker', 'One who or that which knocks specifically an instrument or kind of hammer fastened to a door to be used in seeking for admittance'),
(53081, 'Knocking', 'A beating a rap a series of raps'),
(53082, 'Knockings', 'Large lumps picked out of the sieve in dressing ore'),
(53083, 'knock knee', 'A condition in which the knees are bent in so as to touch each other in walking inknee'),
(53084, 'knock kneed', 'Having the legs bent inward so that the knees touch in walking'),
(53085, 'knock knees', 'knock knee'),
(53086, 'knock off', 'a device in a knitting machine to remove loops from the needles'),
(53087, 'knockoff', 'A cheap imitation of something popular produced illegally without a license from the trademark owner and of inferior materials'),
(53088, 'Knock out', 'That knocks out characterized by knocking out as a knock out blow a knock out key for knocking out a drill from a collet'),
(53089, 'knockout', 'Act of knocking out or state of being knocked out the act of rendering a person unconscious by a blow'),
(53090, 'knockout drops', 'Drops of any drug used to a person to fall asleep or become unconscious or stupefied for the purpose of robbery etc they are usually mixed into a drink so that the person consuming it does so unknowingly  One compound used for this purpose is chloral hydrate'),
(53091, 'Knockstone', 'A block upon which ore is broken up'),
(53092, 'Knoll', 'A little round hill a mound a small elevation of earth the top or crown of a hill'),
(53093, 'Knoller', 'One who tolls a bell'),
(53094, 'Knop', 'A knob a bud a bunch a button'),
(53095, 'Knopped', 'Having knops or knobs fastened as with buttons'),
(53096, 'Knoppern', 'A kind of gall produced by a gallfly on the cup of an acorn    used in tanning and dyeing'),
(53097, 'Knopweed', 'Same as Knapweed'),
(53098, 'Knor', 'See Knur'),
(53099, 'Knosp', 'Same as Knop 2'),
(53100, 'Knot', 'A fastening together of the parts or ends of one or more threads cords ropes etc by any one of various ways of tying or entangling'),
(53101, 'Knotberry', 'The cloudberry Rudus Cham'),
(53102, 'Knotgrass', 'a common weed with jointed stems Polygonum aviculare knotweed'),
(53103, 'Knotless', 'Free from knots without knots'),
(53104, 'Knotted', 'Full of knots having knots knurled as a knotted cord the knotted oak'),
(53105, 'Knottiness', 'The quality or state of being knotty or full of knots'),
(53106, 'Knotty', 'Full of knots knotted having many knots as knotty timber a knotty rope'),
(53107, 'Knotweed', 'See Knotgrass'),
(53108, 'Knotwort', 'A small herbaceous trailing plant of the genus Illecebrum Illecebrum verticillatum'),
(53109, 'Knout', 'A kind of whip for flogging criminals formerly much used in Russia The lash is a tapering bundle of leather thongs twisted with wire and hardened so that it mangles the flesh'),
(53110, 'Know', 'Knee'),
(53111, 'Knowable', 'That may be known capable of being discovered understood or ascertained'),
(53112, 'Knowa bleness', 'The state or quality of being knowable'),
(53113, 'Know all', 'One who knows everything hence one who makes pretension to great knowledge a wiseacre a know it all    usually ironical'),
(53114, 'Knower', 'One who knows'),
(53115, 'knowhow', 'the knowledge and skill required to do something practical knowledge for a specific task'),
(53116, 'Knowing', 'Skilful well informed intelligent as a knowing man a knowing dog'),
(53117, 'Knowingly', 'With knowledge in a knowing manner intelligently consciously deliberately as he would not knowingly offend'),
(53118, 'Knowingness', 'The state or quality of being knowing or intelligent shrewdness skillfulness'),
(53119, 'Know it all', 'One who knows everything'),
(53120, 'Knowleche', 'See Knowledge'),
(53121, 'Knowleching', 'Knowledge'),
(53122, 'Knowledge', 'To acknowledge'),
(53123, 'knowledgeable', 'thoroughly acquainted with and skilled in something through study or experience as knowledgeable in classical languages  Opposite of unversed unacquainted and unfamiliar'),
(53124, 'knowledge engineering', 'The application of computerized data and text manipulation to manage and interpret large bodies of knowledge or find useful information in large bodies of data  The study of methods for knowledge engineering is generally considered as a branch of artificial intelligence'),
(53125, 'knowledge intensive', 'Requiring access to and manipulation of large quantities of knowledge as knowledge intensive labor'),
(53126, 'knowledge worker', 'A person whose occupation is predominantly concerned with generating or interpreting information as contrasted with manual labor'),
(53127, 'Known', 'of Know'),
(53128, 'Know nothing', 'A member of a secret political organization in the United States the chief objects of which were the proscription of foreigners by the repeal of the naturalization laws and the exclusive choice of native Americans for office'),
(53129, 'Know nothingism', 'The doctrines principles or practices of the Know nothings'),
(53130, 'Knubs', 'Waste silk formed in winding off the threads from a cocoon'),
(53131, 'Knuckle', 'The joint of a finger particularly when made prominent by the closing of the fingers'),
(53132, 'Knuckled', 'Jointed'),
(53133, 'knuckle under', 'To yield to submit    usually used with to'),
(53134, 'knuckles', 'a small metal weapon worn over the knuckles on the back of the hand called also brass knuckles and knuckle duster'),
(53135, 'knucks', 'same as knuckles'),
(53136, 'Knuff', 'A lout a clown'),
(53137, 'Knur', 'A knurl'),
(53138, 'Knurl', 'A contorted knot in wood a crossgrained protuberance a nodule a boss or projection'),
(53139, 'Knurled', 'Full of knots gnarled'),
(53140, 'Knurly', 'Full of knots hard tough hence capable of enduring or resisting much'),
(53141, 'Knurry', 'Full of knots'),
(53142, 'KO', 'a knockout a blow that renders the opponent unconscious    used especially in boxing'),
(53143, 'Koaita', 'Same as Coaita'),
(53144, 'Koala', 'A tailless furry marsupial Phascolarctos cinereus found in Australia The female carries her young on the back of her neck Called also Australian bear koala bear native bear and native sloth  The koala lives almost all of its life in trees moves sluggishly like a sloth and eats eucalyptus leaves almost exclusively'),
(53145, 'Kob', 'Any one of several species of African antelopes of the genus Kobus esp the species Kobus sing sing'),
(53146, 'Kobalt', 'See Cobalt'),
(53147, 'Kobellite', 'A blackish gray mineral a sulphide of antimony bismuth and lead'),
(53148, 'Kobold', 'A kind of domestic spirit in German mythology corresponding to the Scottish brownie and the English Robin Goodfellow'),
(53149, 'KOd', 'rendered unconscious usually by a blow'),
(53150, 'Kodachrome', 'A brand of photographic transparency bearing a positive color image'),
(53151, 'Kodagu', 'A Dravidian language'),
(53152, 'Kodak', 'A kind of portable photographic camera esp adapted for snapshot work in which a succession of negatives is made upon a continuous roll of sensitized film    originally a trademark name of the Eastman Kodak Company but from early 1900s through the 1930s it was popularly applied to almost any hand camera'),
(53153, 'Kodiak', 'Same as Kodiak bear'),
(53154, 'Kodiak bear', 'A large brown bear Ursus middendorffi syn Ursus arctos middendorffi of coastal Alaska and British Columbia related to the grizzly bear called also Kodiak bear'),
(53155, 'Koel', 'Any one of several species of cuckoos of the genus Eudynamys found in India the East Indies and Australia  They deposit their eggs in the nests of other birds'),
(53156, 'Koellia', 'The genus comprising the American mountain mint synonymous with Pycnanthemum'),
(53157, 'Koff', 'A two masted Dutch vessel'),
(53158, 'Koftgari', 'Ornamental work produced by inlaying steel with gold    a variety of damascening much used in the arts of India'),
(53159, 'Kogia', 'The genus comprising the pygmy sperm whales'),
(53160, 'Kohinoor', 'A famous diamond surrendered to the British crown on the annexation of the Punjab  According to Hindu legends it was found in a Golconda mine and has been the property of various Hindu and Persian rulers'),
(53161, 'Kohl', 'A mixture of soot and other ingredients used by Egyptian and other Eastern women to darken the edges of the eyelids'),
(53162, 'Kohl rabi', 'A variety of cabbage in which the edible part is a large turnip shaped swelling of the stem above the surface of the ground'),
(53163, 'Kokama', 'The gemsbok'),
(53164, 'Koklass', 'Any pheasant of the genus Pucrasia  The birds of this genus inhabit India and China and are distinguished by having a long central and two lateral crests on the head  Called also pucras'),
(53165, 'Kokoon', 'The gnu'),
(53166, 'Kola', 'Same as Cola Cola nut'),
(53167, 'Kolarian', 'An individual of one of the races of aboriginal inhabitants which survive in Hindustan'),
(53168, 'Kolinsky', 'Among furriers any of several Asiatic minks esp Putorius sibiricus the yellowish brown pelt of which is valued esp for the tail used for making artists brushes Trade names for the fur are red sable and Tatar sable'),
(53169, 'kolkhoz', 'A collective farm owned by the communist state in the former USSR'),
(53170, 'kolkhoznik', 'a member of a kolkhoz'),
(53171, 'Koluschan', 'Designating or pertaining to a linguistic stock of North American Indians comprising the Tlinkit tribes of the Alexander Archipelago of southeastern Alaska and adjacent coast lands Their language bears some affinity to Mexican tongues'),
(53172, 'Kolkwitzia', 'A Chinese genus having only one species the beauty bush'),
(53173, 'Komenic', 'Of or pertaining to or designating an acid derived from meconic acid'),
(53174, 'komondor', 'A Hungarian breed of large powerful shaggy coated white dog used also as guard dog'),
(53175, 'Komtok', 'An African freshwater fish Protopterus annectens belonging to the Dipnoi It can breathe air by means of its lungs and when waters dry up it encases itself in a nest of hard mud where it remains till the rainy season It is used as food'),
(53176, 'Kon', 'To know See Can and Con'),
(53177, 'konini', 'An erect deciduous shrub or tree Fuchsia excorticata native to New Zealand growing up to 10 feet with maroon flushed flowers'),
(53178, 'Konite', 'See Conite'),
(53179, 'Konseal', 'A form of capsule for inclosing a dose of medicine that is offensive caustic or the like'),
(53180, 'Konze', 'A large African antelope Alcelaphus Lichtensteini allied to the hartbeest but having shorter and flatter horns and lacking a black patch on the face'),
(53181, 'Koodoo', 'A large South African antelope Strepsiceros kudu The males have graceful spiral horns sometimes four feet long The general color is reddish or grayish brown with eight or nine white bands on each side and a pale dorsal stripe The old males become dark bluish gray due to the skin showing through the hair The females are hornless Called also nellut'),
(53182, 'Kookoom', 'The oryx or gemsbok'),
(53183, 'Koolokamba', 'A west African anthropoid ape Troglodytes koolokamba or Troglodytes Aubryi allied to the chimpanzee and gorilla and in some respects intermediate between them'),
(53184, 'Koolslaa', 'See Coleslaw'),
(53185, 'Koord', 'See Kurd'),
(53186, 'Koordish', 'See Kurdish'),
(53187, 'Koorilian', 'Same as Kurilian'),
(53188, 'Kop', 'Hill mountain'),
(53189, 'kopeck', 'A small Russian coin continued as a unit of currency within the Soviet Union  One hundred kopecks make a ruble  The ruble was worth about sixty cents U S in 1910 in 1991 a two kopeck coin could be used for a local telephone call at a pay telephone  After the breakup of the Soviet Union in 1993 the exchange value of the ruble declined rapidly and by the end of 1994 the ruble was worth three hundredths of a cent and by 1997 two hundredths of a cent By 1993 the kopek had become of such small value that it was obsolete and no longer minted'),
(53190, 'kopje', 'A hillock a small kop especially a small hill rising up from the African veld'),
(53191, 'kor', 'A Hebrew unit of capacity'),
(53192, 'Koran', 'The Scriptures of the Muslims containing the professed revelations to Mohammed    called also Alcoran'),
(53193, 'Korea', 'An Asian peninsula off Manchuria'),
(53194, 'Korean', 'Of or pertaining to Korea as Korean handicrafts the Korean war'),
(53195, 'Korin', 'The gazelle'),
(53196, 'Korinthos', 'A city in Greece called Corinth in English the modern city is near the site of the ancient city that was 2nd only to Athens in size and power in ancient Greece'),
(53197, 'Korrigum', 'A West African antelope Damalis Senegalensis allied to the sassaby It is reddish gray with a black face and a black stripe on the outside of the legs above the knees'),
(53198, 'kos', 'an Indian unit of length having different values in different localities'),
(53199, 'Kosher', 'Ceremonially clean according to Jewish law    applied to food esp to meat of animals slaughtered according to the requirements of Jewish law Opposed to tref  For food to be officially kosher it must be certified fit to eat by a Rabbi according to Jewish ritual law as kosher food'),
(53200, 'Kosmos', 'See Cosmos'),
(53201, 'Kosteletzya', 'A small genus of herbs of the Southeastern U S and tropical America and Africa'),
(53202, 'Kotow', 'Same as kowtow the more common spellings'),
(53203, 'Koulan', 'A wild horse Equus onager or  Asinus onager inhabiting the plains of Central Asia    called also gour khur and onager'),
(53204, 'Koumiss', 'An intoxicating fermented or distilled liquor originally made by the Tartars of central Asia from mares or camels milk It can be obtained from any kind of milk and is now largely made in Europe'),
(53205, 'Kousso', 'An Abyssinian rosaceous tree Brayera anthelmintica the flowers of which are used as a vermifuge'),
(53206, 'kowhai', 'A shrub or small tree Sophora tetraptera of New Zealand and Chile having pendulous racemes of tubular golden yellow flowers it yields a hard strong wood'),
(53207, 'Kowtow', 'The prostration made by mandarins and others to their superiors either as homage or worship by knocking the forehead on the ground same as Kotow  There are degrees in the rite the highest being expressed by three knockings'),
(53208, 'Kr', 'The chemical symbol for the element krypton one of the six noble gases'),
(53209, 'Kra', 'A long tailed ape Macacus cynomolgus of India and Sumatra It is reddish olive spotted with black and has a black tail'),
(53210, 'Kraal', 'A collection of huts within a stockade a village sometimes a single hut'),
(53211, 'Krait', 'A very venomous snake of India Bungarus coeruleus allied to the cobra  Its upper parts are bluish or brownish black often with narrow white streaks the belly is whitish'),
(53212, 'Kraken', 'A fabulous Scandinavian sea monster often represented as resembling an island but sometimes as resembling an immense octopus'),
(53213, 'Krakowiak', 'A lively Polish dance See Cracovienne'),
(53214, 'Krameria', 'A genus of spreading shrubs with many stems from one species of which Krameria triandra found in Peru rhatany root used as a medicine is obtained'),
(53215, 'Krameric', 'Pertaining to or derived from Krameria rhatany as krameric acid usually called ratanhia tannic acid'),
(53216, 'Krang', 'The carcass of a whale after the blubber has been removed'),
(53217, 'Kranging hook', 'A hook for holding the blubber while cutting it away'),
(53218, 'Kreatic', 'See Creatic'),
(53219, 'Kreatin', 'See Creatin'),
(53220, 'Kreatinin', 'See Creatinin'),
(53221, 'Kreel', 'See Creel'),
(53222, 'Kremlin', 'The citadel of a town or city especially the citadel of Moscow a large inclosure which contains imperial palaces cathedrals churches an arsenal etc'),
(53223, 'Krems', 'A variety of white lead See Krems lead under Lead n'),
(53224, 'Kreng', 'See Krang'),
(53225, 'Kreosote', 'See Creosote'),
(53226, 'Kreutzer', 'A small copper coin formerly used in South Germany also a small Austrian copper coin'),
(53227, 'Kriegsspiel', 'A game of war played for practice on maps'),
(53228, 'Kris', 'A Malay dagger See Creese'),
(53229, 'Krishna', 'The most popular of the Hindu divinities usually held to be the eighth incarnation of the god Vishnu'),
(53230, 'Kritarchy', 'The rule of the judges over Israel'),
(53231, 'Krokidolite', 'See Crocidolite'),
(53232, 'Krone', 'A coin of Denmark Norway and Sweden of the value of about twenty eight cents in 1913 See Crown n 9'),
(53233, 'Krooman', 'One of a negro tribe of Liberia and the adjacent coast whose members are much employed on shipboard'),
(53234, 'Kruller', 'See Cruller'),
(53235, 'Krummhorn', 'A reed instrument of music of the cornet kind now obsolete see Cornet 1 a'),
(53236, 'Krupp gun', 'A breech loading steel cannon manufactured at the works of Friedrich Krupp at Essen in Prussia  Guns of over eight inch bore are made up of several concentric cylinders those of a smaller size are forged solid'),
(53237, 'Kruppize', 'To treat by or subject to the Krupp process'),
(53238, 'Krupp process', 'A process practiced by Friedrich Krupp Essen Germany for washing pig iron differing from the Bell process in using manganese as well as iron oxide and performed in a Pernot furnace  Called also the Bell Krupp process'),
(53239, 'Kryolite', 'See Cryolite'),
(53240, 'Krypton', 'An inert gaseous element of the argon noble gas group of atomic number 36 occurring in air to the extent of about one volume in a million  It was discovered by Ramsay and Travers in 1898  Boiling point  1523'),
(53241, 'Ksar', 'See Czar'),
(53242, 'Kshatriya', 'The military caste the second of the four great Hindu castes also a member of that caste See Caste'),
(53243, 'Kuda', 'The East Indian tapir See Tapir'),
(53244, 'Kudos', 'Glory fame renown praise'),
(53245, 'Kudu', 'See Koodoo'),
(53246, 'Kufic', 'See Cufic'),
(53247, 'Kukang', 'The slow lemur See Lemur');
INSERT INTO `dictionary` (`id`, `word`, `definition`) VALUES
(53248, 'Kuklux', 'The name adopted in the southern part of the United States by a secret political organization active for several years after the close of the Civil War and having for its aim the repression of the political power of the freed negroes    called also Kuklux Klan and the Klan  It exerienced a revival in the 1920s in the north as well as the south and persists as a weak organization into the 1990s  Its goals were primarily anti negro and anti Catholic and its tactics included terrorist attacks on negroes for the purpose of intimidation with the goal of continuing segregation  The signature activity of the Klan was the burning of a cross either at rallies of Klansmen or on the property of African Americans which they hoped to intimidate'),
(53249, 'Kulan', 'See Koulan'),
(53250, 'Kulturkampf', 'Lit culture war    a name originating with Virchow 1821   1902 given to a struggle between the Roman Catholic Church and the German government chiefly over the latters efforts to control educational and ecclesiastical appointments in the interest of the political policy of centralization The struggle began with the passage by the Prussian Diet in May 1873 of the so called May laws or Falk laws aiming at the regulation of the clergy  Opposition eventually compelled the government to change its policy and from 1880 to 1887 laws virtually nullifying the May laws were enacted'),
(53251, 'Kumish', 'See Koumiss'),
(53252, 'Kummel', 'A Russian and German liqueur consisting of a sweetened spirit flavored with caraway seeds'),
(53253, 'kumquat', 'any of several trees or shrubs of the genus Fortunella formerly Citrus of the rue family Rutaceae especially Citrus Japonica growing in China and Japan bearing small orange colored edible fruits with thick sweet flavored skin and sour pulp also any of the small acid orange colored citrus fruits of such plants used mostly for preserves'),
(53254, 'Kupfernickel', 'Copper nickel niccolite See Niccolite'),
(53255, 'kurakkan', 'An East Indian cereal grass Eleusine coracana whose seed yield a somewhat bitter flour a staple in the Orient'),
(53256, 'kurchatovium', 'A transuranic element of atomic number 104 symbol Ku also called rutherfordium symbol Rf  It is produced in very small quantities by nuclear reactions  In November 1993 the nomenclature committe of the American Chemical Society approved the name rutherfordium for element 104  Russsian investigators who claim to have first discovered element 104 isotope 260 half life 03 seconds in 1964 at Dubna proposed the name kurchatovium  However investigators at Berkely in 1969 produced several isotopes of element 104 but were unable to produce isotope 260 they reported finding isotope 257 with a half life of 4 5 seconds isotope 259 with a half life of 3 4 seconds and isotope 258 with a shorter half life'),
(53257, 'kurchee', 'A tropical Asian tree Holarrhena antidysenterica syn Holarrhena pubescens with hard white wood and bark formerly used as a remedy for dysentery and diarrhea'),
(53258, 'Kurd', 'A member of a people who inhabit a mountainous region of Western Asia sometimes referred to as Kurdistan spread over an area including adjoining parts of Turkey Iran Iraq Armenia Azerbaijan and Syria  The people of this region speak Kurdish and are mostly Moslem'),
(53259, 'Kurdish', 'Of or pertaining to the Kurds the Kurdish language or Kurdistan'),
(53260, 'Kurilian', 'Of or pertaining to the Kurile Islands a chain of islands in the Pacific ocean extending from the southern extremity of Kamchatka to Yesso'),
(53261, 'Kuro Siwo', 'See Japan Current above'),
(53262, 'Kursaal', 'A public hall or room for the use of visitors at watering places and health resorts in Germany'),
(53263, 'Kusimanse', 'A carnivorous animal Crossarchus obscurus of tropical Africa It its allied to the civets Called also kusimansel and mangue'),
(53264, 'Kuskus', 'See Vetiver'),
(53265, 'Kussier', 'A Turkish instrument of music with a hollow body covered with skin over which five strings are stretched'),
(53266, 'Kutauss', 'The India civet Viverra zibetha'),
(53267, 'Kutch', 'The packet of vellum leaves in which the gold is first beaten into thin sheets'),
(53268, 'kvass', 'A thin sour beer made by pouring warm water on rye or barley meal and letting it ferment    much used by the Russians'),
(53269, 'Kwajalein', 'A battle of World War II January 1944 American forces landed and captured a Japanese airbase'),
(53270, 'Kwan yin', 'A female Bodhisattva often called Goddess of Mercy and considered an aspect of the Bodisattva Avalokitesvara identified with Japanese Kwannon'),
(53271, 'Kwannon', 'Japanese counterpart of the Chinese Kuan Yin'),
(53272, 'kweek', 'A trailing grass Cynodon dactylon native to Europe now cosmopolitan in warm regions used for lawns and pastures especially in the Southern U S and India  Called also Bahama grass and Bermuda grass'),
