(58076, 'mannequin', 'a woman who wears clothes to display fashions a fashion model'),
(58077, 'Manner', 'Mode of action way of performing or effecting anything method style form fashion'),
(58078, 'Mannered', 'Having a certain way esp a polite way of carrying and conducting ones self as a well mannered child'),
(58079, 'Mannerism', 'Adherence to a peculiar style or manner a characteristic mode of action bearing behavior or treatment of others'),
(58080, 'Mannerist', 'One addicted to mannerism a person who in action bearing or treatment carries characteristic peculiarities to excess See citation under Mannerism'),
(58081, 'Mannerliness', 'The quality or state of being mannerly civility complaisance'),
(58082, 'Mannerly', 'Showing good manners civil respectful complaisant'),
(58083, 'manner name', 'A word that denotes a manner of doing something a troponym as march is a manner name for walk'),
(58084, 'manner of speaking', 'The characteristic style or manner that a person uses to express himself orally'),
(58085, 'Mannheim gold', 'A kind of brass made in imitation of gold  It contains eighty per cent of copper and twenty of zinc'),
(58086, 'Mannide', 'A white amorphous or crystalline substance obtained by dehydration of mannite and distinct from but convertible into mannitan'),
(58087, 'Mannish', 'Resembling a human being in form or nature human'),
(58088, 'Mannitan', 'A white amorphous or crystalline substance obtained by the partial dehydration of mannite'),
(58089, 'Mannitate', 'A salt of mannitic acid'),
(58090, 'Mannite', 'Same as mannitol'),
(58091, 'Mannitic', 'Of pertaining to resembling or derived from mannitol'),
(58092, 'mannitol', 'A white crystalline hexose HOCH2CHOH4CH2OH of a sweet taste obtained from a so called manna the dried sap of the flowering ash Fraxinus ornus    called also mannite and hydroxy hexane  Cf Dulcite  It is used in pharmacy as excipient and diluent for solids and liquids  It is also used as a food additive for anti caking properties or as a sweetener and illegally to cut dilute illegal drugs such as cocaine or heroin'),
(58093, 'Mannitose', 'A variety of sugar obtained by the partial oxidation of mannite and closely resembling levulose'),
(58094, 'Mano', 'The muller or crushing and grinding stone used in grinding corn on a metate'),
(58095, 'Manoeuvre', 'See Maneuver'),
(58096, 'man of action', 'A person who prefers to act rather than contemplate and gets things accomplished quickly an efficiently'),
(58097, 'man of affairs', 'A person engaged in commercial or industrial business especially an owner or executive'),
(58098, 'man of deeds', 'Same as man of action'),
(58099, 'man of letters', 'A writer especially one who writes for a living'),
(58100, 'man of means', 'A wealthy person'),
(58101, 'man of science', 'A scientist'),
(58102, 'man of the earth', 'A long rooted morning glory Ipomoea leptophylla of Western U S'),
(58103, 'man of the world', 'A worldly wise person a sophisticate'),
(58104, 'Man of war', 'A government vessel employed for the purposes of war esp one of large size a ship of war'),
(58105, 'man of war bird', 'The frigate bird a long billed warm water seabird with wide wingspan and forked tail also applied to the skua gulls and to the wandering albatross'),
(58106, 'Manograph', 'An optical device for making an indicator diagram for high speed engines It consists of a light tight box or camera having at one end a small convex mirror which reflects a beam of light on to the ground glass or photographic plate at the other end The mirror is pivoted so that it can be moved in one direction by a small plunger operated by an elastic metal diaphragm which closes a tube connected with the engine cylinder It is also moved at right angles to this direction by a reducing motion called a reproducer so as to copy accurately on a smaller scale the motion of the engine piston The resultant of these two movements imparts to the reflected beam of light a motion similar to that of the pencil of the ordinary indicator and this can be traced on the sheet of ground glass or photographed'),
(58107, 'Manometer', 'An instrument for measuring the tension or elastic force of gases steam etc constructed usually on the principle of allowing the gas to exert its elastic force in raising a column of mercury in an open tube or in compressing a portion of air or other gas in a closed tube with mercury or other liquid intervening or in bending a metallic or other spring so as to set in motion an index a pressure gauge See Pressure and Illust of Air pump'),
(58108, 'Manometric', 'Of or pertaining to the manometer made by the manometer'),
(58109, 'Manor', 'The land belonging to a lord or nobleman or so much land as a lord or great personage kept in his own hands for the use and subsistence of his family'),
(58110, 'manor hall', 'The large room of a manor or castle'),
(58111, 'Manorial', 'Of or pertaining to a manor'),
(58112, 'Manoscope', 'Same as Manometer'),
(58113, 'Manoscopy', 'The science of the determination of the density of vapors and gases'),
(58114, 'Manovery', 'A contrivance or maneuvering to catch game illegally'),
(58115, 'Manqueller', 'A killer of men a manslayer'),
(58116, 'Manred', 'Homage or service rendered to a superior as to a lord vassalage'),
(58117, 'Manrope', 'One of the side ropes to the gangway of a ship'),
(58118, 'Mansard roof', 'A hipped curb roof that is a roof having on all sides two slopes the lower one being steeper than the upper one'),
(58119, 'Manse', 'A dwelling house generally with land attached'),
(58120, 'Manservant', 'A male servant'),
(58121, 'Mansion', 'A dwelling place    whether a part or whole of a house or other shelter'),
(58122, 'Mansionary', 'Resident residentiary as mansionary canons'),
(58123, 'mansion house', 'A large and imposing house'),
(58124, 'Mansionry', 'The state of dwelling or residing occupancy as a dwelling place'),
(58125, 'man sized', 'Calling for manly attributes as a man sized job'),
(58126, 'Manslaughter', 'The slaying of a human being destruction of men'),
(58127, 'Manslayer', 'One who kills a human being one who commits manslaughter'),
(58128, 'Manstealer', 'A person who steals or kidnaps a human being or beings'),
(58129, 'Manstealing', 'The act or business of stealing or kidnaping human beings especially with a view to e'),
(58130, 'Mansuete', 'Tame gentle kind'),
(58131, 'Mansuetude', 'Tameness gentleness mildness'),
(58132, 'Manswear', 'To swear falsely Same as Mainswear'),
(58133, 'Manta', 'The manta ray  See also Cephaloptera and Sea devil'),
(58134, 'manta ray', 'An extremely large pelagic tropical ray of the family Mobulidae that feeds on plankton and small fishes  It is usually harmless but its size up to 20 feet across and up to a ton in weight make it dangerous if harpooned  Called also manta sea devil and devilfish  See also Cephaloptera and Sea devil'),
(58135, 'Mantchoo', 'Same as Manchu'),
(58136, 'Manteau', 'A womans cloak or mantle'),
(58137, 'Manteidae', 'A natural family of insects including the mantises  Also spelled Mantidae'),
(58138, 'Mantel', 'The finish around a fireplace covering the chimney breast in front and sometimes on both sides especially a shelf above the fireplace and its supports  The shelf is called also a mantelpiece or mantlepiece'),
(58139, 'Mantelet', 'A short cloak formerly worn by knights'),
(58140, 'Mantelletta', 'A silk or woolen vestment without sleeves worn by cardinals bishops abbots and the prelates of the Roman court It has a low collar is fastened in front and reaches almost to the knees'),
(58141, 'Mantelpiece', 'Same as Mantel'),
(58142, 'Mantelshelf', 'The shelf of a mantel'),
(58143, 'Manteltree', 'The lintel of a fireplace when of wood as frequently in early houses'),
(58144, 'Mantic', 'Of or pertaining to divination or to the condition of one inspired or supposed to be inspired by a deity prophetic'),
(58145, 'mantichora', 'A mythical monster having the head of man with horns and the body of a lion and the tail of a scorpion'),
(58146, 'mantid', 'A mantis'),
(58147, 'Mantidae', 'The natural family conmprising the mantises  Same as Manteidae'),
(58148, 'mantiger', 'The manticore'),
(58149, 'Mantilla', 'A ladys light cloak of cape of silk velvet lace or the like'),
(58150, 'Mantinea', 'The site of three famous battles among Greek city states in 418 BC and 362 BC and 207 BC'),
(58151, 'Mantis', 'Any one of numerous species of voracious orthopterous insects of the genus Mantis and allied genera They are  predacious long bodied large eyed insects of warm regions are remarkable for their slender grotesque forms and for holding their stout anterior legs in a manner suggesting hands folded in prayer The common American species is Mantis Carolina'),
(58152, 'mantis crab', 'Same as Squilla'),
(58153, 'mantis prawn', 'Same as Squilla'),
(58154, 'mantispid', 'Any neuropterous insect of the genus Mantispa and allied genera  The larvae feed on plant lice  Also used adjectively See Illust under Neuroptera'),
(58155, 'Mantispidae', 'The natural family comprising the mantispids'),
(58156, 'mantissa', 'The decimal part of a logarithm as distinguished from the integral part or characteristic'),
(58157, 'Mantle', 'To cover or envelop as with a mantle to cloak to hide to disguise'),
(58158, 'mantled', 'Covered with or as if with clothes or a wrap or cloak'),
(58159, 'mantled ground squirrel', 'A common black striped reddish brown ground squirrel Citellus lateralis of western North America resembling a large chipmunk'),
(58160, 'mantlepiece', 'A shelf that projects from the wall above a fireplace a mantel as in England they call a mantelpiece a chimneypiece'),
(58161, 'Mantlet', 'See Mantelet'),
(58162, 'Mantling', 'The representation of a mantle or the drapery behind and around a coat of arms    called also lambrequin'),
(58163, 'Manto', 'See Manteau'),
(58164, 'Mantologist', 'One who is skilled in mantology a diviner'),
(58165, 'Mantology', 'The act or art of divination'),
(58166, 'Mantoux test', 'A skin test for hypersensitivity to tuberculin  It determines whether there is or has been any infection with tubercle bacilli'),
(58167, 'Mantra', 'A prayer an invocation a religious formula a charm'),
(58168, 'Mantrap', 'A trap for catching trespassers'),
(58169, 'Mantua', 'A superior kind of rich silk formerly exported from Mantua in Italy'),
(58170, 'Mantuamaker', 'One who makes dresses cloaks etc for women a dressmaker'),
(58171, 'Mantuan', 'Of or pertaining to Mantua'),
(58172, 'Manu', 'One of a series of progenitors of human beings and authors of human wisdom'),
(58173, 'Manual', 'Of or pertaining to the hand'),
(58174, 'manual alphabet', 'An alphabet of signs made by movement of the hands used by the deaf in it letters are represented by finger positions  See Dactylology'),
(58175, 'manual dexterity', 'Adroitness in using the hands'),
(58176, 'Manualist', 'One who works with the hands an artificer'),
(58177, 'manual labor', 'Labor done with the hands'),
(58178, 'manual laborer', 'A person who works with the hands rather than with the mind'),
(58179, 'manually', 'By hand  In some contexts contrasted with automatically'),
(58180, 'Manuary', 'Manual'),
(58181, 'Manubial', 'Belonging to spoils taken in war'),
(58182, 'Manubrial', 'Of or pertaining to a manubrium shaped like a manubrium handlelike'),
(58183, 'Manucode', 'Any bird of the genus Manucodia of Australia and New Guinea  They are related to the bird of paradise'),
(58184, 'Manuducent', 'One who leads by the hand a manuductor'),
(58185, 'Manuduction', 'Guidance by the hand'),
(58186, 'Manuductor', 'A conductor an officer in the ancient church who gave the signal for the choir to sing and who beat time with the hand and regulated the music'),
(58187, 'Manufactory', 'Manufacture'),
(58188, 'Manufactural', 'Of or pertaining to manufactures'),
(58189, 'Manufacture', 'The operation of making wares or any products by hand by machinery or by other agency'),
(58190, 'manufactured', 'Produced in a large scale industrial operation  Contrasted with hand made'),
(58191, 'Manufacturer', 'One who manufactures'),
(58192, 'Manufacturing', 'Employed or chiefly employed in manufacture as a manufacturing community a manufacturing town'),
(58193, 'manufacturing business', 'A business engaged in manufacturing some product'),
(58194, 'manufacturing plant', 'A factory'),
(58195, 'manul', 'A wild cat Felis manul having long soft light colored fur  It is found in the mountains of Central Asia and dwells among rocks'),
(58196, 'Manumise', 'To manumit'),
(58197, 'Manumission', 'The act of manumitting or of liberating a slave from bondage'),
(58198, 'Manumit', 'To release from slavery to liberate from personal bondage or servitude to free as a slave'),
(58199, 'Manumotive', 'Movable by hand'),
(58200, 'Manumotor', 'A small wheel carriage so constructed that a person sitting in it may move it'),
(58201, 'Manurable', 'Capable of cultivation'),
(58202, 'Manurage', 'Cultivation'),
(58203, 'Manurance', 'Cultivation'),
(58204, 'Manure', 'To cultivate by manual labor to till hence to develop by culture'),
(58205, 'Manurement', 'Cultivation'),
(58206, 'Manurer', 'One who manures land'),
(58207, 'Manurial', 'Relating to manures'),
(58208, 'Manuring', 'The act of process of applying manure also the manure applied'),
(58209, 'Manus', 'The distal segment of the fore limb including the carpus and fore foot or hand'),
(58210, 'Manuscript', 'Written with or by the hand not printed as a manuscript volume'),
(58211, 'Manuscriptal', 'Manuscript'),
(58212, 'Manutenency', 'Maintenance'),
(58213, 'Manway', 'A small passageway as in a mine that a man may pass through'),
(58214, 'Manx', 'Of or pertaining to the Isle of Man or its inhabitants as the Manx language'),
(58215, 'Manx cat', 'A breed of domestic cats having a rudimentary tail containing only about three vertebrae  It is believed to have originated on the Isle of Man'),
(58216, 'Manx shearwater', 'A small black and white oceanic bird Puffinus puffinus or Puffinus anglorum common in the Eastern North Atlantic  Called also Manx petrel Manx puffin  It is avariety of shearwater  It was formerly abundant in the Isle of Man'),
(58217, 'Many', 'A retinue of servants a household'),
(58218, 'Many minded', 'Having many faculties versatile many sided'),
(58219, 'Manyplies', 'The third division or that between the reticulum or honeycomb stomach and the abomasum or rennet stomach in the stomach of ruminants the omasum the psalterium  So called from the numerous folds in its mucous membrane See Illust of Ruminant'),
(58220, 'Many sided', 'Having many sides    said of figures Hence presenting many questions or subjects for consideration as a many sided topic'),
(58221, 'manyways', 'In many different ways variously'),
(58222, 'manzanilla', 'A kind of small roundish olive with a small freestone pit a fine skin and a peculiar bitterish flavor Manzanillas are commonly pitted and stuffed with Spanish pimientos'),
(58223, 'manzanita', 'A name given to several species of Arctostaphylos but mostly to Arctostaphylos glauca and Arctostaphylos pungens shrubs of California Oregon etc with reddish smooth bark ovate or oval coriaceous evergreen leaves and bearing clusters of red berries which are said to be a favorite food of the grizzly bear'),
(58224, 'Mao', 'See Mao Tsetung'),
(58225, 'Maoism', 'A form of communism developed in China by Mao Zedong'),
(58226, 'Maoist', 'An advocate of Maoism'),
(58227, 'Maori', 'One of the aboriginal inhabitants of New Zealand also the original language of New Zealand'),
(58228, 'maori hen', 'A flightless New Zealand rail of thievish disposition having short wings each with a spur used in fighting'),
(58229, 'Mao Tsetung', 'The leader of the Chinese communist party and later of the Peoples Republic of China b 1893 d 1976  Also referred to a simply Mao'),
(58230, 'Map', 'A representation of the surface of the earth or of some portion of it showing the relative position of the parts represented    usually on a flat surface  Also such a representation of the celestial sphere or of some part of it'),
(58231, 'Mapach', 'The raccoon'),
(58232, 'mapinguari', 'A supposed human sized slothlike creature reported sighted by Indians in the Amazon rain forest'),
(58233, 'Maple', 'A tree of the genus Acer including about fifty species Acer saccharinum is the rock maple or sugar maple from the sap of which sugar is made in the United States in great quantities by evaporation the red maple or swamp maple is Acer rubrum the silver maple Acer dasycarpum having fruit wooly when young the striped maple Acer Pennsylvanium called also moosewood  The common maple of Europe is Acer campestre the sycamore maple is Acer Pseudo platanus and the Norway maple is Acer platanoides'),
(58234, 'maple leaf begonia', 'A tuberous or semi tuberous South African begonia Begonia dregei having shallowly lobed ovate leaves and small white flowers'),
(58235, 'maple leaved bayur', 'An Indian tree Pterospermum acerifolium having fragrant nocturnal white flowers and yielding a reddish wood used for planking often grown as an ornamental or shade tree  Called also mayeng'),
(58236, 'maple family', 'The natural family of trees Aceraceae including the maples'),
(58237, 'Maplike', 'Having or consisting of lines resembling a map as the maplike figures in which certain lichens grow'),
(58238, 'map maker', 'A person who makes maps a cartographer'),
(58239, 'mapmaking', 'The making of maps and charts cartography'),
(58240, 'mappery', 'The making or study of maps'),
(58241, 'mapping', 'A function such that for every element of one set there is a unique element of another set  Same as map4'),
(58242, 'Maqui', 'A Chilian shrub Aristotelia Maqui Its bark furnishes strings for musical instruments and a medicinal wine is made from its berries'),
(58243, 'Maquis', 'A guerrilla fighter in the French underground in World War II'),
(58244, 'Mar', 'A small lake See Mere'),
(58245, 'Mara', 'The principal or ruling evil spirit'),
(58246, 'Marabou', 'A large black and white carrion eating stork of the genus Leptoptilos formerly Ciconia esp the African species Leptoptilus crumeniferus syn Leptoptilos crumenifer whose downy under wing feathers are used to trim garments called also marabout  The Asiatic species Leptoptilos dubius or Leptoptilos argala is the adjutant See Adjutant'),
(58247, 'Marabout', 'A Muslim saint especially one who claims to work cures supernaturally'),
(58248, 'Maracan', 'A macaw'),
(58249, 'Marai', 'A sacred inclosure or temple    so called by the islanders of the Pacific Ocean'),
(58250, 'Maranatha', ''),
(58251, 'marang tree', 'A Philippine tree Artocarpus odoratissima similar to the breadfruit tree bearing edible fruit  Called also marang'),
(58252, 'Maranta', 'A genus of endogenous plants found in tropical America and some species also in India  They have tuberous roots containing a large amount of starch and from one species Maranta arundinacea the American arrowroot or obedience plant arrowroot arrowroot starch is obtained  Many kinds are cultivated for ornament'),
(58253, 'Marantaceae', 'A natural family of tropical perennial herbs with usually starchy rhizomes including the arrowroot the arrowroot family'),
(58254, 'marasca', 'The small bitter fruit of the marasca cherry tree from whose juice maraschino liqueur is made'),
(58255, 'marasca cherry', 'A Dalmation bitter wild cherry tree Prunus cerasus marasca bearing fruit whose juice is made into maraschino liqueur'),
(58256, 'Maraschino', 'A liqueur distilled from fermented cherry juice and flavored with the pit of a variety of cherry which grows in Dalmatia'),
(58257, 'maraschino cherry', 'A cherry which is colored a deep red and sweetened by cooking in colored syrup and flavored with maraschino  It is used as a garnish in deserts and cocktails'),
(58258, 'Marasmius', 'A genus of chiefly small white spored mushrooms including the Marasmius oreades the mushroon that grow in a fairy ring'),
(58259, 'marasmus', 'A wasting of flesh without fever or apparent disease a kind of consumption atrophy phthisis'),
(58260, 'Marathi', 'A Sanskritic language of western India prob descended from the Maharastri Prakrit spoken by the Marathas and neighboring peoples  It has an abundant literature dating from the 13th century  It has a book alphabet nearly the same as Devanagari and a cursive script translation between the Devanagari and the Gujarati'),
(58261, 'marathon', 'A footrace of 26 miles 385 yards'),
(58262, 'Marattia', 'The type genus of the Marattiaceae consisting of ferns having the sporangia fused together in two rows'),
(58263, 'Marattiaceae', 'The natural family of ferns coextensive with the order Marattiales consisting of chiefly tropical eusporantiate ferns with gigantic fronds'),
(58264, 'Marattiales', 'An order of lower ferns coextensive with the family Marattiaceae'),
(58265, 'maraud', 'To rove in quest of plunder to make an excursion for booty to plunder'),
(58266, 'Marauder', 'A rover in quest of booty or plunder a plunderer one who pillages'),
(58267, 'Maravedi', 'A small copper coin of Spain equal to three mils American money less than a farthing sterling  Also an ancient Spanish gold coin'),
(58268, 'maravilla', 'A wildflower Mirabilis multiflora having vibrant deep pink tubular evening blooming flowers found in sandy and desert areas from Southern California to Southern Colorado and into Mexico'),
(58269, 'Marble', 'A massive compact limestone a variety of calcite capable of being polished and used for architectural and ornamental purposes The color varies from white to black being sometimes yellow red and green and frequently beautifully veined or clouded The name is also given to other rocks of like use and appearance as serpentine or verd antique marble and less properly to polished porphyry granite etc'),
(58270, 'Marbled', 'Made of or faced with marble'),
(58271, 'marble cake', 'A made of light and dark batter very lightly blended so as to produce a variegated appearance resembling that of marble'),
(58272, 'Marble edged', 'Having the edge veined or spotted with different colors like marble as a book'),
(58273, 'Marbleize', 'To stain or grain in imitation of marble to cover with a surface resembling marble as to marbleize slate wood or iron'),
(58274, 'marbleizing', 'A texture like that of marble'),
(58275, 'Marbler', 'One who works upon marble or other stone'),
(58276, 'marbles', 'A childrens game played with marbles3 little balls made of a hard substance as glass'),
(58277, 'marblewood', 'A hard marbled wood'),
(58278, 'Marbling', 'The art or practice of variegating in color in imitation of marble'),
(58279, 'Marbly', 'Containing or resembling marble'),
(58280, 'Marbrinus', 'A cloth woven so as to imitate the appearance of marble    much used in the 15th and 16th centuries'),
(58281, 'Marc', 'The refuse matter which remains after the pressure of fruit particularly of grapes'),
(58282, 'Marcantant', 'A merchant'),
(58283, 'Marcasite', 'A sulphide of iron resembling pyrite or common iron pyrites in composition but differing in form white iron pyrites'),
(58284, 'Marcasitic', 'Containing or having the nature of marcasite'),
(58285, 'Marcassin', 'A young wild boar'),
(58286, 'Marcato', 'In a marked emphatic manner    used adverbially as a direction'),
(58287, 'Marceline', 'A thin silk fabric used for linings etc in ladies dresses'),
(58288, 'Marcel Proust', 'A French novelist 1871 1922'),
(58289, 'Marcescent', 'Withering without falling off fading decaying'),
(58290, 'Marcescible', 'Liable to wither or decay'),
(58291, 'March', 'The third month of the year containing thirty one days'),
(58292, 'marchand de vin', 'A sauce consisting of brown sauce with mushrooms and red wine or madeira'),
(58293, 'Marchantia', 'The type genus of Marchantiaceae liverworts that reproduce asexually by gemmae and have stalked antheridiophores'),
(58294, 'Marchantiaceae', 'A natural family of liverworts with prostrate and usually dichotomously branched thalli'),
(58295, 'Marchantiales', 'An oder of liverworts with gametophyte differentiated internally'),
(58296, 'march equinox', 'The vernal equinox'),
(58297, 'Marcher', 'One who marches'),
(58298, 'Marchet', 'In old English and in Scots law a fine paid to the lord of the soil by a tenant upon the marriage of one the tenants daughters'),
(58299, 'Marching', 'a  n fr March v'),
(58300, 'Marchioness', 'The wife or the widow of a marquis a woman who has the rank and dignity of a marquis'),
(58301, 'March mad', 'Extremely rash foolhardy See under March the month');
INSERT INTO `dictionary` (`id`, `word`, `definition`) VALUES
(58302, 'Marchman', 'A person living in the marches between England and Scotland or Wales'),
(58303, 'Marchpane', 'A kind of sweet bread or biscuit a cake of pounded almonds and sugar  Called also marzipan'),
(58304, 'March ward', 'A warden of the marches a marcher'),
(58305, 'Marcian', 'Under the influence of Mars courageous bold'),
(58306, 'Marcid', 'Pining lean withered'),
(58307, 'Marcidity', 'The state or quality of being withered or lean'),
(58308, 'Marcionite', 'A follower of Marcion a Gnostic of the second century who adopted the Oriental notion of the two conflicting principles and imagined that between them there existed a third power neither wholly good nor evil the Creator of the world and of man and the God of the Jewish dispensation'),
(58309, 'Marcobrunner', 'A celebrated Rhine wine'),
(58310, 'Marconi', 'Designating or pert to Marconis system of wireless telegraphy as Marconi aerial coherer station system etc archaic '),
(58311, 'Marconigram', 'same as radiogram a wireless message'),
(58312, 'Marconigraph', 'The apparatus used in Marconi wireless telegraphy'),
(58313, 'marconi rig', 'A rig of triangular sails for a yacht'),
(58314, 'Marconis law', 'The law that the maximum good signaling distance varies directly as the square of the height of the transmitting antenna'),
(58315, 'Marconism', 'The theory or practice of Marconis wireless telegraph system'),
(58316, 'Marconi system', 'A system or wireless telegraphy developed by G Marconi an Italian physicist in which Hertzian waves are used in transmission and a coherer is used as the receiving instrument'),
(58317, 'marco polo sheep', 'An Asiatic wild sheep Ovis poli with exceptionally large horns sometimes considered a variety of the argali Ovis ammon poli'),
(58318, 'Marcor', 'A wasting away of flesh decay'),
(58319, 'Marcosian', 'One of a Gnostic sect of the second century so called from Marcus an Egyptian who was reputed to be a margician'),
(58320, 'Mardi gras', 'The last day of Carnival the Tuesday before Ash Wednesday the beginning of Lent and fasting Shrove Tuesday    in some cities a great day of carnival and merrymaking in the United States it is especially associated with New Orleans'),
(58321, 'Mare', 'The female of the horse and other equine quadrupeds'),
(58322, 'Marechal Niel', 'A kind of large yellow rose'),
(58323, 'Mare clausum', 'Lit closed sea hence a body of water within the separate jurisdiction of the nation    opposed to open sea mare liberum the water open to all nations and over which no single nation has special control'),
(58324, 'Mareis', 'A Marsh'),
(58325, 'mare liberum', 'A navigable body of water to which all nations have equal access the opposite of mare clausum'),
(58326, 'Marena', 'A European whitefish of the genus Coregonus'),
(58327, 'mare nostrum', 'The term used by the ancient Romans to refer to the Mediterranean Sea'),
(58328, 'Mareschal', 'A military officer of high rank a marshal'),
(58329, 'mares nest', 'A supposed discovery which turns out to be a hoax something grossly absurd'),
(58330, 'Mares tail', 'A long streaky cloud spreading out like a horses tail and believed to indicate rain a cirrus cloud See Cloud'),
(58331, 'Margarate', 'A compound of the so called margaric acid with a base'),
(58332, 'Margaric', 'Pertaining to or resembling pearl pearly'),
(58333, 'Margarin', 'A fatty substance extracted from animal fats and certain vegetable oils formerly supposed to be a definite compound of glycerin and margaric acid but now known to be simply a mixture or combination of tristearin and tripalmitin'),
(58334, 'Margarine', 'A processed food product used as an inexpensive substitute for butter made primarily from refined vegetable oils sometimes including animal fats and churned with skim milk to form a semisolid emulsion also called oleomargarine artificial butter'),
(58335, 'Margaritaceous', 'Pertaining to or resembling pearl pearly'),
(58336, 'Margarite', 'A pearl'),
(58337, 'Margaritic', 'Margaric'),
(58338, 'Margaritiferous', 'Producing pearls'),
(58339, 'Margarodite', 'A hidrous potash mica related to muscovite'),
(58340, 'Margarone', 'The ketone of margaric acid'),
(58341, 'Margarous', 'Margaric    formerly designating a supposed acid'),
(58342, 'Margaryize', 'To impregnate wood with a preservative solution of copper sulphate often called Margarys fluid  r'),
(58343, 'Margate fish', 'A sparoid fish Diabasis aurolineatus of the Gulf of Mexico esteemed as a food fish    called also red mouth grunt'),
(58344, 'Margay', 'A small American wild cat Felis wiedi syn Felis tigrina ranging from Mexico to Brazil  It is spotted with black  Called also long tailed cat'),
(58345, 'Marge', 'Border margin edge verge'),
(58346, 'Margent', 'A margin border brink edge'),
(58347, 'Margin', 'A border edge brink verge as the margin of a river or lake'),
(58348, 'margin account', 'An account with a securities brokerage in which the broker extends credit'),
(58349, 'Marginal', 'Of or pertaining to a margin'),
