(68360, 'Overwork', 'To work beyond the strength to cause to labor too much or too long to tire excessively as to overwork a horse'),
(68361, 'Overworn', 'Worn out or subdued by toil worn out so as to be trite'),
(68362, 'Overwrest', 'To wrest or force from the natural or proper position'),
(68363, 'Overwrestle', 'To subdue by wrestling'),
(68364, 'Overwrought', 'Wrought upon excessively overworked'),
(68365, 'Overzeal', 'Excess of zeal'),
(68366, 'Overzealous', 'Too zealous'),
(68367, 'Ovicapsule', 'The outer layer of a Graafian follicle'),
(68368, 'Ovicell', 'One of the dilatations of the body wall of Bryozoa in which the ova sometimes undergo the first stages of their development See Illust of Chilostoma'),
(68369, 'Ovicular', 'Of or pertaining to an egg'),
(68370, 'Ovicyst', 'The pouch in which incubation takes place in some Tunicata'),
(68371, 'Ovidian', 'Of or pertaining to the Latin poet Ovid resembling the style of Ovid'),
(68372, 'Oviducal', 'Of or pertaining to oviducts as oviducal glands'),
(68373, 'Oviduct', 'A tube or duct for the passage of ova from the ovary to the exterior of the animal or to the part where further development takes place  In mammals the oviducts are also called Fallopian tubes'),
(68374, 'Oviferous', 'Egg bearing    applied particularly to certain receptacles as in Crustacea that retain the eggs after they have been excluded from the formative organs until they are hatched'),
(68375, 'Oviform', 'Having the form or figure of an egg egg shaped as an oviform leaf'),
(68376, 'Ovigerous', 'Bearing eggs oviferous'),
(68377, 'Ovile', 'See Ovine'),
(68378, 'Ovine', 'Of or pertaining to sheep consisting of sheep'),
(68379, 'Ovipara', 'An artificial division of vertebrates including those that lay eggs    opposed to Vivipara'),
(68380, 'Oviparity', 'Generation by means of ova See Generation'),
(68381, 'Oviparous', 'Producing young from eggs as an oviparous animal in which the egg is generally separated from the animal and hatched after exclusion    opposed to viviparous'),
(68382, 'Oviposit', 'To lay or deposit eggs    said esp of insects'),
(68383, 'Ovipositing', 'The depositing of eggs esp by insects'),
(68384, 'Ovipositor', 'The organ with which many insects and some other animals deposit their eggs  Some ichneumon files have a long ovipositor fitted to pierce the eggs or larvae of other insects in order to lay their own eggs within the same'),
(68385, 'Ovisac', 'A Graafian follicle any sac containing an ovum or ova'),
(68386, 'Ovism', 'The old theory that the egg contains the whole embryo of the future organism and the germs of all subsequent offsprings and is merely awakened to activity by the spermatozoon    opposed to spermism or animalculism'),
(68387, 'Ovist', 'A believer in ovism  Same as Ovulist'),
(68388, 'Ovococcus', 'A germinal vesicle'),
(68389, 'ovoflavin', 'An uncommon name for riboflavin also called vitamin B2'),
(68390, 'Ovoid', 'Resembling an egg in shape egg shaped ovate as an ovoidal apple'),
(68391, 'Ovolo', 'A round convex molding See Illust of Column'),
(68392, 'Ovology', 'That branch of natural history which treats of the origin and functions of eggs'),
(68393, 'Ovoplasma', 'Yolk egg yolk'),
(68394, 'Ovotesttis', 'An organ which produces both ova and spermatozoids an hermaphrodite gland'),
(68395, 'Ovoviviparous', 'Oviparous but hatching the egg while it is within the body as some fishes and reptiles'),
(68396, 'Ovular', 'Relating or belonging to an ovule as an ovular growth'),
(68397, 'Ovulary', 'Pertaining to ovules'),
(68398, 'Ovulate', 'Containing an ovule or ovules'),
(68399, 'ovulation', 'The formation of ova or eggs in the ovary and the discharge of the same  In the human female the discharge occurs about halfway between menstruation times'),
(68400, 'Ovule', 'The rudiment of a seed  It grows from a placenta and consists of a soft nucleus within two delicate coatings  The attached base of the ovule is the hilum the coatings are united with the nucleus at the chalaza and their minute orifice is the foramen'),
(68401, 'Ovuliferous', 'Producing ovules'),
(68402, 'Ovulist', 'A believer in the theory called encasement theory current during the last century that the egg was the real animal germ and that at the time of fecundation the spermatozoa simply gave the impetus which caused the unfolding of the egg in which all generations were inclosed one within the other Also called ovist'),
(68403, 'Ovulite', 'A fossil egg'),
(68404, 'Ovulum', 'An ovule'),
(68405, 'Ovum', 'A more or less spherical and transparent cell which by a process of multiplication and growth develops into a mass of cells constituting a new individual like the parent an egg spore germ or germ cell See Illust of Mycropyle'),
(68406, 'Owch', 'See Ouch'),
(68407, 'Owe', 'To possess to have as the rightful owner to own'),
(68408, 'Owel', 'Equal'),
(68409, 'Owelty', 'Equality    sometimes written ovelty and ovealty'),
(68410, 'Owen', 'Own'),
(68411, 'Owenite', 'A follower of Robert Owen who tried to reorganize society on a socialistic basis and established an industrial community on the Clyde Scotland and later a similar one in Indiana'),
(68412, 'Owher', 'Anywhere'),
(68413, 'Owing', 'Had or held under obligation of paying due'),
(68414, 'Owl', 'To pry about to prowl'),
(68415, 'Owler', 'One who owls esp one who conveys contraband goods See Owling n'),
(68416, 'Owlery', 'An abode or a haunt of owls'),
(68417, 'Owlet', 'A small owl especially the European species Athene noctua and the California flammulated owlet Megascops flammeolus'),
(68418, 'Owl eyed', 'Having eyes like an owls'),
(68419, 'Owling', 'The offense of transporting wool or sheep out of England contrary to the statute formerly existing'),
(68420, 'Owlish', 'Resembling or characteristic of an owl'),
(68421, 'Owlism', 'Affected wisdom pompous dullness'),
(68422, 'Owllight', 'Glimmering or imperfect light'),
(68423, 'Own', 'To grant to acknowledge to admit to be true to confess to recognize in a particular character as we own that we have forfeited your love'),
(68424, 'owned', 'having an owner often used in combination as state owned railways  Opposite of unowned'),
(68425, 'Owner', 'One who owns a rightful proprietor one who has the legal or rightful title whether he is the possessor or not'),
(68426, 'owner driver', 'a motorist who owns the vehicle that heshe drives'),
(68427, 'Ownerless', 'Without an owner'),
(68428, 'owner occupied', 'lived in by the owner    of dwellings'),
(68429, 'owner occupier', 'a occupant who owns the home that heshe lives in'),
(68430, 'Ownership', 'The state of being an owner the right to own exclusive right of possession legal or just claim or title proprietorship'),
(68431, 'Owre', 'The aurochs'),
(68432, 'Owse', 'Tanners ooze See Ooze 3'),
(68433, 'Ox', 'The male of bovine quadrupeds especially the domestic animal when castrated and grown to its full size or nearly so The word is also applied as a general name to any species of bovine animals male and female'),
(68434, 'Oxacid', 'See Oxyacid'),
(68435, 'oxalacetate', 'a salt or ester of oxalacetic acid'),
(68436, 'oxalacetic acid', 'A dicarboxylic acid HOCOCOCH2COOH'),
(68437, 'Oxalan', 'A complex nitrogenous substance C3N3H5O3 obtained from alloxan or when urea is fused with ethyl oxamate as a stable white crystalline powder    called also oxaluramide'),
(68438, 'Oxalantin', 'A white crystalline nitrogenous substance C6H4N4O5  obtained by the reduction of parabanic acid    called also leucoturic acid'),
(68439, 'Oxalate', 'A salt of oxalic acid'),
(68440, 'Oxaldehyde', 'Same as Glyoxal'),
(68441, 'Oxalethyline', 'A poisonous nitrogenous base C6H10N2 obtained indirectly from oxamide as a thick transparent oil which has a strong narcotic odor and a physiological action resembling that of atropine It is probably related to pyridine'),
(68442, 'Oxalic', 'Pertaining to derived from or contained in sorrel or oxalis specifically designating an acid found in and characteristic of oxalis and also certain plant of the Buckwheat family'),
(68443, 'Oxaline', 'See Glyoxaline'),
(68444, 'Oxalis', 'A genus of plants mostly herbs with acid tasting trifoliolate or multifoliolate leaves    called also wood sorrel'),
(68445, 'Oxalite', 'A yellow mineral consisting of oxalate of iron'),
(68446, 'Oxaluramide', 'Same as Oxalan'),
(68447, 'Oxalurate', 'A salt of oxaluric acid'),
(68448, 'Oxaluric', 'Pertaining to or designating a complex nitrogenous acid related to the ureids and obtained from parabanic acid as a white silky crystalline substance'),
(68449, 'Oxalyl', 'A hydrocarbon radical C2O2 regarded as a residue of oxalic acid and occurring in derivatives of it'),
(68450, 'Oxamate', 'A salt of oxamic acid'),
(68451, 'Oxamethane', 'Ethyl oxamate obtained as a white scaly crystalline powder'),
(68452, 'Oxamethylane', 'Methyl oxamate obtained as a pearly white crystalline substance'),
(68453, 'Oxamic', 'Pertaining to or designating an acid NH2COCOHO obtained as a fine crystalline powder intermediate between oxalic acid and oxamide Its ammonium salt is obtained by boiling oxamide with ammonia'),
(68454, 'Oxamide', 'A white crystalline neutral substance C2O2NH22 obtained by treating ethyl oxalate with ammonia It is the acid amide of oxalic acid  Formerly called also oxalamide'),
(68455, 'Oxamidine', 'One of a series of bases containing the amido and the isonitroso groups united to the same carbon atom'),
(68456, 'Oxanilamide', 'A white crystalline nitrogenous substance obtained indirectly by the action of cyanogen on aniline and regarded as an anilide of oxamic acid    called also phenyl oxamide'),
(68457, 'Oxanilate', 'A salt of oxanilic acid'),
(68458, 'Oxanilic', 'Pertaining to or derived from oxalic acid and aniline    used to designate an acid obtained in white crystalline scales by heating these substances together'),
(68459, 'Oxanilide', 'A white crystalline substance resembling oxanilamide obtained by heating aniline oxalate and regarded as a double anilide of oxalic acid    called also diphenyl oxamide'),
(68460, 'Oxbane', 'A poisonous bulbous plant Buphane toxicaria of the Cape of Good Hope'),
(68461, 'Oxbird', 'The dunlin'),
(68462, 'Oxbiter', 'The cow blackbird'),
(68463, 'Oxbow', 'A frame of wood bent into the shape of the letter U and embracing an oxs neck as a kind of collar the upper ends passing through the bar of the yoke also anything so shaped as a bend in a river'),
(68464, 'Oxeye', 'The oxeye daisy See under Daisy'),
(68465, 'Oxeyed', 'Having large full eyes like those of an ox'),
(68466, 'Oxfly', 'The gadfly of cattle'),
(68467, 'Oxford', 'Of or pertaining to the city or university of Oxford England'),
(68468, 'Oxgang', 'See Bovate'),
(68469, 'Oxgoad', 'A goad for driving oxen'),
(68470, 'Oxhead', 'Literally the head of an ox emblem of cuckoldom hence a dolt a blockhead'),
(68471, 'Oxheal', 'Same as Bears foot'),
(68472, 'Oxheart', 'A large heart shaped cherry either black red or white'),
(68473, 'Oxhide', 'The skin of an ox or leather made from it'),
(68474, 'Oxid', 'See Oxide'),
(68475, 'Oxidability', 'Capability of being converted into an oxide'),
(68476, 'Oxidable', 'Capable of being converted into an oxide'),
(68477, 'Oxidate', 'To oxidize'),
(68478, 'Oxidation', 'The act or process of oxidizing or the state or result of being oxidized'),
(68479, 'oxidative', 'of or pertaining to oxidation accompanied by oxidation'),
(68480, 'Oxidator', 'An oxidizer'),
(68481, 'Oxide', 'A binary compound of oxygen with an atom or radical or a compound which is regarded as binary as iron oxide ethyl oxide nitrogen oxide etc'),
(68482, 'oxidise', 'Same as oxidize and oxidized'),
(68483, 'Oxidizable', 'Capable of being oxidized'),
(68484, 'oxidization', 'The process of oxidizing the addition of oxygen to a compound with a loss of electrons  The process always occurs accompanied by reduction'),
(68485, 'Oxidize', 'To combine with oxygen or subject to the action of oxygen or of an oxidizing agent'),
(68486, 'oxidized', 'combined with or having undergone a chemical reaction with oxygen as the oxidized form of iodine'),
(68487, 'Oxidizement', 'Oxidation'),
(68488, 'Oxidizer', 'An agent employed in oxidation or which facilitates or brings about combination with oxygen as nitric acid chlorine bromine etc are strong oxidizers'),
(68489, 'Oxidulated', 'Existing in the state of a protoxide    said of an oxide'),
(68490, 'Oxime', 'One of a series of isonitroso derivatives obtained by the action of hydroxylamine on aldehydes or ketones'),
(68491, 'Oxindol', 'A white crystalline nitrogenous substance C8H7NO of the indol group obtained by the reduction of dioxindol  It is a so called lactam compound'),
(68492, 'Oxiodic', 'Pertaining to or designating certain compounds of iodine and oxygen'),
(68493, 'Oxlike', 'Characteristic of or like an ox'),
(68494, 'Oxlip', 'The great cowslip Primula veris var elatior'),
(68495, 'Oxonate', 'A salt of oxonic acid'),
(68496, 'Oxonian', 'Of or relating to the city or the university of Oxford England'),
(68497, 'Oxonic', 'Pertaining to or designating a complex nitrogenous acid C4H5N3O4 not known in the free state but obtained in combination with its salts by a slow oxidation of uric acid to which it is related');
INSERT INTO `dictionary` (`id`, `word`, `definition`) VALUES
(68498, 'Oxpecker', 'An African bird of the genus Buphaga the beefeater'),
(68499, 'Oxshoe', 'A shoe for oxen consisting of a flat piece of iron nailed to the hoof'),
(68500, 'Oxter', 'The armpit also the arm'),
(68501, 'oxtongue', 'A widespread European weed Picris echioides formerly Helminthia echioides with spiny tongue shaped leaves and yellow flowers  The name is applied to several plants from the shape and roughness of their leaves as Anchusa officinalis a kind of bugloss and Helminthia echioides both European herbs  It has been naturalized in the U S'),
(68502, 'Oxy ', 'A prefix also used adjectively'),
