(72485, 'Phytopathologist', 'One skilled in diseases of plants'),
(72486, 'Phytopathology', 'The science of diseases to which plants are liable'),
(72487, 'Phytophaga', 'A division of Hymenoptera the sawflies'),
(72488, 'Phytophagic', 'Phytophagous'),
(72489, 'Phytophagous', 'Feeding on plants herbivorous as a phytophagous animal'),
(72490, 'Phytophagy', 'The eating of plants'),
(72491, 'Phytophysiology', 'Vegetable physiology'),
(72492, 'Phytotomist', 'One versed in phytotomy'),
(72493, 'Phytotomy', 'The dissection of plants vegetable anatomy'),
(72494, 'Phytozoaria', 'Same as Infusoria'),
(72495, 'Phytozo', 'A plantlike animal The term is sometimes applied to zoophytes'),
(72496, 'Phyz', 'See Phiz'),
(72497, 'Pi', 'The inorganic orthophoshate ion    a symbol used in biochemistry'),
(72498, 'Pia', 'See Piassava'),
(72499, 'Piacle', 'A heinous offense which requires expiation'),
(72500, 'Piacular', 'Expiatory atoning'),
(72501, 'Piacularity', 'The quality or state of being piacular criminality wickedness'),
(72502, 'Piaculous', 'Same as Piacular'),
(72503, 'Pial', 'Pertaining to the pia mater'),
(72504, 'Pia mater', 'The delicate and highly vascular membrane immediately investing the brain and spinal cord'),
(72505, 'Pian', 'The yaws See Yaws'),
(72506, 'Pianet', 'The magpie'),
(72507, 'Pianette', 'A small piano a pianino'),
(72508, 'Pianino', 'A pianette or small piano'),
(72509, 'Pianissimo', 'Very soft    a direction to execute a passage as softly as possible Abbrev pp'),
(72510, 'Pianist', 'A performer esp a skilled performer on the piano'),
(72511, 'pianistic', 'Of pertaining to or characteristic of a pianist as pianistic abilities'),
(72512, 'Piano', 'Soft    a direction to the performer to execute a certain passage softly and with diminished volume of tone Abbrev p'),
(72513, 'Pianograph', 'A form of melodiograph applied to a piano'),
(72514, 'Pianola', 'A mechanically operated piano that uses a roll of perforated paper to activate the keys'),
(72515, 'Piapec', 'A West African pie Ptilostomus Senegalensis'),
(72516, 'Piarist', 'One of a religious order who are the regular clerks of the Scuole Pie religious schools an institute of secondary education founded at Rome in the last years of the 16th century'),
(72517, 'Piassava', 'A fibrous product of two Brazilian palm trees Attalea funifera and Leopoldinia Piassaba    used in making brooms and for other purposes Called also pia'),
(72518, 'Piaster', 'A silver coin of Spain and various other countries See Peso The Spanish piaster commonly called peso or peso duro is of about the value of the American dollar The Italian piaster or scudo was worth from 80 to 100 cents The Turkish and Egyptian piasters are now worth about four and a half cents'),
(72519, 'Piastre', 'See Piaster'),
(72520, 'Piation', 'The act of making atonement expiation'),
(72521, 'Piatti', 'Cymbals'),
(72522, 'Piazza', 'An open square in a European town especially an Italian town hence Arch an arcaded and roofed gallery a portico In the United States the word is popularly applied to a veranda'),
(72523, 'Pibcorn', 'A wind instrument or pipe with a horn at each end    used in Wales'),
(72524, 'Pibroch', 'A Highland air suited to the particular passion which the musician would either excite or assuage generally applied to those airs that are played on the bagpipe before the Highlanders when they go out to battle'),
(72525, 'Pic', 'A Turkish cloth measure varying from 18 to 28 inches'),
(72526, 'Pica', 'The genus that includes the magpies'),
(72527, 'Picador', 'A horseman armed with a lance who in a bullfight receives the first attack of the bull and excites him by picking him without attempting to kill him'),
(72528, 'Picamar', 'An oily liquid hydrocarbon extracted from the creosote of beechwood tar It consists essentially of certain derivatives of pyrogallol'),
(72529, 'Picapare', 'The finfoot'),
(72530, 'Picard', 'One of a sect of Adamites in the fifteenth century    so called from one Picard of Flanders See Adamite'),
(72531, 'Picaresque', 'Applied to that class of literature in which the principal personage is the Spanish picaro meaning a rascal a knave a rogue an adventurer'),
(72532, 'Picari', 'An extensive division of birds which includes the woodpeckers toucans trogons hornbills kingfishers motmots rollers and goatsuckers By some writers it is made to include also the cuckoos swifts and humming birds'),
(72533, 'Picarian', 'Of or pertaining to Picari'),
(72534, 'Picaroon', 'One who plunders especially a plunderer of wrecks a pirate a corsair a marauder a sharper'),
(72535, 'Picayune', 'A small coin of the value of six and a quarter cents See Fippenny bit'),
(72536, 'Picayunish', 'Petty paltry mean as a picayunish business'),
(72537, 'Piccadil', 'A high stiff collar for the neck also a hem or band about the skirt of a garment    worn by men in the 17th century'),
(72538, 'Piccage', 'Money paid at fairs for leave to break ground for booths'),
(72539, 'Piccalilli', 'A pickle of various vegetables with pungent species    originally made in the East Indies'),
(72540, 'Piccolo', 'A small shrill flute the pitch of which is an octave higher than the ordinary flute an octave flute'),
(72541, 'Pice', 'A small copper coin of the East Indies worth less than a cent'),
(72542, 'Picea', 'A genus of coniferous trees of the northen hemisphere including the Norway spruce and the American black and white spruces These trees have pendent cones which do not readily fall to pieces in this and other respects differing from the firs'),
(72543, 'Picene', 'A hydrocarbon C'),
(72544, 'Piceous', 'Of or pertaining to pitch resembling pitch in color or quality pitchy'),
(72545, 'Pichey', 'A Brazilian armadillo Dasypus minutus the little armadillo'),
(72546, 'Pichiciago', 'A small burrowing South American edentate Chlamyphorus truncatus allied to the armadillos The shell is attached only along the back'),
(72547, 'Pichurim bean', 'The seed of a Brazilian lauraceous tree Nectandra Puchury of a taste and smell between those of nutmeg and of sassafras    sometimes used medicinally  Called also sassafras nut'),
(72548, 'Pici', 'A division of birds including the woodpeckers and wrynecks'),
(72549, 'Piciform', 'Of or pertaining to the Piciformes'),
(72550, 'Piciformes', 'A group of birds including the woodpeckers toucans barbets colies kingfishes hornbills and some other related groups'),
(72551, 'Picine', 'Of or pertaining to the woodpeckers Pici or to the Piciformes'),
(72552, 'Pick', 'To throw to pitch'),
(72553, 'Pickaback', 'On the back or shoulders as to ride pickback'),
(72554, 'Pickaninny', 'A small child especially a negro or mulatto infant  Now 2001 used primarily in the latter sense and in that sense often considered derogatory'),
(72555, 'Pickapack', 'Pickaback'),
(72556, 'Pickax', 'A pick with a point at one end a transverse edge or blade at the other and a handle inserted at the middle a hammer with a flattened end for driving wedges and a pointed end for piercing as it strikes'),
(72557, 'Pickback', 'On the back'),
(72558, 'Picked', 'Pointed sharp'),
(72559, 'Pickedness', 'The state of being sharpened pointedness'),
(72560, 'Pickeer', 'To make a raid for booty to maraud also to skirmish in advance of an army See Picaroon'),
(72561, 'Pickeerer', 'One who pickeers'),
(72562, 'Picker', 'One who or that which picks in any sense    as one who uses a pick one who gathers a thief a pick a pickax as a cotton picker'),
(72563, 'Pickerel', 'A young or small pike'),
(72564, 'Pickering', 'The sauger of the StLawrence River'),
(72565, 'Pickery', 'Petty theft'),
(72566, 'Picket', 'A stake sharpened or pointed especially one used in fortification and encampments to mark bounds and angles or one used for tethering horses'),
(72567, 'Picketee', 'See Picotee'),
(72568, 'Pick fault', 'One who seeks out faults'),
(72569, 'Picking', 'The act of digging or breaking up as with a pick'),
(72570, 'Pickle', 'See Picle'),
(72571, 'Pickled', 'Preserved in a pickle'),
(72572, 'Pickle herring', 'A herring preserved in brine a pickled herring'),
(72573, 'Pickler', 'One who makes pickles'),
(72574, 'Picklock', 'An instrument for picking locks'),
(72575, 'Pick me up', 'A stimulant restorative or tonic a bracer'),
(72576, 'Pickmire', 'The pewit or black headed gull'),
(72577, 'Picknick', 'See Picnic'),
(72578, 'Pickoff', 'a play in which a base runner is picked off See pick off'),
(72579, 'Pick off', 'to put out a baserunner who is off base by tagging himher especially by a quick throw from the pitcher or catcher'),
(72580, 'Pickpack', 'Pickaback'),
(72581, 'Pickpenny', 'A miser also a sharper'),
(72582, 'Pickpocket', 'One who steals purses or other articles from pockets');
INSERT INTO `dictionary` (`id`, `word`, `definition`) VALUES
(72583, 'Pickpurse', 'One who steals purses or money from purses'),
(72584, 'Picksy', 'See Pixy'),
(72585, 'Pickthank', 'One who strives to put another under obligation an officious person hence a flatterer Used also adjectively'),
(72586, 'Picktooth', 'A toothpick'),
(72587, 'Pickup', 'Act of picking up as in various games the fielding or hitting of a ball just after it strikes the ground'),
(72588, 'Picle', 'A small piece of land inclosed with a hedge a close'),
(72589, 'Picnic', 'Formerly an entertainment at which each person contributed some dish to a common table now an excursion or pleasure party in which the members partake of a collation or repast usually in the open air and from food carried by themselves'),
(72590, 'Picnicker', 'One who takes part in a picnic'),
(72591, 'Picoid', 'Like or pertaining to the Pici'),
(72592, 'Picoline', 'Any one of three isometric bases C6H7N related to pyridine and obtained from bone oil acrolein ammonia and coal tar naphtha as colorless mobile liquids of strong odor    called also methyl pyridine'),
(72593, 'Picot', 'One of many small loops as of thread forming an ornamental border as on a ribbon'),
(72594, 'Picotee', 'A variety of carnation having petals of a light color variously dotted and spotted at the edges'),
(72595, 'Picquet', 'See Piquet'),
(72596, 'Picra', 'The powder of aloes with canella formerly officinal employed as a cathartic'),
(72597, 'Picrate', 'A salt of picric acid'),
(72598, 'Picric', 'Pertaining to or designating a strong organic acid called picric acid intensely bitter'),
(72599, 'Picrite', 'A dark green igneous rock consisting largely of chrysolite with hornblende augite biotite etc'),
(72600, 'Picrolite', 'A fibrous variety of serpentine'),
(72601, 'Picromel', 'A colorless viscous substance having a bitter sweet taste'),
(72602, 'Picrotoxin', 'A bitter white crystalline substance found in the cocculus indicus It is a peculiar poisonous neurotic and intoxicant and consists of a mixture of several neutral substances'),
(72603, 'Picryl', 'The hypothetical radical of picric acid analogous to phenyl'),
(72604, 'Pictish', 'Of or pertaining to Picts resembling the Picts'),
(72605, 'Pictograph', 'A picture or hieroglyph representing and expressing an idea'),
