(77940, 'Quater cousin', 'A cousin within the first four degrees of kindred'),
(77941, 'Quaternary', 'The number four'),
(77942, 'Quaternate', 'Composed of or arranged in sets of four quaternary as quaternate leaves'),
(77943, 'Quaternion', 'The number four'),
(77944, 'Quaternity', 'The number four'),
(77945, 'Quateron', 'See 2d Quarteron'),
(77946, 'Quatorzain', 'A poem of fourteen lines a sonnet'),
(77947, 'Quatorze', 'The four aces kings queens knaves or tens in the game of piquet    so called because quatorze counts as fourteen points'),
(77948, 'Quatrain', 'A stanza of four lines rhyming alternately'),
(77949, 'Quatre', 'A card die or domino having four spots or pips'),
(77950, 'Quatrefeuille', 'Same as Quarterfoil'),
(77951, 'Quattrocento', 'The fifteenth century when applied to Italian art or literature as the sculpture of the quattrocento quattrocento style'),
(77952, 'Quatuor', 'A quartet    applied chiefly to instrumental compositions'),
(77953, 'Quave', 'See Quaver'),
(77954, 'Quavemire', 'See Quagmire'),
(77955, 'Quaver', 'To tremble to vibrate to shake'),
(77956, 'Quaverer', 'One who quavers a warbler'),
(77957, 'Quay', 'A mole bank or wharf formed toward the sea or at the side of a harbor river or other navigable water for convenience in loading and unloading vessels'),
(77958, 'Quayage', 'Wharfage'),
(77959, 'Quayd', 'p p of Quail'),
(77960, 'qubit', 'A measure of information being the information that can be stored in one object that can take a quantum state of 0 or 1  It differs from the classical bit of information theory in that quantum states may assume many intermediate states that are superpositions of the individual discrete states the quantum states of multiple quantum systems may also be correlated by a phenomenon called entanglement increasing the complexity of the information storage and retrieval process  See 4th bit n'),
(77961, 'Que', 'A half farthing'),
(77962, 'Queach', 'A thick bushy plot a thicket'),
(77963, 'Queachy', 'Yielding or trembling under the feet as moist or boggy ground shaking moving'),
(77964, 'Quean', 'A woman a young or unmarried woman a girl'),
(77965, 'Queasily', 'In a queasy manner'),
(77966, 'Queasiness', 'The state of being queasy nausea qualmishness squeamishness'),
(77967, 'Queasy', 'Sick at the stomach affected with nausea inclined to vomit qualmish'),
(77968, 'Quebec group', 'The middle of the three groups into which the rocks of the Canadian period have been divided in the American Lower Silurian system See the Chart of Geology'),
(77969, 'Quebracho', 'A Chilian apocynaceous tree Aspidosperma Quebracho also its bark which is used as a febrifuge and for dyspn'),
(77970, 'Quebrith', 'Sulphur'),
(77971, 'Quech', 'A word occurring in a corrupt passage of Bacons Essays and probably meaning to stir to move'),
(77972, 'Queen', 'The wife of a king'),
(77973, 'Queencraft', 'Craft or skill in policy on the part of a queen'),
(77974, 'Queendom', 'The dominion condition or character of a queen'),
(77975, 'Queenfish', 'A California sci'),
(77976, 'Queenhood', 'The state personality or character of a queen queenliness'),
(77977, 'Queening', 'Any one of several kinds of apples as summer queening scarlet queening and early queening An apple called the queening was cultivated in England two hundred years ago'),
(77978, 'Queenliness', 'The quality of being queenly the characteristic of a queen stateliness eminence among women in attractions or power'),
(77979, 'Queenly', 'Like becoming or suitable to a queen'),
(77980, 'Queen olive', 'Properly a kind of superior olive grown in the region of Seville Spain It is large size and oblong shape with a small but long pit it is cured when green keeps well and has a delicate flavor Loosely any olive of similar character'),
(77981, 'Queen post', 'One of two suspending posts in a roof truss or other framed truss of similar form See King post'),
(77982, 'Queenship', 'The state rank or dignity of a queen'),
(77983, 'Queensland nut', 'The nut of an Australian tree Macadamia ternifolia It is about an inch in diameter and contains a single round edible seed or sometimes two hemispherical seeds So called from Queensland in Australia'),
(77984, 'Queen truss', 'A truss framed with queen posts a queen post truss'),
(77985, 'Queer', 'At variance with what is usual or normal differing in some odd way from what is ordinary odd singular strange whimsical as a queer story or act'),
(77986, 'Queerish', 'Rather queer somewhat singular'),
(77987, 'Queerly', 'In a queer or odd manner'),
(77988, 'Queerness', 'The quality or state of being queer'),
(77989, 'Queest', 'The European ringdove Columba palumbus the cushat'),
(77990, 'Quegh', 'A drinking vessel See Quaich'),
(77991, 'Queint', 'See Quaint'),
(77992, 'Queintise', 'See Quaintise'),
(77993, 'Quell', 'To take the life of to kill'),
(77994, 'Queller', 'A killer as Jack the Giant Queller'),
(77995, 'Quellio', 'A ruff for the neck'),
(77996, 'Quelquechose', 'A trifle a kickshaw'),
(77997, 'Queme', 'To please'),
(77998, 'Quemeful', 'Kindly merciful'),
(77999, 'Quench', 'To extinguish to overwhelm to make an end of    said of flame and fire of things burning and figuratively of sensations and emotions as to quench flame to quench a candle to quench thirst love hate etc'),
(78000, 'Quenchable', 'Capable of being quenched'),
(78001, 'Quencher', 'One who or that which quenches'),
(78002, 'Quenchless', 'Incapable of being quenched inextinguishable as quenchless fire or fury'),
(78003, 'Quenelle', 'A kind of delicate forcemeat commonly poached and used as a dish by itself or for garnishing'),
(78004, 'Quenouille training', 'A method of training trees or shrubs in the shape of a cone or distaff by tying down the branches and pruning'),
(78005, 'Quercitannic', 'Pertaining to or designating a tannic acid found in oak bark and extracted as a yellowish brown amorphous substance'),
(78006, 'Quercite', 'A white crystalline substance C6H7OH5 found in acorns the fruit of the oak Quercus  It has a sweet taste and is regarded as a pentacid alcohol'),
(78007, 'Quercitin', 'A yellow crystalline substance occurring quite widely distributed in the vegetable kingdom as in apple tree bark horse chestnut leaves etc but originally obtained by the decomposition of quercitrin Called also meletin'),
(78008, 'Quercitrin', 'A glucoside extracted from the bark of the oak Quercus as a bitter citron yellow crystalline substance used as a pigment and called quercitron'),
(78009, 'Quercitron', 'The yellow inner bark of the Quercus tinctoria the American black oak yellow oak dyers oak or quercitron oak a large forest tree growing from Maine to eastern Texas'),
(78010, 'Quercus', 'A genus of trees constituted by the oak See Oak'),
(78011, 'Querele', 'A complaint to a court See Audita Querela'),
(78012, 'Querent', 'A complainant a plaintiff'),
(78013, 'Querimonious', 'Complaining querulous apt to complain'),
(78014, 'Querimony', 'A complaint or complaining'),
(78015, 'Querist', 'One who inquires or asks questions'),
(78016, 'Querken', 'To stifle or choke'),
(78017, 'Querl', 'To twirl to turn or wind round to coil as to querl a cord thread or rope'),
(78018, 'Quern', 'A mill for grinding grain the upper stone of which was turned by hand    used before the invention of windmills and watermills'),
(78019, 'Querpo', 'The inner or body garments taken together See Cuerpo'),
(78020, 'Querquedule', 'A teal'),
(78021, 'Querry', 'A groom an equerry'),
(78022, 'Querulential', 'Querulous'),
(78023, 'Querulous', 'Given to quarreling quarrelsome'),
(78024, 'Query', 'A question an inquiry to be answered or solved');
INSERT INTO `dictionary` (`id`, `word`, `definition`) VALUES
(78025, 'Quesal', 'The long tailed or resplendent trogon Pharomachus mocinno formerly Trogon resplendens native of Southern Mexico and Central America Called also quetzal and golden trogon'),
(78026, 'Quest', 'The act of seeking or looking after anything attempt to find or obtain search pursuit as to rove in quest of game of a lost child of property etc'),
(78027, 'Questant', 'One who undertakes a quest a seeker'),
(78028, 'Quester', 'One who seeks a seeker'),
(78029, 'Question', 'The act of asking interrogation inquiry as to examine by question and answer'),
(78030, 'Questionability', 'The state or condition of being questionable'),
(78031, 'Questionable', 'Admitting of being questioned inviting or seeming to invite inquiry'),
(78032, 'Questionableness', 'The quality or state of being questionable doubtful or suspicious'),
(78033, 'Questionably', 'In a questionable manner'),
(78034, 'Questionary', 'Inquiring asking questions testing'),
(78035, 'Questioner', 'One who asks questions an inquirer'),
(78036, 'Questionist', 'A questioner an inquirer'),
(78037, 'Questionless', 'Unquestioning incurious'),
(78038, 'questionnaire', 'same as Questionary'),
(78039, 'Questman', 'One legally empowered to make quest of certain matters esp of abuses of weights and measures'),
(78040, 'Questmonger', 'One who lays informations and encourages petty lawsuits'),
(78041, 'Questor', 'An officer who had the management of the public treasure a receiver of taxes tribute etc treasurer of state'),
(78042, 'Questorship', 'The office or the term of office of a questor'),
(78043, 'Questrist', 'A seeker a pursuer'),
(78044, 'Questuary', 'Studious of profit'),
(78045, 'Quet', 'The common guillemot'),
(78046, 'Queue', 'A tail like appendage of hair a pigtail'),
(78047, 'Quey', 'A heifer'),
(78048, 'Quib', 'A quip a gibe'),
(78049, 'Quibble', 'A shift or turn from the point in question a trifling or evasive distinction an evasion a cavil'),
(78050, 'Quibbler', 'One who quibbles a caviler also a punster'),
(78051, 'Quibblingly', 'Triflingly evasively'),
(78052, 'Quica', 'A small South American opossum Didelphys quica native of Guiana and Brazil It feeds upon insects small birds and fruit'),
(78053, 'Quice', 'See Queest'),
(78054, 'Quich', 'To stir'),
(78055, 'Quichuan', 'Designating or pertaining to a linguistic stock of South American Indians including the majority of the civilized tribes of the ancient Peruvian Empire with some wild tribes never subjugated by the Incas Most of these Indians are short but heavy and strong They are brachycephalic and of remarkably low cranial capacity Nevertheless they represent one of the highest of native American civilizations characterized by agricultural military and administrative skill rather than by science or literature although they were adept potters weavers and goldsmiths and preserved by the aid of the mnemonic quipu a body of legendary lore in part written down since the introduction of writing'),
(78056, 'Quick', 'Alive living animate    opposed to dead or inanimate'),
(78057, 'Quickbeam', 'See Quicken tree'),
(78058, 'Quicken', 'To make alive to vivify to revive or resuscitate as from death or an inanimate state hence to excite to stimulate to incite'),
(78059, 'Quickener', 'One who or that which quickens'),
(78060, 'Quickening', 'The act or process of making or of becoming quick'),
(78061, 'Quickens', 'Quitch grass'),
(78062, 'Quicken tree', 'The European rowan tree    called also quickbeam and quickenbeam See Rowan tree'),
(78063, 'quick freeze', 'To freeze rapidly so as to preserve the natural juices and flavors    usually used of food or other biologicql matter'),
(78064, 'Quickhatch', 'The wolverine'),
(78065, 'Quicklime', 'Calcium oxide unslacked lime    so called because when wet it develops great heat See 4th Lime 2'),
(78066, 'Quickly', 'Speedily with haste or celerity soon without delay quick'),
(78067, 'Quickness', 'The condition or quality of being quick or living life'),
(78068, 'Quicksand', 'Sand easily moved or readily yielding to pressure especially a deep mass of loose or moving sand mixed with water sometimes found at the mouth of a river or along some coasts and very dangerous from the difficulty of extricating a person who begins sinking into it'),
(78069, 'Quick scented', 'Acute of smell'),
(78070, 'Quickset', 'A living plant set to grow esp when set for a hedge specifically the hawthorn'),
(78071, 'Quick sighted', 'Having quick sight or acute discernment quick to see or to discern'),
(78072, 'Quicksilver', 'The metal mercury    so called from its resemblance to liquid silver'),
(78073, 'Quicksilvered', 'Overlaid with quicksilver or with an amalgam of quicksilver and tinfoil'),
(78074, 'Quicksilvering', 'The mercury and foil on the back of a looking glass'),
(78075, 'Quickstep', 'A lively spirited march also a lively style of dancing'),
(78076, 'Quick witted', 'Having ready wit'),
(78077, 'Quick wittedness', 'Readiness of wit'),
(78078, 'Quickwork', 'All the submerged section of a vessels planking'),
(78079, 'Quid', 'A portion suitable to be chewed a cud as a quid of tobacco'),
(78080, 'Quidam', 'Somebody one unknown'),
(78081, 'Quiddany', 'A confection of quinces in consistency between a sirup and marmalade'),
(78082, 'Quiddative', 'Constituting or containing the essence of a thing quidditative'),
(78083, 'Quiddit', 'A subtilty an equivocation'),
(78084, 'Quidditative', 'Quiddative'),
(78085, 'Quiddity', 'The essence nature or distinctive peculiarity of a thing that which answers the question Quid est or What is it'),
(78086, 'Quiddle', 'To spend time in trifling employments or to attend to useful subjects in an indifferent or superficial manner to dawdle'),
(78087, 'Quidnunc', 'One who is curious to know everything that passes one who knows or pretends to know all that is going on'),
(78088, 'Quiesce', 'To be silent as a letter to have no sound'),
(78089, 'Quiescence', 'The state or quality of being quiescent'),
(78090, 'Quiescent', 'Being in a state of repose at rest still not moving as a quiescent body or fluid'),
(78091, 'Quiescently', 'In a quiescent manner'),
(78092, 'Quiet', 'In a state of rest or calm without stir motion or agitation still as a quiet sea quiet air'),
(78093, 'Quietage', 'Quietness'),
(78094, 'Quieter', 'One who or that which quiets'),
(78095, 'Quietism', 'Peace or tranquillity of mind calmness indifference apathy dispassion indisturbance inaction'),
(78096, 'Quietist', 'One of a sect of mystics originated in the seventeenth century by Molinos a Spanish priest living in Rome See Quietism'),
(78097, 'Quietistic', 'Of or pertaining to the Quietists or to Quietism'),
(78098, 'Quietly', 'In a quiet state or manner without motion in a state of rest as to lie or sit quietly'),
(78099, 'Quietness', 'The quality or state of being quiet freedom from noise agitation disturbance or excitement stillness tranquillity calmness'),
(78100, 'Quietsome', 'Calm still'),
(78101, 'Quietude', 'Rest repose quiet tranquillity'),
(78102, 'Quietus', 'Final discharge or acquittance as from debt or obligation that which silences claims Fig rest death'),
(78103, 'Quill', 'One of the large feathers of a birds wing or one of the rectrices of the tail also the stock of such a feather'),
(78104, 'Quillaia bark', 'The bark of a rosaceous tree Quillaja Saponaria native of Chile  The bark is finely laminated and very heavy with alkaline substances and is used commonly by the Chileans instead of soap  Also called soap bark'),
(78105, 'Quillback', 'An American fresh water fish Ictiobus cyprinus syn Carpiodes cyprinus    called also carp sucker sailfish spearfish and skimback'),
(78106, 'Quilled', 'Furnished with quills also shaped like quills'),
(78107, 'Quillet', 'Subtilty nicety quibble'),
(78108, 'Quilling', 'A band of linen muslin or the like fluted folded or plaited so as somewhat to resemble a row of quills'),
(78109, 'Quillwort', 'Any plant or species of the genus Isoetes cryptogamous plants with a cluster of elongated four tubed rushlike leaves rising from a corm and containing spores in their enlarged and excavated bases There are about seventeen American species usually growing in the mud under still shallow water So called from the shape of the shape of the leaves'),
(78110, 'Quilt', 'Anything that is quilted esp a quilted bed cover or a skirt worn by women any cover or garment made by putting wool cotton etc between two cloths and stitching them together also any outer bed cover'),
(78111, 'Quilter', 'One who or that which quilts'),
(78112, 'Quilting', 'The act of stitching or running in patterns as in making a quilt'),
(78113, 'Quin', 'A European scallop Pecten opercularis used as food'),
(78114, 'Quinaldine', 'A colorless liquid of a slightly pungent odor C9H6NCH3 first obtained as a condensation product of aldehyde and aniline and regarded as a derivative of quinoline    called also methyl quinoline'),
(78115, 'Quinary', 'Consisting of five arranged by fives'),
(78116, 'Quinate', 'Growing in sets of five    said especially of leaves composed of five leaflets set at the end of a common petiole'),
(78117, 'Quinazol', 'A complex nitrogenous base related to cinnoline'),
(78118, 'Quince', 'The fruit of a shrub Cydonia vulgaris belonging to the same tribe as the apple It somewhat resembles an apple but differs in having many seeds in each carpel It has hard flesh of high flavor but very acid and is largely used for marmalade jelly and preserves'),
(78119, 'Quincewort', 'The squinancy  Called also quinsywort'),
(78120, 'Quinch', 'To stir to wince'),
(78121, 'Quincuncially', 'In the manner or order of a quincunx'),
(78122, 'Quincunx', 'An arrangement of things by fives in a square or a rectangle one being placed at each corner and one in the middle especially such an arrangement of trees repeated indefinitely so as to form a regular group with rows running in various directions'),
(78123, 'Quindecagon', 'A plane figure with fifteen angles and consequently fifteen sides'),
(78124, 'Quindecemvir', 'One of a sacerdotal college of fifteen men whose chief duty was to take care of the Sibylline books'),
(78125, 'Quindecemvirate', 'The body or office of the quindecemviri'),
(78126, 'Quindecone', 'An unsaturated hydrocarbon C15H26 of the valylene series produced artificially as an oily liquid'),
(78127, 'Quindecylic', 'Pertaining to or designating an acid of the fatty acid series containing fifteen atoms of carbon called also pentadecylic acid'),
(78128, 'Quindem', 'A fifteenth part'),
(78129, 'Quindism', 'A fifteenth'),
(78130, 'Quinhydrone', 'A green crystalline substance formed by the union of quinone with hydroquinone or as an intermediate product in the oxidation of hydroquinone or the reduction of quinone'),
(78131, 'Quinia', 'Quinine'),
(78132, 'Quinible', 'An interval of a fifth also a part sung with such intervals'),
(78133, 'Quinic', 'Pertaining to derived from or connected with quinine and related compounds specifically designating a nonnitrogenous acid obtained from cinchona bark coffee beans etc as a white crystalline substance'),
(78134, 'Quinicine', 'An uncrystallizable alkaloid obtained by the action of heat from quinine with which it is isomeric'),
(78135, 'Quinidine', 'An alkaloid isomeric with and resembling quinine found in certain species of cinchona from which it is extracted as a bitter white crystalline substance conchinine It is used somewhat as a febrifuge'),
(78136, 'Quinine', 'An alkaloid extracted from the bark of several species of cinchona esp Cinchona Calisaya as a bitter white crystalline substance C20H24N2O2 Hence by extension Med any of the salts of this alkaloid as the acetate chloride sulphate etc employed as a febrifuge or antiperiodic Called also quinia quinina etc'),
(78137, 'Quininic', 'Pertaining to or designating a nitrogenous acid obtained as a yellow crystalline substance by the oxidation of quinine'),
(78138, 'Quininism', 'See Cinchonism'),
(78139, 'Quinizarin', 'A yellow crystalline substance produced artificially It is isomeric with alizarin'),
(78140, 'Quinizine', 'any one of a series of nitrogenous bases certain of which are used as antipyretics'),
(78141, 'Quinnat', 'The California salmon Oncorhynchus choicha    called also chouicha king salmon chinnook salmon and Sacramento salmon It is of great commercial importance'),
(78142, 'Quinoa', 'The seeds of a kind of goosewort Chenopodium Quinoa used in Chili and Peru for making porridge or cakes also food thus made'),
(78143, 'Quinogen', 'A hypothetical radical of quinine and related alkaloids'),
(78144, 'Quinoidine', 'A brownish resinous substance obtained as a by product in the treatment of cinchona bark  It consists of a mixture of several alkaloids'),
(78145, 'Quinoline', 'A nitrogenous base C9H7N obtained as a pungent colorless liquid by the distillation of alkaloids bones coal tar etc It the nucleus of many organic bodies especially of certain alkaloids and related substances hence by extension any one of the series of alkaloidal bases of which quinoline proper is the type'),
(78146, 'Quinologist', 'One who is versed in quinology'),
(78147, 'Quinology', 'The science which treats of the cultivation of the cinchona and of its use in medicine'),
(78148, 'Quinone', 'A crystalline substance C6H4O2 called also benzoketone first obtained by the oxidation of quinic acid and regarded as a double ketone also by extension any one of the series of which quinone proper is the type'),
(78149, 'Quinovic', 'Pertaining to or designating a crystalline acid obtained from some varieties of cinchona bark'),
(78150, 'Quinovin', 'An amorphous bitter glucoside derived from cinchona and other barks Called also quinova bitter and quinova'),
(78151, 'Quinoxaline', 'Any one of a series of complex nitrogenous bases obtained by the union of certain aniline derivatives with glyoxal or with certain ketones'),
(78152, 'Quinoxyl', 'The hypothetical radical of certain quinone derivatives related to rhodizonic acid'),
(78153, 'Quinoyl', 'A radical of which quinone is the hydride analogous to phenyl'),
(78154, 'Quinquagesima', 'Fiftieth'),
(78155, 'Quinquangular', 'Having five angles or corners'),
(78156, 'Quinquarticular', 'Relating to the five articles or points as the quinquarticular controversy between Arminians and Calvinists'),
(78157, 'Quinque ', 'A combining form meaning five five times fivefold as quinquefid five cleft quinquedentate five toothed'),
(78158, 'Quinqueangled', 'Having five angles quinquangular'),
(78159, 'Quinquedentate', 'Five toothed as a quinquedentate leaf'),
(78160, 'Quinquefarious', 'Arranged in five vertical rows pentastichous'),
(78161, 'Quinquefid', 'Sharply cut about halfway to the middle or base into five segments as a quinquefid leaf or corolla'),
(78162, 'Quinquefoliate', 'Having five leaves or leaflets'),
(78163, 'Quinque foliolate', 'Having five leaflets'),
(78164, 'Quinqueliteral', 'Consisting of five letters'),
(78165, 'Quinquelobate', 'Cut less than halfway into portions usually somewhat rounded five lobed as a quinquelobate leaf or corolla'),
(78166, 'Quinquelobed', 'Same as Quinquelobate'),
(78167, 'Quinquelocular', 'Having five cells or loculi five celled as a quinquelocular pericarp'),
(78168, 'Quinquenerved', 'Having five nerves    said of a leaf with five nearly equal nerves or ribs rising from the end of the petiole'),
(78169, 'Quinquennalia', 'Public games celebrated every five years'),
(78170, 'Quinquennial', 'Occurring once in five years or at the end of every five years also lasting five years A quinquennial event'),
(78171, 'Quinquennium', 'Space of five years'),
(78172, 'Quinquereme', 'A galley having five benches or banks of oars as an Athenian quinquereme'),
(78173, 'Quinquesyllable', 'A word of five syllables'),
(78174, 'Quinquevalve', 'Having five valves as a pericarp'),
(78175, 'Quinquevir', 'One of five commissioners appointed for some special object'),
(78176, 'Quinquina', 'Peruvian bark'),
(78177, 'Quinquivalent', 'Same as Pentavalent'),
(78178, 'Quinsy', 'An inflammation of the throat or parts adjacent especially of the fauces or tonsils attended by considerable swelling painful and impeded deglutition and accompanied by inflammatory fever It sometimes creates danger of suffocation    called also squinancy and squinzey'),
(78179, 'Quint', 'A set or sequence of five as in piquet'),
(78180, 'Quintain', 'An object to be tilted at    called also quintel'),
(78181, 'Quintal', 'A hundredweight either 112 or 100 pounds according to the scale used  Cf Cental'),
(78182, 'Quintan', 'Occurring as the fifth after four others also occurring every fifth day reckoning inclusively as a quintan fever'),
(78183, 'Quintel', 'See Quintain'),
(78184, 'Quintessence', 'The fifth or last and highest essence or power in a natural body See Ferment oils under Ferment'),
(78185, 'Quintessential', 'Of the nature of a quintessence purest most characteristic'),
(78186, 'Quintet', 'A composition for five voices or instruments also the set of five persons who sing or play five part music'),
(78187, 'Quintic', 'Of the fifth degree or order'),
(78188, 'Quintile', 'The aspect of planets when separated the fifth part of the zodiac or 72'),
(78189, 'Quintilllion', 'According to the French notation which is used on the Continent and in America the cube of a million or a unit with eighteen ciphers annexed according to the English notation a number produced by involving a million to the fifth power or a unit with thirty ciphers annexed See the Note under Numeration'),
(78190, 'Quintin', 'See Quintain'),
(78191, 'Quintine', 'The embryonic sac of an ovule sometimes regarded as an innermost fifth integument  Cf Quartine and Tercine'),
(78192, 'Quintole', 'A group of five notes to be played or sung in the time of four of the same species'),
(78193, 'Quintroon', 'The off spring of an octoroon and a white person'),
(78194, 'Quintuple', 'Multiplied by five increased to five times the amount fivefold'),
(78195, 'Quintuplet', 'A collection or combination of five of a kind'),
(78196, 'Quintuplets', 'five children born from one mother in a single pregnancy'),
(78197, 'Quintuple nerved', 'The same as Quinquenerved'),
(78198, 'Quinzaine', 'The fifteenth day after a feast day including both in the reckoning'),
(78199, 'Quinze', 'A game at cards in which the object is to make fifteen points'),
(78200, 'Quip', 'A smart sarcastic turn or jest a taunt a severe retort a gibe'),
(78201, 'Quipo', 'Same as Quipu'),
(78202, 'quipster', 'A person who frequently makes quips'),
(78203, 'Quipu', 'A contrivance employed by the ancient Peruvians Mexicans etc as a substitute for writing and figures consisting of a main cord from which hung at certain distances smaller cords of various colors each having a special meaning as silver gold corn soldiers etc Single double and triple knots were tied in the smaller cords representing definite numbers It was chiefly used for arithmetical purposes and to register important facts and events'),
(78204, 'Quirboilly', 'Leather softened by boiling so as to take any required shape Upon drying it becomes exceedingly hard and hence was formerly used for armor'),
(78205, 'Quire', 'See Choir'),
(78206, 'Quirinal', 'Of pertaining to or designating the hill Collis Quirinalis now Monte Quirinale one of the seven hills of Rome or a modern royal place situated upon it Also used substantively'),
(78207, 'Quirister', 'A chorister See Chorister'),
(78208, 'Quiritation', 'A crying for help'),
(78209, 'Quirite', 'One of the Quirites'),
(78210, 'Quirites', 'Roman citizens'),
(78211, 'Quirk', 'A sudden turn a starting from the point or line hence an artful evasion or subterfuge a shift a quibble as the quirks of a pettifogger'),
(78212, 'Quirked', 'Having or formed with a quirk or quirks'),
(78213, 'Quirkish', 'Consisting of quirks resembling a quirk'),
(78214, 'Quirky', 'Full of quirks tricky as a quirky lawyer'),
(78215, 'Quirl', 'See Querl'),
(78216, 'Quirpele', 'The Indian ferret'),
(78217, 'Quirt', 'A rawhide whip plaited with two thongs of buffalo hide'),
(78218, 'Quish', 'See Cuish'),
(78219, 'Quit', 'Any one of numerous species of small passerine birds native of tropical America See Banana quit under Banana and Guitguit'),
(78220, 'Quitch', 'Same as Quitch grass'),
