(84484, 'Scytheman', 'One who uses a scythe a mower'),
(84485, 'Scythestone', 'A stone for sharpening scythes a whetstone'),
(84486, 'Scythewhet', 'Wilsons thrush    so called from its note'),
(84487, 'Scythian', 'Of or pertaining to Scythia a name given to the northern part of Asia and Europe adjoining to Asia or its language or inhabitants'),
(84488, 'Scytodermata', 'Same as Holothurioidea'),
(84489, 'Sdain', 'Disdain'),
(84490, 'Sdeath', 'An exclamation expressive of impatience or anger'),
(84491, 'Sdeign', 'To disdain'),
(84492, 'Sea', 'One of the larger bodies of salt water less than an ocean found on the earths surface a body of salt water of second rank generally forming part of or connecting with an ocean or a larger sea as the Mediterranean Sea the Sea of Marmora the North Sea the Carribean Sea'),
(84493, 'Sea acorn', 'An acorn barnacle Balanus'),
(84494, 'Sea adder', 'The European fifteen spined stickleback Gasterosteus spinachia    called also bismore'),
(84495, 'Sea anchor', 'See Drag sail under 4th Drag'),
(84496, 'Sea anemone', 'Any one of numerous species of soft bodied Anthozoa belonging to the order Actinaria an actinian'),
(84497, 'Sea ape', 'The thrasher shark'),
(84498, 'Sea apple', 'The fruit of a West Indian palm Manicaria Plukenetii often found floating in the sea'),
(84499, 'Sea arrow', 'A squid of the genus Ommastrephes See Squid'),
(84500, 'Sea bank', 'The seashore'),
(84501, 'Sea bar', 'A tern'),
(84502, 'Sea barrow', 'A sea purse'),
(84503, 'Sea bass', 'A large marine food fish Serranus atrarius syn Centropristis atrarius which abounds on the Atlantic coast of the United States  It is dark bluish with black bands and more or less varied with small white spots and blotches  Called also locally blue bass black sea bass blackfish bluefish and black perch'),
(84504, 'Sea bat', 'See Batfish a'),
(84505, 'Seabeach', 'A beach lying along the sea'),
(84506, 'Sea bean', 'Same as Florida bean'),
(84507, 'Sea bear', 'Any fur seal See under Fur'),
(84508, 'Seabeard', 'A green seaweed Cladophora rupestris growing in dense tufts'),
(84509, 'Sea beast', 'Any large marine mammal as a seal walrus or cetacean'),
(84510, 'Sea bird', 'Any swimming bird frequenting the sea a sea fowl'),
(84511, 'Sea blite', 'A plant Suaeda maritima of the Goosefoot family growing in salt marshes'),
(84512, 'Sea blubber', 'A jellyfish'),
(84513, 'Seaboard', 'The seashore seacoast'),
(84514, 'Seaboat', 'A boat or vessel adapted to the open sea hence a vessel considered with reference to her power of resisting a storm or maintaining herself in a heavy sea as a good sea boat'),
(84515, 'Seabord', 'See Seaboard'),
(84516, 'Sea bordering', 'Bordering on the sea situated beside the sea'),
(84517, 'Sea born', 'Born of the sea produced by the sea'),
(84518, 'Seabound', 'Bounded by the sea'),
(84519, 'Sea bow', 'See Marine rainbow under Rainbow'),
(84520, 'Sea boy', 'A boy employed on shipboard'),
(84521, 'Sea breach', 'A breaking or overflow of a bank or a dike by the sea'),
(84522, 'Sea bream', 'Any one of several species of sparoid fishes especially the common European species Pagellus centrodontus the Spanish Pagellus Oweni and the black sea bream Cantharus lineatus    called also old wife'),
(84523, 'Sea brief', 'Same as Sea letter'),
(84524, 'Sea bug', 'A chiton'),
(84525, 'Sea built', 'Built at in or by the sea'),
(84526, 'Sea butterfly', 'A pteropod'),
(84527, 'Sea cabbage', 'See Sea kale under Kale'),
(84528, 'Sea calf', 'The common seal'),
(84529, 'Sea canary', 'The beluga or white whale'),
(84530, 'Sea captain', 'The captain of a vessel that sails upon the sea'),
(84531, 'Sea card', 'Mariners card or compass'),
(84532, 'Sea catfish', 'The wolf fish'),
(84533, 'Sea chart', 'A chart or map on which the lines of the shore islands shoals harbors etc are delineated'),
(84534, 'Sea chickweed', 'A fleshy plant Arenaria peploides growing in large tufts in the sands of the northern Atlantic seacoast    called also sea sandwort and sea purslane'),
(84535, 'Sea clam', 'Any one of the large bivalve mollusks found on the open seacoast especially those of the family Mactridae as the common American species Mactra solidissima or Spisula solidissima    called also beach clam and surf clam'),
(84536, 'Sea coal', 'Coal brought by sea    a name by which mineral coal was formerly designated in the south of England in distinction from charcoal which was brought by land'),
(84537, 'Seacoast', 'The shore or border of the land adjacent to the sea or ocean Also used adjectively'),
(84538, 'Sea cob', 'The black backed gull'),
(84539, 'Sea cock', 'In a steamship a cock or valve close to the vessels side for closing a pipe which communicates with the sea'),
(84540, 'Sea cocoa', 'A magnificent palm Lodoicea Sechellarum found only in the Seychelles Islands The fruit is an immense two lobed nut It was found floating in the Indian Ocean before the tree was known and called sea cocoanut and double cocoanut'),
(84541, 'Sea colander', 'A large blackfish seaweed Agarum Turneri the frond of which is punctured with many little holes'),
(84542, 'Sea colewort', 'Sea cabbage'),
(84543, 'Sea compass', 'The mariners compass See under Compass'),
(84544, 'Sea coot', 'A scoter duck'),
(84545, 'Sea corn', 'A yellow cylindrical mass of egg capsules of certain species of whelks Buccinum which resembles an ear of maize'),
(84546, 'Sea cow', 'The mantee'),
(84547, 'Sea crawfish', 'Any crustacean of the genus Palinurus and allied genera as the European spiny lobster Palinurus vulgaris which is much used as an article of food See Lobster'),
(84548, 'Sea crow', 'The chough'),
(84549, 'Sea cucumber', 'Any large holothurian especially one of those belonging to the genus Pentacta or Cucumaria as the common American and European species Pentacta frondosa'),
(84550, 'Sea dace', 'The European sea perch'),
(84551, 'Sea daffodil', 'A European amaryllidaceous plant Pancratium maritimum'),
(84552, 'Sea devil', 'Any very large ray especially any species of the genus Manta or Cephaloptera some of which become more than twenty feet across and weigh several tons See also Ox ray under Ox'),
(84553, 'Sea dog', 'The dogfish'),
(84554, 'Sea dotterel', 'The turnstone'),
(84555, 'Sea dove', 'The little auk or rotche See Illust of Rotche'),
(84556, 'Sea dragon', 'A dragonet or sculpin'),
(84557, 'Sea drake', 'The pewit gull'),
(84558, 'Sea duck', 'Any one of numerous species of ducks which frequent the seacoasts and feed mainly on fishes and mollusks The scoters eiders old squaw and ruddy duck are examples They may be distinguished by the lobate hind toe'),
(84559, 'Sea eagle', 'Any one of several species of fish eating eagles of the genus Haliaeetus and allied genera as the North Pacific sea eagle Haliaeetus pelagicus which has white shoulders head rump and tail the European white tailed eagle Haliaeetus albicilla and the Indian white tailed sea eagle or fishing eagle Polioaetus ichthyaetus  The bald eagle and the osprey are also sometimes classed as sea eagles'),
(84560, 'Sea ear', 'Any species of ear shaped shells of the genus Haliotis See Abalone'),
(84561, 'Sea eel', 'The conger eel'),
(84562, 'Sea egg', 'A sea urchin'),
(84563, 'Sea elephant', 'A very large seal Macrorhinus proboscideus of the Antarctic seas much hunted for its oil It sometimes attains a length of thirty feet and is remarkable for the prolongation of the nose of the adult male into an erectile elastic proboscis about a foot in length Another species of smaller size Macrorhinus angustirostris occurs on the coast of Lower California but is now nearly extinct'),
(84564, 'Sea fan', 'Any gorgonian which branches in a fanlike form especially Gorgonia flabellum of Florida and the West Indies'),
(84565, 'Seafarer', 'One who follows the sea as a business a mariner a sailor'),
(84566, 'Seafaring', 'Following the business of a mariner as a seafaring man'),
(84567, 'Sea feather', 'Any gorgonian which branches in a plumelike form'),
(84568, 'Sea fennel', 'Samphire'),
(84569, 'Sea fern', 'Any gorgonian which branches like a fern'),
(84570, 'Sea fight', 'An engagement between ships at sea a naval battle'),
(84571, 'Sea fir', 'A sertularian hydroid especially Sertularia abietina which branches like a miniature fir tree'),
(84572, 'Sea flower', 'A sea anemone or any related anthozoan'),
(84573, 'Sea foam', 'Foam of sea water'),
(84574, 'Sea fowl', 'Any bird which habitually frequents the sea as an auk gannet gull tern or petrel also all such birds collectively'),
(84575, 'Sea fox', 'The thrasher shark See Thrasher'),
(84576, 'Sea froth', 'See Sea foam 2'),
(84577, 'Sea gate', 'A long rolling swell of the sea'),
(84578, 'Sea gauge', 'See under Gauge n'),
(84579, 'Sea gherkin', 'Any small holothurian resembling in form a gherkin'),
(84580, 'Sea ginger', 'A hydroid coral of the genus Millepora especially Millepora alcicornis of the West Indies and Florida  So called because it stings the tongue like ginger See Illust under Millepore'),
(84581, 'Sea girdles', 'A kind of kelp Laminaria digitata with palmately cleft fronds    called also sea wand seaware and tangle'),
(84582, 'Seagirt', 'Surrounded by the water of the sea or ocean as a seagirt isle'),
(84583, 'Sea god', 'A marine deity a fabulous being supposed to live in or have dominion over the sea or some particular sea or part of the sea as Neptune'),
(84584, 'Sea goddess', 'A goddess supposed to live in or reign over the sea or some part of the sea'),
(84585, 'Seagoing', 'Going upon the sea especially sailing upon the deep sea    used in distinction from coasting or river as applied to vessels'),
(84586, 'Sea goose', 'A phalarope'),
(84587, 'Sea gown', 'A gown or frock with short sleeves formerly worn by mariners'),
(84588, 'Sea grape', 'The gulf weed See under Gulf'),
(84589, 'Sea grass', 'Eelgrass'),
(84590, 'Sea green', 'The green color of sea water'),
(84591, 'Sea gudgeon', 'The European black goby Gobius niger'),
(84592, 'Sea gull', 'Any gull living on the seacoast'),
(84593, 'Seah', 'A Jewish dry measure containing one third of an ephah'),
(84594, 'Sea hare', 'Any tectibranchiate mollusk of the genus Aplysia See Aplysia'),
(84595, 'Sea hawk', 'A jager gull'),
(84596, 'Sea heath', 'A low perennial plant Frankenia laevis resembling heath growing along the seashore in Europe'),
(84597, 'Sea hedgehog', 'A sea urchin'),
(84598, 'Sea hen', 'The common guillemot    applied also to various other sea birds'),
(84599, 'Sea hog', 'The porpoise'),
(84600, 'Sea holly', 'An evergeen seashore plant Eryngium maritimum See Eryngium'),
(84601, 'Sea holm', 'A small uninhabited island'),
(84602, 'Sea horse', 'A fabulous creature half horse and half fish represented in classic mythology as driven by sea dogs or ridden by the Nereids It is also depicted in heraldry See Hippocampus'),
(84603, 'Sea hulver', 'Sea holly'),
(84604, 'Sea island', 'Of or pertaining to certain islands along the coast of South Carolina and Georgia as sea island cotton a superior cotton of long fiber produced on those islands'),
(84605, 'Sea jelly', 'A medusa or jellyfish'),
(84606, 'Seak', 'Soap prepared for use in milling cloth'),
(84607, 'Sea kale', 'See under Kale'),
(84608, 'Sea king', 'One of the leaders among the Norsemen who passed their lives in roving the seas in search of plunder and adventures a Norse pirate chief See the Note under Viking'),
(84609, 'Seal', 'Any aquatic carnivorous mammal of the families Phocidae and Otariidae'),
(84610, 'Sea laces', 'A kind of seaweed Chorda Filum having blackish cordlike fronds often many feet long'),
(84611, 'Sea lamprey', 'The common lamprey'),
(84612, 'Sea language', 'The peculiar language or phraseology of seamen sailors cant'),
(84613, 'Sea lark', 'The rock pipit Anthus obscurus'),
(84614, 'Sea lavender', 'See Marsh rosemary under Marsh'),
(84615, 'Sea lawyer', 'The gray snapper See under Snapper'),
(84616, 'Seal brown', 'Of a rich dark brown color like the fur of the fur seal after it is dyed'),
(84617, 'Sea legs', 'Legs able to maintain their possessor upright in stormy weather at sea that is ability to stand or walk steadily on deck when a vessel is rolling or pitching in a rough sea'),
(84618, 'Sea lemon', 'Any one of several species of nudibranchiate mollusks of the genus Doris and allied genera having a smooth thick convex yellow body'),
(84619, 'Sea leopard', 'Any one of several species of spotted seals especially Ogmorhinus leptonyx and Leptonychotes Weddelli of the Antarctic Ocean The North Pacific sea leopard is the harbor seal'),
(84620, 'Sealer', 'One who seals especially an officer whose duty it is to seal writs or instruments to stamp weights and measures or the like'),
(84621, 'Sea letter', 'The customary certificate of national character which neutral merchant vessels are bound to carry in time of war a passport for a vessel and cargo'),
(84622, 'Sea lettuce', 'The green papery fronds of several seaweeds of the genus Ulva sometimes used as food'),
(84623, 'Sea level', 'The level of the surface of the sea any surface on the same level with the sea'),
(84624, 'Sealgh', 'A seal'),
(84625, 'Sea lily', 'A crinoid'),
(84626, 'Sealing wax', 'A compound of the resinous materials pigments etc used as a material for seals as for letters documents etc'),
(84627, 'Sea lion', 'Any one of several large species of seals of the family Otariidae native of the Pacific Ocean especially the southern sea lion Otaria jubata of the South American coast the northern sea lion Eumetopias Stelleri found from California to Japan and the black or California sea lion Zalophus Californianus which is common on the rocks near San Francisco'),
(84628, 'Sea loach', 'The three bearded rockling See Rockling'),
(84629, 'Sea louse', 'Any one of numerous species of isopod crustaceans of Cymothoa Livoneca and allied genera mostly parasites on fishes'),
(84630, 'Sealskin', 'The skin of a seal the pelt of a seal prepared for use esp of the fur seal also a garment made of this material'),
(84631, 'Seam', 'Grease tallow lard'),
(84632, 'Sea maid', 'The mermaid'),
(84633, 'Sea mail', 'A gull the mew'),
(84634, 'Seaman', 'A merman the male of the mermaid'),
(84635, 'Seamanlike', 'Having or showing the skill of a practical seaman'),
(84636, 'Seamanship', 'The skill of a good seaman the art or skill in the art of working a ship'),
(84637, 'Sea mantis', 'A squilla'),
(84638, 'Sea marge', 'Land which borders on the sea the seashore'),
(84639, 'Seamark', 'Any elevated object on land which serves as a guide to mariners a beacon a landmark visible from the sea as a hill a tree a steeple or the like'),
(84640, 'Sea mat', 'Any bryozoan of the genus Flustra or allied genera which form frondlike corals'),
(84641, 'Sea maw', 'The sea mew'),
(84642, 'Seamed', 'Out of condition not in good condition    said of a hawk'),
(84643, 'Sea mell', 'The sea mew'),
(84644, 'Sea mew', 'A gull the mew'),
(84645, 'Sea mile', 'A geographical mile See Mile'),
(84646, 'Sea milkwort', 'A low fleshy perennial herb Glaux maritima found along northern seashores'),
(84647, 'Seaming', 'The act or process of forming a seam or joint'),
(84648, 'Seamless', 'Without a seam'),
(84649, 'Sea monk', 'See Monk seal under Monk'),
(84650, 'Sea monster', 'Any large sea animal'),
(84651, 'Sea moss', 'Any branched marine bryozoan resembling moss'),
(84652, 'Sea mouse', 'A dorsibranchiate annelid belonging to Aphrodite and allied genera having long slender hairlike setae on the sides'),
(84653, 'Seamster', 'One who sews well or whose occupation is to sew'),
(84654, 'Seamstress', 'A woman whose occupation is sewing a needlewoman'),
(84655, 'Seamstressy', 'The business of a seamstress'),
(84656, 'Sea mud', 'A rich slimy deposit in salt marshes and along the seashore sometimes used as a manure    called also sea ooze'),
(84657, 'Seamy', 'Having a seam containing seams or showing them'),
(84658, 'Sean', 'A seine See Seine'),
(84659, 'Sea needle', 'See Garfish a'),
(84660, 'Sea nettle', 'A jellyfish or medusa'),
(84661, 'Seannachie', 'A bard among the Highlanders of Scotland who preserved and repeated the traditions of the tribes also a genealogist'),
(84662, 'Sea onion', 'The officinal squill See Squill'),
(84663, 'Sea ooze', 'Same as Sea mud'),
(84664, 'Sea orange', 'A large American holothurian Lophothuria Fabricii having a bright orange convex body covered with finely granulated scales  Its expanded tentacles are bright red'),
(84665, 'Sea orb', 'A globefish'),
(84666, 'Sea otter', 'An aquatic carnivore Enhydris lutris syn Enhydris marina found in the North Pacific Ocean   Its fur is highly valued especially by the Chinese  It is allied to the common otter but is larger with feet more decidedly webbed'),
(84667, 'Sea owl', 'The lumpfish'),
(84668, 'Sea pad', 'A starfish'),
(84669, 'Sea parrot', 'The puffin'),
(84670, 'Sea partridge', 'The gilthead Crenilabrus melops a fish of the British coasts'),
(84671, 'Sea pass', 'A document carried by neutral merchant vessels in time of war to show their nationality a sea letter or passport See Passport'),
(84672, 'Sea peach', 'A beautiful American ascidian Cynthia pyriformis syn Halocynthia pyriformis having the size form velvety surface and color of a ripe peach'),
(84673, 'Sea pear', 'A pedunculated ascidian of the genus Boltonia'),
(84674, 'Sea pen', 'A pennatula'),
(84675, 'Sea perch', 'The European bass Roccus lupus syn Labrax lupus    called also sea dace'),
(84676, 'Sea pheasant', 'The pintail duck'),
(84677, 'Sea pie', 'The oyster catcher a limicoline bird of the genus Haematopus'),
(84678, 'Seapiece', 'A picture representing a scene at sea a marine picture'),
(84679, 'Sea piet', 'See 1st Sea pie'),
(84680, 'Sea pig', 'A porpoise or dolphin'),
(84681, 'Sea pigeon', 'The common guillemot'),
(84682, 'Sea pike', 'The garfish'),
(84683, 'Sea pincushion', 'A sea purse'),
(84684, 'Sea pink', 'See Thrift'),
(84685, 'Sea plover', 'The black bellied plover'),
(84686, 'Sea poacher', 'The lyrie'),
(84687, 'Sea pool', 'A pool of salt water'),
(84688, 'Sea poppy', 'The horn poppy See under Horn'),
(84689, 'Sea porcupine', 'Any fish of the genus Diodon and allied genera whose body is covered with spines See Illust under Diodon'),
(84690, 'Sea pork', 'An American compound ascidian Amoroecium stellatum which forms large whitish masses resembling salt pork'),
(84691, 'Seaport', 'A port on the seashore or one accessible for seagoing vessels Also used adjectively as a seaport town'),
(84692, 'Seapoy', 'See Sepoy'),
(84693, 'Sea pudding', 'Any large holothurian'),
(84694, 'Sea purse', 'The horny egg case of a skate and of certain sharks'),
(84695, 'Sea purslane', 'See under Purslane'),
(84696, 'Sea pye', 'See 1st Sea pie'),
(84697, 'Sea pyot', 'See 1st Sea pie'),
(84698, 'Sea quail', 'The turnstone'),
(84699, 'Seaquake', 'A quaking of the sea'),
(84700, 'Sear', 'Dry withered no longer green    applied to leaves'),
(84701, 'Sea rat', 'A pirate'),
(84702, 'Sea raven', 'An American cottoid fish Hemitripterus Americanus allied to the sculpins found on the northern Atlantic coasts'),
(84703, 'Searce', 'A fine sieve'),
(84704, 'Searcer', 'One who sifts or bolts'),
(84705, 'Search', 'To look over or through for the purpose of finding something to examine to explore as to search the city'),
(84706, 'Searchable', 'Capable of being searched'),
(84707, 'Searchableness', 'Quality of being searchable'),
(84708, 'Searcher', 'One who or that which searches or examines a seeker an inquirer an examiner a trier'),
(84709, 'Searching', 'Exploring thoroughly scrutinizing penetrating trying as a searching discourse a searching eye'),
(84710, 'Searchless', 'Impossible to be searched inscrutable impenetrable'),
(84711, 'Searchlight', 'An apparatus for projecting a powerful beam of light of approximately parallel rays usually devised so that it can be swiveled about'),
(84712, 'Searcloth', 'Cerecloth'),
(84713, 'Seared', 'Scorched cauterized hence figuratively insensible not susceptible to moral influences'),
(84714, 'Searedness', 'The state of being seared or callous insensibility'),
(84715, 'Sea reed', 'The sea sand reed See under Reed'),
(84716, 'Sea risk', 'Risk of injury destruction or loss by the sea or while at sea'),
(84717, 'Sea robber', 'A pirate a sea rover'),
(84718, 'Sea robin', 'See under Robin and Illustration in Appendix'),
(84719, 'Sea rocket', 'See under Rocket'),
(84720, 'Sea room', 'Room or space at sea for a vessel to maneuver drive or scud without peril of running ashore or aground'),
(84721, 'Sea rover', 'One that cruises or roves the sea for plunder a sea robber a pirate also a piratical vessel'),
(84722, 'Sea roving', 'Cruising at random on the ocean'),
(84723, 'Sea salmon', 'A young pollock'),
(84724, 'Sea salt', 'Common salt obtained from sea water by evaporation'),
(84725, 'Sea sandpiper', 'The purple sandpiper'),
(84726, 'Sea sandwort', 'See Sea chickweed'),
(84727, 'Sea saurian', 'Any marine saurian esp Paleon the large extinct species of Mosasaurus Ichthyosaurus Plesiosaurus and related genera'),
(84728, 'Seascape', 'A picture representing a scene at sea  Compare landscape'),
(84729, 'Sea scorpion', 'A European sculpin Cottus scorpius having the head armed with short spines'),
(84730, 'Sea scurf', 'Any bryozoan which forms rounded or irregular patches of coral on stones seaweeds etc'),
(84731, 'Sea serpent', 'Any marine snake See Sea snake'),
(84732, 'Seashell', 'The shell of any marine mollusk'),
(84733, 'Seashore', 'The coast of the sea the land that lies adjacent to the sea or ocean'),
(84734, 'Seasick', 'Affected with seasickness'),
(84735, 'Seasickness', 'The peculiar sickness characterized by nausea and prostration which is caused by the pitching or rolling of a vessel'),
(84736, 'Seaside', 'The land bordering on or adjacent to the sea the seashore Also used adjectively'),
(84737, 'Sea slater', 'Any isopod crustacean of the genus Ligia'),
(84738, 'Sea slug', 'A holothurian'),
(84739, 'Sea snail', 'A small fish of the genus Liparis having a ventral sucker It lives among stones and seaweeds'),
(84740, 'Sea snake', 'Any one of many species of venomous aquatic snakes of the family Hydrophidae having a flattened tail and living entirely in the sea especially in the warmer parts of the Indian and Pacific Oceans They feed upon fishes and are mostly of moderate size but some species become eight or ten feet long and four inches broad'),
(84741, 'Sea snipe', 'A sandpiper as the knot and dunlin');
INSERT INTO `dictionary` (`id`, `word`, `definition`) VALUES
(84742, 'Season', 'One of the divisions of the year marked by alterations in the length of day and night or by distinct conditions of temperature moisture etc caused mainly by the relative position of the earth with respect to the sun In the north temperate zone four seasons namely spring summer autumn and winter are generally recognized Some parts of the world have three seasons    the dry the rainy and the cold other parts have but two    the dry and the rainy'),
(84743, 'Seasonable', 'Occurring in good time in due season or in proper time for the purpose suitable to the season opportune timely as a seasonable supply of rain'),
(84744, 'Seasonage', 'A seasoning'),
(84745, 'Seasonal', 'Of or pertaining to the seasons'),
(84746, 'seasonal affective disorder', 'A form of mild depression that occurs in winters associated with reduction in the amount of sunlight  It is characterized by oversleeping irritability and sometimes overeating  It can be treated by light therapy and usually disappears with the arrival of spring'),
(84747, 'Seasoner', 'One who or that which seasons or gives a relish a seasoning'),
(84748, 'Seasoning', 'The act or process by which anything is seasoned'),
(84749, 'Seasonless', 'Without succession of the seasons'),
(84750, 'Sea spider', 'Any maioid crab a spider crab See Maioid and Spider crab under Spider'),
(84751, 'Sea squirt', 'An ascidian See Illust under Tunicata'),
(84752, 'Sea star', 'A starfish or brittle star'),
(84753, 'Sea surgeon', 'A surgeon fish'),
(84754, 'Sea swallow', 'The common tern'),
(84755, 'Seat', 'The place or thing upon which one sits hence anything made to be sat in or upon as a chair bench stool saddle or the like'),
(84756, 'Sea tang', 'A kind of seaweed tang tangle'),
(84757, 'Sea term', 'A term used specifically by seamen a nautical word or phrase'),
(84758, 'Sea thief', 'A pirate'),
(84759, 'Sea thongs', 'A kind of blackish seaweed Himanthalia lorea found on the northern coasts of the Atlantic It has a thonglike forking process rising from a top shaped base'),
(84760, 'Seating', 'The act of providing with a seat or seats as the seating of an audience'),
(84761, 'Sea titling', 'The rock pipit'),
(84762, 'Seatless', 'Having no seat'),
(84763, 'Sea toad', 'A sculpin'),
(84764, 'Sea trout', 'Any one of several species of true trouts which descend rivers and enter the sea after spawning as the European bull trout and salmon trout and the eastern American spotted trout'),
(84765, 'Sea trumpet', 'A great blackish seaweed of the Southern Ocean having a hollow and expanding stem and a pinnate frond sometimes twenty feet long'),
(84766, 'Sea turn', 'A breeze gale or mist from the sea'),
(84767, 'Sea turtle', 'Any one of several very large species of chelonians having the feet converted into paddles as the green turtle hawkbill loggerhead and leatherback They inhabit all warm seas'),
(84768, 'Sea unicorn', 'The narwhal'),
(84769, 'Sea urchin', 'Any one of numerous species of echinoderms of the order Echinoidea'),
(84770, 'Seave', 'A rush'),
(84771, 'Seavy', 'Overgrown with rushes'),
(84772, 'Sea wall', 'A wall or embankment to resist encroachments of the sea'),
(84773, 'Sea walled', 'Surrounded bounded or protected by the sea as if by a wall'),
(84774, 'Seawan', 'The name used by the Algonquin Indians for the shell beads which passed among the Indians as money'),
(84775, 'Seawand', 'See Sea girdles'),
(84776, 'Seaward', 'Directed or situated toward the sea'),
(84777, 'Seaware', 'Seaweed esp coarse seaweed See Ware and Sea girdles'),
(84778, 'Seaweed', 'Popularly any plant or plants growing in the sea'),
(84779, 'Sea whip', 'A gorgonian having a simple stem'),
