(88259, 'Snuffy', 'Soiled with snuff'),
(88260, 'Snug', 'Close and warm as an infant lies snug'),
(88261, 'Snuggery', 'A snug cozy place'),
(88262, 'Snuggle', 'To move one way and the other so as to get a close place to lie close for comfort to cuddle to nestle'),
(88263, 'Snugly', 'In a snug manner closely safely'),
(88264, 'Snugness', 'The quality or state of being snug'),
(88265, 'Sny', 'An upward bend in a piece of timber the sheer of a vessel'),
(88266, 'Snying', 'A curved plank placed edgewise to work in the bows of a vessel'),
(88267, 'So', 'In that manner or degree as indicated in any way or as implied or as supposed to be known'),
(88268, 'Soak', 'To cause or suffer to lie in a fluid till the substance has imbibed what it can contain to macerate in water or other liquid to steep as for the purpose of softening or freshening as to soak cloth to soak bread to soak salt meat salt fish or the like'),
(88269, 'Soakage', 'The act of soaking or the state of being soaked also the quantity that enters or issues by soaking'),
(88270, 'Soaker', 'One who or that which soaks'),
(88271, 'Soaking', 'Wetting thoroughly drenching as a soaking rain'),
(88272, 'Soaky', 'Full of moisture wet soppy'),
(88273, 'Soal', 'The sole of a shoe'),
(88274, 'Soam', 'A chain by which a leading horse draws a plow'),
(88275, 'Soap', 'A substance which dissolves in water thus forming a lather and is used as a cleansing agent Soap is produced by combining fats or oils with alkalies or alkaline earths usually by boiling and consists of salts of sodium potassium etc with the fatty acids oleic stearic palmitic etc See the Note below and cf Saponification By extension any compound of similar composition or properties whether used as a cleaning agent or not'),
(88276, 'Soapberry tree', 'Any tree of the genus Sapindus esp Sapindus saponaria the fleshy part of whose fruit is used instead of soap in washing linen    also called soap tree'),
(88277, 'Soapfish', 'Any serranoid fish of the genus Rhypticus    so called from the soapy feeling of its skin'),
(88278, 'Soapiness', 'Quality or state of being soapy'),
(88279, 'Soaproot', 'A perennial herb Gypsophila Struthium the root of which is used in Spain as a substitute for soap'),
(88280, 'Soapstone', 'See Steatite and Talc'),
(88281, 'Soapsuds', 'Suds made with soap'),
(88282, 'Soapwort', 'A common plant Saponaria officinalis of the Pink family    so called because its bruised leaves when agitated in water produce a lather like that from soap Called also Bouncing Bet'),
(88283, 'Soapy', 'Resembling soap having the qualities of or feeling like soap soft and smooth'),
(88284, 'Soar', 'To fly aloft as a bird to mount upward on wings or as on wings'),
(88285, 'Soaring', 'a  n from Soar'),
(88286, 'Soave', 'Sweet'),
(88287, 'Soavemente', 'Sweetly'),
(88288, 'Sob', 'To soak'),
(88289, 'Sobbing', 'A series of short convulsive inspirations the glottis being suddenly closed so that little or no air enters into the lungs'),
(88290, 'Sober', 'Temperate in the use of spirituous liquors habitually temperate as a sober man'),
(88291, 'Soberize', 'To sober'),
(88292, 'Soberly', 'In a sober manner temperately cooly calmly gravely seriously'),
(88293, 'Sober minded', 'Having a disposition or temper habitually sober'),
(88294, 'Soberness', 'The quality or state of being sober'),
(88295, 'Soboles', 'A shoot running along under ground forming new plants at short distances'),
(88296, 'Soboliferous', 'Producing soboles See Illust of Houseleek'),
(88297, 'Sobranje', 'The unicameral national assembly of Bulgaria elected for a term of five years by universal suffrage of adult males'),
(88298, 'Sobriety', 'Habitual soberness or temperance as to the use of spirituous liquors as a man of sobriety'),
(88299, 'Sobriquet', 'An assumed name a fanciful epithet or appellation a nickname'),
(88300, 'Soc', 'The lords power or privilege of holding a court in a district as in manor or lordship jurisdiction of causes and the limits of that jurisdiction'),
(88301, 'Socage', 'A tenure of lands and tenements by a certain or determinate service a tenure distinct from chivalry or knights service in which the obligations were uncertain The service must be certain in order to be denominated socage as to hold by fealty and twenty shillings rent'),
(88302, 'Socager', 'A tennant by socage a socman'),
(88303, 'So called', 'So named called by such a name but perhaps called thus with doubtful propriety'),
(88304, 'Sociability', 'The quality of being sociable sociableness'),
(88305, 'Sociable', 'Capable of being or fit to be united in one body or company associable'),
(88306, 'Sociableness', 'The quality of being sociable'),
(88307, 'Sociably', 'In a sociable manner'),
(88308, 'Social', 'Of or pertaining to society relating to men living in society or to the public as an aggregate body as social interest or concerns social pleasure social benefits social happiness social duties'),
(88309, 'Socialism', 'A theory or system of social reform which contemplates a complete reconstruction of society with a more just and equitable distribution of property and labor In popular usage the term is often employed to indicate any lawless revolutionary social scheme See Communism Fourierism Saint Simonianism forms of socialism'),
(88310, 'Socialist', 'One who advocates or practices the doctrines of socialism'),
(88311, 'Sociality', 'The quality of being social socialness'),
(88312, 'Socialize', 'To render social'),
(88313, 'Socially', 'In a social manner sociably'),
(88314, 'Socialness', 'The quality or state of being social'),
(88315, 'Sociate', 'Associated'),
(88316, 'Societarian', 'Of or pertaining to society social'),
(88317, 'Societary', 'Societarian'),
(88318, 'Society', 'The relationship of men to one another when associated in any way companionship fellowship company'),
(88319, 'Socinian', 'Of or pertaining to Socinus or the Socinians'),
(88320, 'Socinianism', 'The tenets or doctrines of Faustus Socinus an Italian theologian of the sixteenth century who denied the Trinity the deity of Christ the personality of the Devil the native and total depravity of man the vicarious atonement and the eternity of future punishment His theory was that Christ was a man divinely commissioned who had no existence before he was conceived by the Virgin Mary that human sin was the imitation of Adams sin and that human salvation was the imitation and adoption of Christs virtue that the Bible was to be interpreted by human reason and that its language was metaphorical and not to be taken literally'),
(88321, 'Socinianize', 'To cause to conform to Socinianism to regulate by or imbue with the principles of Socinianism'),
(88322, 'Sociologic', 'Of or pertaining to sociology or social science'),
(88323, 'Sociologist', 'One who treats of or devotes himself to the study of sociology'),
(88324, 'Sociology', 'That branch of philosophy which treats of the constitution phenomena and development of human society social science'),
(88325, 'Sock', 'A plowshare'),
(88326, 'Sock away', 'to save money to put something into a reserve store'),
(88327, 'Sock in', 'to cause an airport to cease functioning by severe weather conditions such as fog or a snowstorm  Used often in the passive the airport was socked in for two days by a blizzard'),
(88328, 'Sockdolager', 'That which finishes or ends a matter a settler a poser as a heavy blow a conclusive answer and the like'),
(88329, 'Socket', 'An opening into which anything is fitted any hollow thing or place which receives and holds something else as the sockets of the teeth'),
(88330, 'Socketed', 'Having a socket'),
(88331, 'Sockless', 'Destitute of socks or shoes'),
(88332, 'Socky', 'Wet soaky'),
(88333, 'Socle', 'A plain block or plinth forming a low pedestal any base especially the base of a statue column or the like See Plinth'),
(88334, 'Socman', 'One who holds lands or tenements by socage a socager'),
(88335, 'Socmanry', 'Tenure by socage'),
(88336, 'Socome', 'A custom of tenants to grind corn at the lords mill'),
(88337, 'Socotrine', 'Of or pertaining to Socotra an island in the Indian Ocean on the east coast of Africa'),
(88338, 'Socratic', 'Of or pertaining to Socrates the Grecian sage and teacher b c 469 399 or to his manner of teaching and philosophizing'),
(88339, 'Socratically', 'In the Socratic method'),
(88340, 'Socratism', 'The philosophy or the method of Socrates'),
(88341, 'Socratist', 'A disciple or follower of Socrates'),
(88342, 'Sod', 'The rock dove'),
(88343, 'Soda', 'Sodium oxide or hydroxide'),
(88344, 'Soda biscuit', 'a bicuit leavened with baking soda and with buttermilk or sour milk'),
(88345, 'Soda bread', 'a bread made with buttermilk and leavened with baking soda'),
(88346, 'Soda fountain', 'a counter at which people may sit and be served soda pop ice cream or light meals  Such counters may be located for example in restaurants ice cream shops drugstores departments stores'),
(88347, 'Sodaic', 'Pertaining to or containing soda'),
(88348, 'Soda jerk', 'a person who serves soda ice cream similar foods from behind a soda fountain'),
(88349, 'Sodalite', 'A mineral of a white to blue or gray color occuring commonly in dodecahedrons also massive  It is a silicate of alumina and soda with some chlorine'),
(88350, 'Sodality', 'A fellowship or fraternity a brotherhood'),
(88351, 'Sodamide', 'A greenish or reddish crystalline substance NaNH2 obtained by passing ammonia over heated sodium'),
(88352, 'Soda water', 'originally a beverage consisting of a weak solution of sodium bicarbonate to which an acid such as phosphoric acid has been added to generate carbon dioxide and thus cause effervescence the term now is also used as a synonym for soda pop  Soda water without flavoring is used as a beverage or as a component of a mixed beverage  It is typically bottled or canned under pressure so that the carbon dioxide and effervescence will be preserved until use'),
(88353, 'Soda pop', 'a popular non alcoholic beverage sweetened by various means containing flavoring and supersaturated with carbon dioxide so as to be effervescent when the container is opened    in different localities it is variously called also soda pop mineral water and minerals It has many variants The sweetening agent may be natural such as cane sugar or corn syrup or artificial such as saccharin or aspartame  The flavoring varies widely popular variants being fruit juices fruit sirups cream or cola flavoring the soda pop is usually served chilled'),
(88354, 'Sodden', 'Boiled seethed also soaked heavy with moisture saturated as sodden beef sodden bread sodden fields'),
(88355, 'Sodden witted', 'Heavy dull'),
(88356, 'Soddy', 'Consisting of sod covered with sod turfy'),
(88357, 'Soder', 'See Solder'),
(88358, 'Sodger', 'Variant of Soldier'),
(88359, 'Sodic', 'Of or pertaining to sodium containing sodium'),
(88360, 'Sodio ', 'A combining form also used adjectively denoting the presence of sodium or one of its compounds'),
(88361, 'Sodium', 'A common metallic element of the alkali group in nature always occuring combined as in common salt in albite etc It is isolated as a soft waxy white unstable metal so highly reactive that it combines violently with water and to be preserved must be kept under petroleum or some similar liquid Sodium is used combined in many salts in the free state as a reducer and as a means of obtaining other metals as magnesium and aluminium is an important commercial product Symbol Na Natrium Atomic weight 22990  Specific gravity 097'),
(88362, 'Sodium bicarbonate', 'a white crystalline substance HNaCO3 with a slight alkaline taste resembling that of sodium carbonate It is found in many mineral springs and also produced artificially It is used in cookery in baking powders and as a source of carbonic acid gas carbon dioxide for soda water Called also baking soda cooking soda bicarbonate of soda bicarb saleratus and technically acid sodium carbonate sodium acid carbonate primary sodium carbonate sodium dicarbonate etc'),
(88363, 'Sodium sulphate', 'A salt well known as a catharic under the name of Glaubers salt which term is properly applied to the hydrate Na2SO410H2O'),
(88364, 'Sodomite', 'An inhabitant of Sodom'),
(88365, 'Sodomitical', 'Pertaining to or of the nature of sodomy'),
(88366, 'Sodomy', 'Carnal copulation in a manner against nature buggery'),
(88367, 'Soe', 'A large wooden vessel for holding water a cowl'),
(88368, 'Soever', 'A word compounded of so and ever used in composition with who what where when how etc and indicating any out of all possible or supposable persons things places times ways etc It is sometimes used separate from the pronoun or adverb'),
(88369, 'Sofa', 'A long seat usually with a cushioned bottom back and ends    much used as a comfortable piece of furniture'),
(88370, 'Soffit', 'The under side of the subordinate parts and members of buildings such as staircases entablatures archways cornices or the like See Illust of Lintel'),
(88371, 'Sofi', 'Same as Sufi'),
(88372, 'Sofism', 'Same as Sufism'),
(88373, 'Soft', 'Easily yielding to pressure easily impressed molded or cut not firm in resisting impressible yielding also malleable    opposed to hard as a soft bed a soft peach soft earth soft wood or metal'),
(88374, 'Softa', 'Any one attached to a Mohammedan mosque esp a student of the higher branches of theology in a mosque school'),
(88375, 'Soften', 'To make soft or more soft'),
(88376, 'Softener', 'One who or that which softens'),
(88377, 'Softening', 'a  n from Soften v'),
(88378, 'Soft finned', 'Having the fin rays cartilaginous or flexible without spines    said of certain fishes'),
(88379, 'Soft headed', 'Weak in intellect'),
(88380, 'Soft hearted', 'Having softness or tenderness of heart susceptible of pity or other kindly affection gentle meek'),
(88381, 'Softish', 'Somewhat soft'),
(88382, 'Softling', 'A soft effeminate person a voluptuary'),
(88383, 'Softly', 'In a soft manner'),
(88384, 'Softner', 'See Softener'),
(88385, 'Softness', 'The quality or state of being soft    opposed to hardness and used in the various specific senses of the adjective'),
(88386, 'Soft shell', 'Having a soft or fragile shell'),
(88387, 'Soft spoken', 'Speaking softly having a mild or gentle voice hence mild affable'),
