(92255, 'Succus', 'The expressed juice of a plant for medicinal use'),
(92256, 'Succussation', 'A trot or trotting'),
(92257, 'Succussion', 'The act of shaking a shake esp Med a shaking of the body to ascertain if there be a liquid in the thorax'),
(92258, 'Succussive', 'Characterized by a shaking motion especially an up and down movement and not merely tremulous oscillation as the succussive motion in earthquakes'),
(92259, 'Such', 'Of that kind of the like kind like resembling similar as we never saw such a day    followed by that or as introducing the word or proposition which defines the similarity or the standard of comparison as the books are not such that I can recommend them or not such as I can recommend these apples are not such as those we saw yesterday give your children such precepts as tend to make them better'),
(92260, 'Suchospondylous', 'Having dorsal vertebrae with long and divided transverse processes    applied to certain reptiles'),
(92261, 'Suchwise', 'In a such a manner so'),
(92262, 'Suck', 'To draw as a liquid by the action of the mouth and tongue which tends to produce a vacuum and causes the liquid to rush in by atmospheric pressure to draw or apply force to by exhausting the air'),
(92263, 'Suckanhock', 'A kind of seawan See Note under Seawan'),
(92264, 'Suckatash', 'See Succotash'),
(92265, 'Sucken', 'The jurisdiction of a mill or that extent of ground astricted to it the tenants of which are bound to bring their grain thither to be ground'),
(92266, 'Sucker', 'One who or that which sucks esp one of the organs by which certain animals as the octopus and remora adhere to other bodies'),
(92267, 'Sucker State', 'Illinois    a nickname'),
(92268, 'Sucket', 'A sweetmeat a dainty morsel'),
(92269, 'suck face', 'To engage in french kissing soul kissing'),
(92270, 'Suckfish', 'A sucker fish'),
(92271, 'Sucking', 'Drawing milk from the mother or dam hence colloquially young inexperienced as a sucking infant a sucking calf'),
(92272, 'Suckle', 'A teat'),
(92273, 'Suckler', 'An animal that suckles its young a mammal'),
(92274, 'Suckling', 'A young child or animal nursed at the breast'),
(92275, 'Sucrate', 'A compound of sucrose or of some related carbohydrate with some base after the analogy of a salt as sodium sucrate'),
(92276, 'Sucre', 'A silver coin of Ecuador worth 68 cents ca 1900'),
(92277, 'Sucrose', 'A common variety of sugar found in the juices of many plants as the sugar cane sorghum sugar maple beet root etc It is extracted as a sweet white crystalline substance which is valuable as a food product and being antiputrescent is largely used in the preservation of fruit Called also saccharose cane sugar etc  At one time the term was used by extension for any one of the class of isomeric substances as lactose maltose etc of which sucrose proper is the type however this usage is now archaic'),
(92278, 'Suction', 'The act or process of sucking the act of drawing as fluids by exhausting the air'),
(92279, 'Suctoria', 'An order of Infusoria having the body armed with somewhat stiff tubular processes which they use as suckers in obtaining their food They are usually stalked'),
(92280, 'Suctorial', 'Adapted for sucking living by sucking as the humming birds are suctorial birds'),
(92281, 'Suctorian', 'A cartilaginous fish with a mouth adapted for suction as the lampery'),
(92282, 'Suctorious', 'Suctorial'),
(92283, 'Sudamina', 'Minute vesicles surrounded by an area of reddened skin produced by excessive sweating'),
(92284, 'Sudarium', 'The handkerchief upon which the Savior is said to have impressed his own portrait miraculously when wiping his face with it as he passed to the crucifixion'),
(92285, 'Sudary', 'A napkin or handkerchief'),
(92286, 'Sudation', 'A sweating'),
