(92426, 'Sulphatic', 'Of pertaining to resembling or containing a sulphate or sulphates'),
(92427, 'Sulphato ', 'A combining form also used adjectively denoting a sulphate as an ingredient in certain double salts as sulphato carbonate'),
(92428, 'Sulphaurate', 'A salt of sulphauric acid'),
(92429, 'Sulphauric', 'Of pertaining to or designating a hypothetical sulphacid of gold aurum known only in its salts'),
(92430, 'Sulphide', 'A binary compound of sulphur or one so regarded    formerly called sulphuret'),
(92431, 'Sulphinate', 'A salt of a sulphinic acid'),
(92432, 'Sulphindigotic', 'Of pertaining to or designating a sulphonic acid obtained as a blue solution by dissolving indigo in sulphuric acid    formerly called also cerulic sulphuric acid but properly called indigo disulphonic acid'),
(92433, 'Sulphine', 'Any one of a series of basic compounds which consist essentially of sulphur united with hydrocarbon radicals In general they are oily or crystalline deliquescent substances having a peculiar odor as trimethyl sulphine CH33SOH  Cf Sulphonium'),
(92434, 'Sulphinic', 'Of pertaining to or designating any one of a series of acids regarded as acid ethereal salts of hyposulphurous acid as methyl sulphinic acid CH3SOOH a thick unstable liquid'),
(92435, 'Sulphinide', 'A white or yellowish crystalline substance C6H4SO2CONH produced artificially by the oxidation of a sulphamic derivative of toluene It is the sweetest substance known having over two hundred times the sweetening power of sugar and is known in commerce under the name of saccharine It has acid properties and forms salts which are inaccurately called saccharinates'),
(92436, 'Sulphion', 'A hypothetical radical SO4 regarded as forming the acid or negative constituent of sulphuric acid and the sulphates in electrolytic decomposition    so called in accordance with the binary theory of salts'),
(92437, 'Sulphionide', 'A binary compound of sulphion or one so regarded thus sulphuric acid H2SO4 is a sulphionide'),
(92438, 'Sulphite', 'A salt of sulphurous acid    called also sulfite'),
(92439, 'Sulpho ', 'A prefix also used adjectively designating sulphur as an ingredient in certain compounds  Cf Thio '),
(92440, 'Sulphoarsenic', 'Of pertaining to or containing sulphur and arsenic    said of an acid which is the same as arsenic acid with the substitution of sulphur for oxygen'),
(92441, 'Sulphocarbonate', 'A salt of sulphocarbonic acid a thiocarbonate'),
(92442, 'Sulphocarbonic', 'Of pertaining to or designating a sulphacid H2CSO2 called also thiocarbonic acid or an acid H2CS3 analogous to carbonic acid obtained as a yellow oily liquid of a pungent odor and forming salts'),
(92443, 'Sulphocyanate', 'A salt of sulphocyanic acid    also called thiocyanate and formerly inaccurately sulphocyanide'),
(92444, 'Sulphocyanic', 'Of pertaining to derived from or designating a sulphacid HSCN analogous to cyanic acid and obtained as a colorless deliquescent crystalline substance having a bitter saline taste and not poisonous'),
(92445, 'Sulphocyanide', 'See Sulphocyanate'),
(92446, 'Sulphocyanogen', 'See Persulphocyanogen'),
(92447, 'Sulphonal', 'A substance employed as a hypnotic produced by the union of mercaptan and acetone'),
(92448, 'Sulphonate', 'A salt of sulphonic acid'),
(92449, 'Sulphone', 'Any one of a series of compounds analogous to the ketones and consisting of the sulphuryl group united with two hydrocarbon radicals as dimethyl sulphone CH'),
(92450, 'Sulphonic', 'Pertaining to or derived from a sulphone    used specifically to designate any one of a series of acids regarded as acid ethereal salts of sulphurous acid obtained by the oxidation of the mercaptans or by treating sulphuric acid with certain aromatic bases as benzene as phenyl sulphonic acid C6H5SO2OH a stable colorless crystalline substance'),
(92451, 'Sulphonium', 'A hypothetical radical SH3 regarded as the type and nucleus of the sulphines'),
(92452, 'Sulphophosphate', 'A salt of sulphophosphoric acid'),
(92453, 'Sulphophosphite', 'A salt of sulphophosphorous acid'),
(92454, 'Sulphophosphoric', 'Of pertaining to or designating a hypothetical sulphacid of phosphorus analogous to phosphoric acid and known in its salts'),
(92455, 'Sulphophosphorous', 'Of pertaining to or designating a hypothetical acid of phosphorus analogous to phosphorous acid and known in its salts'),
(92456, 'Sulphosalt', 'A salt of a sulphacid'),
(92457, 'Sulphostannate', 'A salt of sulphostannic acid'),
(92458, 'Sulphostannic', 'Of pertaining to or designating a sulphacid of tin more exactly called metasulphostannic acid which is obtained as a dark brown amorphous substance H'),
(92459, 'Sulphotungstate', 'A salt of sulphotungstic acid'),
(92460, 'Sulphotungstic', 'Of pertaining to or designating hypothetical sulphacid of tungsten called also sulphowolframic acid analogous to sulphuric acid and known in its salts'),
(92461, 'Sulphovinic', 'Of pertaining to and formerly designating ethylsulphuric acid'),
(92462, 'Sulphur', 'A nonmetallic element occurring naturally in large quantities either combined as in the sulphides as pyrites and sulphates as gypsum or native in volcanic regions in vast beds mixed with gypsum and various earthy materials from which it is melted out Symbol S Atomic weight 32 The specific gravity of ordinary octohedral sulphur is 205 of prismatic sulphur 196'),
(92463, 'Sulphurate', 'Sulphureous'),
(92464, 'Sulphuration', 'The act or process of combining or impregnating with sulphur or its compounds also the state of being so combined or impregnated'),
(92465, 'Sulphurator', 'An apparatus for impregnating with or exposing to the action of sulphur especially an apparatus for fumigating or bleaching by means of the fumes of burning sulphur'),
(92466, 'Sulphur bottom', 'A very large whalebone whale of the genus Sibbaldius having a yellowish belly especially Sibbaldius sulfureus of the North Pacific and Sibbaldius borealis of the North Atlantic    called also sulphur whale'),
(92467, 'Sulphureity', 'The quality or state of being sulphureous'),
(92468, 'Sulphureous', 'Consisting of sulphur having the qualities of sulphur or brimstone impregnated with sulphur'),
(92469, 'Sulphuret', 'A sulphide as a sulphuret of potassium'),
(92470, 'Sulphureted', 'Combined or impregnated with sulphur sulphurized'),
(92471, 'Sulphuric', 'Of or pertaining to sulphur as a sulphuric smell'),
(92472, 'Sulphurine', 'Sulphureous'),
(92473, 'Sulphuring', 'Exposure to the fumes of burning sulphur as in bleaching the process of bleaching by exposure to the fumes of sulphur'),
(92474, 'Sulphurize', 'To combine or impregnate with sulphur or any of its compounds as to sulphurize caoutchouc in vulcanizing');
INSERT INTO `dictionary` (`id`, `word`, `definition`) VALUES
(92475, 'Sulphurous', 'Of or pertaining to sulphur'),
(92476, 'Sulphurwort', 'The hogs fennel See under Fennel'),
(92477, 'Sulphury', 'Resembling or partaking of the nature of sulphur having the qualities of sulphur'),
(92478, 'Sulphuryl', 'The hypothetical radical SO2    called also sulphon'),
(92479, 'Sulphydrate', 'A compound analogous to a hydrate regarded as a salt of sulphydric acid or as a derivative of hydrogen sulphide in which one half of the hydrogen is replaced by a base as potassium sulphydrate KSH or as a hydrate in which the oxygen has been wholly or partially replaced by sulphur'),
(92480, 'Sulphydric', 'Of pertaining to or designating hydrogen sulphide which is regarded as an acid especially when in solution'),
(92481, 'Sulpician', 'One of an order of priests established in France in 1642 to educate men for the ministry The order was introduced soon afterwards into Canada and in 1791 into the United States'),
(92482, 'Sultan', 'A ruler or sovereign of a Mohammedan state specifically the ruler of the Turks the Padishah or Grand Seignior    officially so called'),
(92483, 'Sultana', 'The wife of a sultan a sultaness'),
(92484, 'Sultanate', 'The rule or dominion of a sultan sultanship'),
(92485, 'Sultaness', 'A sultana'),
(92486, 'Sultanic', 'Pertaining to a sultan'),
(92487, 'Sultan red', 'Having a deep red color'),
(92488, 'Sultanry', 'The dominions of a sultan'),
(92489, 'Sultanship', 'The office or dignity of a sultan'),
(92490, 'Sultany', 'Sultanry'),
(92491, 'Sultrily', 'In a sultry manner'),
(92492, 'Sultriness', 'The quality or state of being sultry'),
(92493, 'Sultry', 'Very hot burning and oppressive as Libyas sultry deserts'),
(92494, 'Sulu', 'A member of the most prominent tribe of the Moro tribes occupying the Sulu Archipelago also their language'),
(92495, 'Sum', 'The aggregate of two or more numbers magnitudes quantities or particulars the amount or whole of any number of individuals or particulars added together as the sum of 5 and 7 is 12'),
(92496, 'Sumac', 'Any plant of the genus Rhus shrubs or small trees with usually compound leaves and clusters of small flowers Some of the species are used in tanning some in dyeing and some in medicine One the Japanese Rhus vernicifera yields the celebrated Japan varnish or lacquer'),
(92497, 'Sumatra leaf', 'A thin elastic uniformly light colored tobacco leaf raised in Sumatra and extensively used for cigar wrappers'),
(92498, 'Sumatran', 'Of or pertaining to Sumatra or its inhabitants'),
(92499, 'Sumbul', 'The musky root of an Asiatic umbelliferous plant Ferula Sumbul It is used in medicine as a stimulant'),
(92500, 'Sumerian', 'Of or pertaining to the region of lower Babylonia which was anciently called Sumer or its inhabitants or their language'),
