(93442, 'Sycamine', 'See Sycamore'),
(93443, 'Sycamore', 'A large tree Ficus Sycomorus allied to the common fig  It is found in Egypt and Syria and is the sycamore or sycamine of Scripture'),
(93444, 'Syce', 'A groom'),
(93445, 'Sycee', 'Silver pounded into ingots of the shape of a shoe and used as currency The most common weight is about one pound troy'),
(93446, 'Sychnocarpous', 'Having the capacity of bearing several successive crops of fruit without perishing as sychnocarpous plants'),
(93447, 'Sycite', 'A nodule of flint or a pebble which resembles a fig'),
(93448, 'Sycoceric', 'Of pertaining to or designating an acid obtained by the oxidation of sycoceryl alcohol'),
