(93962, 'Taja', 'The common or collared peccary Tayassu tajacu  Called also javelina and tayaussa'),
(93963, 'Taj Mahal', 'A marble mausoleum built at Agra India by the Mogul Emperor Shah Jahan in memory of his favorite wife  In beauty of design and rich decorative detail it is one of the best examples of Saracenic architecture and specifically of Mogul architecture'),
(93964, 'Take', 'Taken'),
(93965, 'Take in', 'Imposition fraud'),
(93966, 'Taken', 'p p of Take'),
(93967, 'Takeoff', 'An imitation especially in the way of caricature    used with of or on as the comedian did a hilarious takeoff on the president'),
(93968, 'Take off', 'to begin a leap from a surface or a flight into the air especially of a bird or an airplane to leave the ground and begin to fly as flight CA123 took off on schedule at 300 PM'),
(93969, 'takeover', 'The acquisition of ownership of one company by another company usually by purchasing a controlling percentage of its stock or by exchanging stock of the purchasing company for that of the purchased company  It is a hostile takeover if the management of the company being taken over is opposed to the deal  A hostile takeover is sometimes organized by a corporate raider'),
(93970, 'Taker', 'One who takes or receives one who catches or apprehends'),
(93971, 'take the heat', 'To be assigned or to accept the blame for some misdeed as Mary broke the vase but she acted innocent and young Johnny had to take the heat'),
(93972, 'Take up', 'That which takes up or tightens specifically a device in a sewing machine for drawing up the slack thread as the needle rises in completing a stitch'),
(93973, 'Taking', 'Apt to take alluring attracting'),
(93974, 'Taking off', 'Removal murder See To take off c under Take v t'),
(93975, 'Talapoin', 'A small African monkey Cercopithecus talapoin or Miopithecus talapoin    called also melarhine'),
(93976, 'Talaria', 'Small wings or winged shoes represented as fastened to the ankles    chiefly used as an attribute of Mercury'),
(93977, 'Talbot', 'A sort of dog noted for quick scent and eager pursuit of game'),
(93978, 'Talbotype', 'Same as Calotype'),
(93979, 'Talc', 'A soft mineral of a soapy feel and a greenish whitish or grayish color usually occurring in foliated masses It is hydrous silicate of magnesia Steatite or soapstone is a compact granular variety'),
(93980, 'Talcose', 'Of or pertaining to talc composed of or resembling talc'),
(93981, 'Talcum', 'Same as Talc'),
(93982, 'Tale', 'See Tael'),
(93983, 'Talebearer', 'One who officiously tells tales one who impertinently or maliciously communicates intelligence scandal etc and makes mischief'),
(93984, 'Talebearing', 'Telling tales officiously'),
(93985, 'Taled', 'A kind of quadrangular piece of cloth put on by the Jews when repeating prayers in the synagogues'),
(93986, 'Taleful', 'Full of stories'),
(93987, 'Talegalla', 'A genus of Australian birds which includes the brush turkey See Brush turkey'),
(93988, 'Talent', 'Among the ancient Greeks a weight and a denomination of money equal to 60 minae or 6000 drachmae  The Attic talent as a weight was about 57 lbs avoirdupois as a denomination of silver money its value was '),
(93989, 'Talented', 'Furnished with talents possessing skill or talent mentally gifted'),
(93990, 'Tales', 'Persons added to a jury commonly from those in or about the courthouse to make up any deficiency in the number of jurors regularly summoned being like or such as the latter'),
(93991, 'Talesman', 'A person called to make up a deficiency in the number of jurors when a tales is awarded'),
(93992, 'Taleteller', 'One who tells tales or stories especially in a mischievous or officious manner a talebearer a telltale a tattler'),
(93993, 'Talewise', 'In a way of a tale or story'),
(93994, 'Taliacotian', 'See Tagliacotian'),
(93995, 'Taliation', 'Retaliation'),
(93996, 'Talion', 'Retaliation'),
(93997, 'Talipes', 'The deformity called clubfoot See Clubfoot'),
(93998, 'Talipot', 'A beautiful tropical palm tree Corypha umbraculifera a native of Ceylon and the Malabar coast It has a trunk sixty or seventy feet high bearing a crown of gigantic fan shaped leaves which are used as umbrellas and as fans in ceremonial processions and when cut into strips as a substitute for writing paper'),
(93999, 'Talisman', 'A magical figure cut or engraved under certain superstitious observances of the configuration of the heavens to which wonderful effects are ascribed the seal figure character or image of a heavenly sign constellation or planet engraved on a sympathetic stone or on a metal corresponding to the star in order to receive its influence'),
(94000, 'Talismanic', 'Of or pertaining to a talisman having the properties of a talisman or preservative against evils by occult influence magical'),
(94001, 'talk', 'To utter words esp to converse familiarly to speak as in familiar discourse when two or more persons interchange thoughts'),
(94002, 'Talkative', 'Given to much talking'),
(94003, 'Talker', 'One who talks especially one who is noted for his power of conversing readily or agreeably a conversationist'),
(94004, 'Talking', 'That talks able to utter words as a talking parrot'),
(94005, 'Tall', 'High in stature having a considerable or an unusual extension upward long and comparatively slender having the diameter or lateral extent small in proportion to the height as a tall person tree or mast'),
(94006, 'Tallage', 'A certain rate or tax paid by barons knights and inferior tenants toward the public expenses'),
(94007, 'Tallboy', 'A kind of long stemmed wineglass or cup'),
(94008, 'Tallier', 'One who keeps tally'),
(94009, 'Tallis', 'Same as Tallith'),
(94010, 'Tallith', 'An undergarment worn by orthodox Jews covering the chest and the upper part of the back It has an opening for the head and has tassels called zizith on its four corners'),
(94011, 'Tallness', 'The quality or state of being tall height of stature'),
(94012, 'Tallow', 'The suet or fat of animals of the sheep and ox kinds separated from membranous and fibrous matter by melting'),
(94013, 'Tallower', 'An animal which produces tallow'),
(94014, 'Tallow face', 'One who has a sickly pale complexion'),
(94015, 'Tallow faced', 'Having a sickly complexion pale'),
(94016, 'Tallowing', 'The act or art of causing animals to produce tallow also the property in animals of producing tallow'),
(94017, 'Tallowish', 'Having the qualities of tallow'),
(94018, 'Tallowy', 'Of the nature of tallow resembling tallow greasy'),
(94019, 'Tallwood', 'Firewood cut into billets of a certain length'),
(94020, 'Tally', 'Originally a piece of wood on which notches or scores were cut as the marks of number later one of two books sheets of paper etc on which corresponding accounts were kept'),
(94021, 'Tallyho', 'The huntsmans cry to incite or urge on his hounds'),
(94022, 'Tallyman', 'One who keeps the tally or marks the sticks'),
(94023, 'Talma', 'A kind of large cape or short full cloak forming part of the dress of ladies'),
(94024, 'Talmud', 'The body of the Jewish civil and canonical law not comprised in the Pentateuch'),
(94025, 'Talmudic', 'Of or pertaining to the Talmud contained in the Talmud as Talmudic Greek Talmudical phrases'),
(94026, 'Talmudism', 'The teachings of the Talmud or adherence to them'),
(94027, 'Talmudist', 'One versed in the Talmud one who adheres to the teachings of the Talmud'),
(94028, 'Talmudistic', 'Resembling the Talmud Talmudic'),
(94029, 'Talon', 'The claw of a predaceous bird or animal especially the claw of a bird of prey'),
(94030, 'Talook', 'A large estate esp one constituting a revenue district or dependency the native proprietor of which is responsible for the collection and payment of the public revenue due from it'),
(94031, 'Talookdar', 'A proprietor of a talook'),
(94032, 'Talpa', 'A genus of small insectivores including the common European mole'),
(94033, 'Talus', 'The astragalus'),
(94034, 'Tamability', 'The quality or state of being tamable tamableness'),
(94035, 'Tamable', 'Capable of being tamed subdued or reclaimed from wildness or savage ferociousness'),
(94036, 'Tamale', 'A Mexican dish made of crushed corn cornmeal mixed with minced meat seasoned with red pepper dipped in oil and steamed'),
(94037, 'Tamandu', 'A small ant eater Tamandua tetradactyla native of the tropical parts of South America'),
(94038, 'Tamanoir', 'The ant bear'),
(94039, 'Tamarack', 'The American larch also the larch of Oregon and British Columbia Larix occidentalis See Hackmatack and Larch'),
(94040, 'Tamaric', 'A shrub or tree supposed to be the tamarisk or perhaps some kind of heath'),
(94041, 'Tamarin', 'Any one of several species of small squirrel like South American monkeys of the genus Midas especially Midas ursulus'),
(94042, 'Tamarind', 'A leguminous tree Tamarindus Indica cultivated both the Indies and the other tropical countries for the sake of its shade and for its fruit The trunk of the tree is lofty and large with wide spreading branches the flowers are in racemes at the ends of the branches The leaves are small and finely pinnated'),
(94043, 'Tamarisk', 'Any shrub or tree of the genus Tamarix the species of which are European and Asiatic  They have minute scalelike leaves and small flowers in spikes  An Arabian species Tamarix mannifera is the source of one kind of manna'),
(94044, 'Tambac', 'See Tombac'),
(94045, 'Tambour', 'A kind of small flat drum a tambourine'),
(94046, 'Tamboura', 'A stringed musical instrument resembling a lute but lacking frets with a small round body and a long neck used to produce an accompaniment for singing    called also tambur tambour and tampur'),
(94047, 'Tambourin', 'A tambourine'),
(94048, 'Tambourine', 'A small drum especially a shallow drum with only one skin played on with the hand and having bells at the sides a timbrel'),
(94049, 'Tambreet', 'The duck mole'),
(94050, 'Tamburin', 'See Tambourine'),
(94051, 'Tame', 'To broach or enter upon to taste as a liquor to divide to distribute to deal out'),
(94052, 'Tameable', 'Tamable'),
(94053, 'Tameless', 'Incapable of being tamed wild untamed untamable'),
(94054, 'Tamely', 'In a tame manner'),
(94055, 'Tameness', 'The quality or state of being tame'),
(94056, 'Tamer', 'One who tames or subdues'),
(94057, 'Tamerlane', 'A Tatar conquerer also called Timur or Timour t'),
(94058, 'Tamias', 'A genus of ground squirrels including the chipmunk'),
(94059, 'Tamil', 'Of or pertaining to the Tamils or to their language'),
(94060, 'Tamilian', 'Tamil'),
(94061, 'Tamine', 'A kind of woolen cloth tammy'),
(94062, 'Tamis', 'A sieve or strainer made of a kind of woolen cloth'),
(94063, 'Tamkin', 'A tampion'),
(94064, 'Tammy', 'A kind of woolen or woolen and cotton cloth often highly glazed    used for curtains sieves strainers etc'),
(94065, 'Tam o shanter', 'A kind of Scotch cap of wool worsted or the like having a round flattish top much wider than the band which fits the head and usually having a tassel in the center'),
(94066, 'Tamoxifen', 'a chemical compound C26H29NO which is non steroidal but physiogically active as an estrogen antagonist  It is used to treat postmenopausal breast cancer  Chemically it is 1 p dimethylaminoethoxyphenyl trans 12 diphenyl but 1 ene It can be obtained as a white crystalline powder'),
(94067, 'Tamp', 'In blasting to plug up with clay earth dry sand sod or other material as a hole bored in a rock in order to prevent the force of the explosion from being misdirected'),
(94068, 'Tampan', 'A venomous South African tick'),
(94069, 'Tampeon', 'See Tampion'),
(94070, 'Tamper', 'One who tamps specifically one who prepares for blasting by filling the hole in which the charge is placed'),
(94071, 'Tamperer', 'One who tampers one who deals unfairly'),
(94072, 'Tampico fiber', 'A tough vegetable fiber used as a substitute for bristles in making brushes The piassava and the ixtle are both used under this name'),
(94073, 'Tamping', 'The act of one who tamps specifically the act of filling up a hole in a rock or the branch of a mine for the purpose of blasting the rock or exploding the mine'),
(94074, 'Tampion', 'A wooden stopper or plug as for a cannon or other piece of ordnance when not in use'),
(94075, 'Tampoe', 'The edible fruit of an East Indian tree Baccaurea Malayana of the Spurge family It somewhat resembles an apple'),
(94076, 'Tampon', 'A plug introduced into a natural or artificial cavity of the body in order to arrest hemorrhage absorb secretions as from menstruation or for the application of medicine'),
(94077, 'Tampoon', 'The stopper of a barrel a bung'),
(94078, 'Tam tam', 'A kind of drum used in the East Indies and other Oriental countries    called also tom tom'),
(94079, 'Tamul', 'Tamil'),
(94080, 'Tamworth', 'One of a long established English breed of large pigs They are red often spotted with black with a long snout and erect or forwardly pointed ears and are valued as bacon producers'),
(94081, 'Tan', 'See Picul'),
(94082, 'Tana', 'Same as Banxring'),
(94083, 'Tanager', 'Any one of numerous species of bright colored singing birds belonging to Tanagra Piranga and allied genera  The scarlet tanager Piranga erythromelas and the summer redbird Piranga rubra are common species of the United States'),
(94084, 'Tanagrine', 'Of or pertaining to the tanagers'),
(94085, 'Tanagroid', 'Tanagrine'),
(94086, 'Tanak', 'A term used among Jews for the Hebrew Bible the Old Testament'),
(94087, 'Tanate', 'An Asiatic wild dog Canis procyonoides native of Japan and adjacent countries It has a short bushy tail Called also raccoon dog'),
(94088, 'Tandem', 'One after another    said especially of horses harnessed and driven one before another instead of abreast'),
(94089, 'Tandem cart', 'A kind of two wheeled vehicle with seats back to back the front one somewhat elevated'),
(94090, 'Tandem engine', 'A steam engine having two or more steam cylinders in line with a common piston rod'),
(94091, 'Tandem system', 'same as Cascade system'),
(94092, 'Tang', 'A coarse blackish seaweed Fucus nodosus'),
(94093, 'Tangalung', 'An East Indian civet Viverra tangalunga'),
(94094, 'Tangelo', 'A hybrid between the tangerine orange and the grapefruit or pomelo also the fruit'),
(94095, 'Tangence', 'Tangency'),
(94096, 'Tangency', 'The quality or state of being tangent a contact or touching'),
(94097, 'Tangent', 'A tangent line curve or surface specifically that portion of the straight line tangent to a curve that is between the point of tangency and a given line the given line being for example the axis of abscissas or a radius of a circle produced See Trigonometrical function under Function'),
(94098, 'Tangental', 'Tangential'),
(94099, 'Tangential', 'Of or pertaining to a tangent in the direction of a tangent'),
(94100, 'Tangentially', 'In the direction of a tangent'),
(94101, 'Tangent spoke', 'A tension spoke of a bicycle or similar wheel secured tangentially to the hub'),
(94102, 'Tangent wheel', 'A worm or worm wheel a tangent screw');
INSERT INTO `dictionary` (`id`, `word`, `definition`) VALUES
(94103, 'Tangerine', 'A kind of orange much like the mandarin but of deeper color and higher flavor It is said to have been produced in America from the mandarin'),
(94104, 'Tangfish', 'The common harbor seal'),
(94105, 'Tanghinia', 'The ordeal tree See under Ordeal'),
(94106, 'Tangibility', 'The quality or state of being tangible'),
(94107, 'Tangible', 'Perceptible to the touch tactile palpable'),
(94108, 'Tangle', 'To unite or knit together confusedly to interweave or interlock as threads so as to make it difficult to unravel the knot to entangle to ravel'),
(94109, 'Tanglefish', 'The sea adder or great pipefish of Europe'),
(94110, 'Tanglingly', 'In a tangling manner'),
(94111, 'Tangly', 'Entangled intricate'),
(94112, 'Tango', 'A difficult dance in two four time characterized by graceful posturing frequent pointing positions and a great variety of steps including the cross step and turning steps The dance is of Spanish origin and is believed to have been in its original form a part of the fandango'),
(94113, 'Tangram', 'A Chinese toy made by cutting a square of thin wood or other suitable material into seven pieces as shown in the cut these pieces being capable of combination in various ways so as to form a great number of different figures It is now often used in primary schools as a means of instruction'),
(94114, 'Tangue', 'The tenrec'),
(94115, 'Tangun', 'A piebald variety of the horse native of Thibet'),
(94116, 'Tangwhaup', 'The whimbrel'),
(94117, 'Tanier', 'An aroid plant Caladium sagittaefolium the leaves of which are boiled and eaten in the West Indies'),
(94118, 'Tanist', 'In Ireland a lord or proprietor of a tract of land or of a castle elected by a family under the system of tanistry'),
(94119, 'Tanistry', 'In Ireland a tenure of family lands by which the proprietor had only a life estate to which he was admitted by election'),
(94120, 'Tanite', 'A firm composition of emery and a certain kind of cement used for making grinding wheels slabs etc'),
(94121, 'Tank', 'A small Indian dry measure averaging 240 grains in weight also a Bombay weight of 72 grains for pearls'),
(94122, 'Tanka', 'A kind of boat used in Canton It is about 25 feet long and is often rowed by women Called also tankia'),
(94123, 'Tankage', 'The act or process of putting or storing in tanks'),
(94124, 'Tankard', 'A large drinking vessel especially one with a cover'),
(94125, 'Tankia', 'See Tanka'),
(94126, 'Tankling', 'A tinkling'),
(94127, 'Tank ship', 'A vessel fitted with tanks for the carrying of oil or other liquid in bulk    called also tanker  A tank ship of very large capacity is called a supertanker'),
(94128, 'Tank top', 'a sleeveless and collarless shirt with wide shoulder straps and no front opening often close fitting and low cut'),
(94129, 'Tank warfare', 'combat between tanks of opposing armies'),
(94130, 'Tanling', 'One tanned by the sun'),
(94131, 'Tannable', 'That may be tanned'),
(94132, 'Tannage', 'A tanning the act operation or result of tanning'),
(94133, 'Tannate', 'A salt of tannic acid'),
(94134, 'Tanner', 'One whose occupation is to tan hides or convert them into leather by the use of tan'),
(94135, 'Tannery', 'A place where the work of tanning is carried on'),
(94136, 'Tannic', 'Of or pertaining to tan derived from or resembling tan as tannic acid'),
(94137, 'Tannier', 'See Tanier'),
(94138, 'Tannigen', 'A compound obtained as a yellowish gray powder by the action of acetyl chloride or acetic anhydride or ordinary tannic acid It is used as an intestinal astringent and locally in rhinitis and pharyngitis'),
(94139, 'Tannin', 'Same as Tannic acid under Tannic'),
(94140, 'Tanning', 'The art or process of converting skins into leather See Tan v t 1'),
(94141, 'Tanrec', 'Same as Tenrec'),
(94142, 'Tansy', 'Any plant of the composite genus Tanacetum The common tansy Tanacetum vulgare has finely divided leaves a strong aromatic odor and a very bitter taste It is used for medicinal and culinary purposes'),
(94143, 'Tant', 'A small scarlet arachnid'),
(94144, 'Tantalate', 'A salt of tantalic acid'),
(94145, 'Tantalic', 'Of or pertaining to tantalum derived from or containing tantalum specifically designating any one of a series of acids analogous to nitric acid and the polyacid compounds of phosphorus'),
(94146, 'Tantalism', 'A punishment like that of Tantalus a teasing or tormenting by the hope or near approach of good which is not attainable tantalization'),
(94147, 'Tantalite', 'A heavy mineral of an iron black color and submetallic luster  It is essentially a tantalate of iron'),
(94148, 'Tantalization', 'The act of tantalizing or state of being tantalized'),
(94149, 'Tantalize', 'To tease or torment by presenting some good to the view and exciting desire but continually frustrating the expectations by keeping that good out of reach to tease to torment'),
(94150, 'Tantalizer', 'One who tantalizes'),
(94151, 'Tantalizingly', 'In a tantalizing or teasing manner'),
(94152, 'Tantalum', 'A rare nonmetallic element found in certain minerals as tantalite samarskite and fergusonite and isolated as a dark powder which becomes steel gray by burnishing Symbol Ta Atomic weight 1820 Formerly called also tantalium'),
(94153, 'Tantalus', 'A Phrygian king who was punished in the lower world by being placed in the midst of a lake whose waters reached to his chin but receded whenever he attempted to allay his thirst while over his head hung branches laden with choice fruit which likewise receded whenever he stretched out his hand to grasp them'),
(94154, 'Tantamount', 'Equivalent in value signification or effect'),
(94155, 'Tantivy', 'Swiftly speedily rapidly    a fox hunting term as to ride tantivy'),
(94156, 'Tantra', 'A ceremonial treatise related to Puranic and magic literature esp one of the sacred works of the worshipers of Sakti'),
(94157, 'Tantrism', 'The system of doctrines and  rites taught in the tantras'),
(94158, 'Tantrum', 'A whim an affected air'),
(94159, 'Tanyard', 'An inclosure where the tanning of leather is carried on a tannery'),
(94160, 'Tanystomata', 'A division of dipterous insects in which the proboscis is large and contains lancelike mandibles and maxillae The horseflies and robber flies are examples'),
(94161, 'Taoism', 'One of the popular religions of China sanctioned by the state'),
(94162, 'Taotai', 'In China an official at the head of the civil and military affairs of a circuit which consists of two or more fu or territorial departments    called also by foreigners intendant of circuit Foreign consuls and commissioners associated with taotais as superintendants of trade at the treaty ports are ranked with the taotai'),
(94163, 'Tap', 'To strike with a slight or gentle blow to touch gently to rap lightly to pat as to tap one with the hand or a cane'),
(94164, 'Tapa', 'A kind of cloth prepared by the Polynesians from the inner bark of the paper mulberry    sometimes called also kapa'),
(94165, 'Tapadera', 'One of the leather hoods which cover the stirrups of a Mexican saddle'),
(94166, 'Tapayaxin', 'A Mexican spinous lizard Phrynosoma orbiculare having a head somewhat like that of a toad    called also horned toad'),
(94167, 'Tape', 'A narrow fillet or band of cotton or linen a narrow woven fabric used for strings and the like as curtains tied with tape'),
(94168, 'Tape deck', 'an electroinic device for recording and playing back sounds on magnetic tape usually it needs to be connected to an amplifier system for playback'),
(94169, 'Tapeline', 'A painted tape marked with linear dimensions as inches feet etc and often inclosed in a case    used for measuring'),
(94170, 'Taper', 'A small wax candle a small lighted wax candle hence a small light'),
(94171, 'Tape recorder', 'an electroinic device for recording and playing back sounds on magnetic tape it often has an integrated microphone amplifier and speaker and in such cases requires no additional equipment other than the magnetic tape for recording or playback'),
(94172, 'Tape recording', 'a recording of sound or video on magnetic tape'),
(94173, 'Tapered', 'Lighted with a taper or tapers as a tapered choir'),
(94174, 'Tapering', 'Becoming gradually smaller toward one end'),
(94175, 'Taperness', 'The quality or state of being taper tapering form taper'),
(94176, 'Tapestry', 'A fabric usually of worsted worked upon a warp of linen or other thread by hand the designs being usually more or less pictorial and the stuff employed for wall hangings and the like The term is also applied to different kinds of embroidery'),
(94177, 'Tapestry beetle', 'A small black dermestoid beetle Attagenus piceus whose larva feeds on tapestry carpets silk fur flour and various other goods'),
(94178, 'Tapet', 'Worked or figured stuff tapestry'),
(94179, 'Tapeti', 'A small South American hare Lepus Braziliensis'),
(94180, 'Tapetum', 'An area in the pigmented layer of the choroid coat of the eye in many animals which has an iridescent or metallic luster and helps to make the eye visible in the dark Sometimes applied to the whole layer of pigmented epithelium of the choroid'),
(94181, 'Tapeworm', 'Any one of numerous species of cestode worms belonging to Taenia and many allied genera The body is long flat and composed of numerous segments or proglottids varying in shape those toward the end of the body being much larger and longer than the anterior ones and containing the fully developed sexual organs The head is small destitute of a mouth but furnished with two or more suckers which vary greatly in shape in different genera and sometimes also with hooks for adhesion to the walls of the intestines of the animals in which they are parasitic The larvae see Cysticercus live in the flesh of various creatures and when swallowed by another animal of the right species develop into the mature tapeworm in its intestine See Illustration in Appendix'),
(94182, 'Taphouse', 'A house where liquors are retailed'),
(94183, 'Taphrenchyma', 'Same as Bothrenchyma'),
(94184, 'Tapinage', 'A lurking or skulking'),
(94185, 'Tapioca', 'A coarsely granular substance obtained by heating and thus partly changing the moistened starch obtained from the roots of the cassava It is much used in puddings and as a thickening for soups See Cassava'),
(94186, 'Tapir', 'Any one of several species of large odd toed ungulates belonging to Tapirus Elasmognathus and allied genera They have a long prehensile upper lip short ears short and stout legs a short thick tail and short close hair They have three toes on the hind feet and four toes on the fore feet but the outermost toe is of little use'),
(94187, 'Tapiroid', 'Allied to the tapir or the Tapir family'),
(94188, 'Tapis', 'Tapestry formerly the cover of a council table'),
(94189, 'Tapiser', 'A maker of tapestry an upholsterer'),
(94190, 'Tapish', 'To lie close to the ground so as to be concealed to squat to crouch hence to hide ones self'),
(94191, 'Taplash', 'Bad small beer also the refuse or dregs of liquor'),
(94192, 'Taplings', 'The strong double leathers by which the two parts of a flail are united'),
(94193, 'Tapoa tafa', 'A small carnivorous marsupial Phascogale penicillata having long soft fur and a very long tail with a tuft of long hairs at the end    called also brush tailed phascogale'),
(94194, 'Tappen', 'An obstruction or indigestible mass found in the intestine of bears and other animals during hibernation'),
(94195, 'Tapper', 'The lesser spotted woodpecker Dendrocopus minor    called also tapperer tabberer little wood pie barred woodpecker wood tapper hickwall and pump borer'),
(94196, 'Tappester', 'A female tapster'),
(94197, 'Tappet', 'A lever or projection moved by some other piece as a cam or intended to tap or touch something else with a view to produce change or regulate motion'),
(94198, 'Tappet rod', 'A rod carrying a tappet or tappets as one for closing the valves in a Cornish pumping engine'),
(94199, 'Tappice', 'See Tapish'),
(94200, 'Tappit hen', 'A hen having a tuft of feathers on her head'),
(94201, 'Tappoon', 'A piece of wood or sheet metal fitted into a ditch to dam up the water so as to overflow a field'),
(94202, 'Taproom', 'A room where liquors are kept on tap a barroom'),
(94203, 'Taproot', 'The root of a plant which penetrates the earth directly downward to a considerable depth without dividing'),
(94204, 'Tapster', 'One whose business is to tap or draw ale or other liquor'),
(94205, 'Taqua nut', 'A Central American name for the ivory nut'),
(94206, 'Tar', 'A sailor a seaman'),
(94207, 'Taranis', 'A Celtic divinity regarded as the evil principle but confounded by the Romans with Jupiter'),
(94208, 'Tarantass', 'A low four wheeled carriage used in Russia The carriage box rests on two long springy poles which run from the fore to the hind axletree When snow falls the wheels are taken off and the body is mounted on a sledge'),
(94209, 'Tarantella', 'A rapid and delirious sort of Neapolitan dance in 6 8 time which moves in whirling triplets    so called from a popular notion of its being a remedy against the poisonous bite of the tarantula Some derive its name from Taranto in Apulia'),
(94210, 'Tarantism', 'A nervous affection producing melancholy stupor and an uncontrollable desire to dance It was supposed to be produced by the bite of the tarantula and considered to be incapable of cure except by protracted dancing to appropriate music'),
(94211, 'Tarantula', 'Any one of several species of large spiders popularly supposed to be very venomous especially the European species Tarantula apuliae The tarantulas of Texas and adjacent countries are large species of Mygale'),
(94212, 'Tarantulated', 'Bitten by a tarantula affected with tarantism'),
(94213, 'Tarbogan', 'See Toboggan'),
