(96054, 'Thy', 'Of thee or belonging to thee the more common form of thine possessive case of thou    used always attributively and chiefly in the solemn or grave style and in poetry Thine is used in the predicate as the knife is thine See Thine'),
(96055, 'Thyine wood', 'The fragrant and beautiful wood of a North African tree Callitris quadrivalvis formerly called Thuja articulata The tree is of the Cedar family and furnishes a balsamic resin called sandarach'),
(96056, 'Thylacine', 'The zebra wolf See under Wolf'),
(96057, 'Thymate', 'A compound of thymol analogous to a salt as sodium thymate'),
(96058, 'Thyme', 'Any plant of the labiate genus Thymus The garden thyme Thymus vulgaris is a warm pungent aromatic much used to give a relish to seasoning and soups'),
(96059, 'Thymene', 'A liquid terpene obtained from oil of thyme'),
(96060, 'Thymiatechny', 'The art of employing perfumes in medicine'),
(96061, 'Thymic', 'Of or pertaining to the thymus gland'),
(96062, 'Thymol', 'A phenol derivative of cymene C10H13OH isomeric with carvacrol found in oil of thyme and extracted as a white crystalline substance of a pleasant aromatic odor and strong antiseptic properties    called also hydroxy cymene'),
(96063, 'thymus', 'Of pertaining to or designating the thymus gland'),
(96064, 'Thymy', 'Abounding with thyme fragrant as a thymy vale'),
(96065, 'Thyro ', 'A combining form used in anatomy to indicate connection with or relation to the thyroid body or the thyroid cartilage as thyrohyal'),
(96066, 'Thyroarytenoid', 'Of or pertaining to both the thyroid and arytenoid cartilages of the larynx'),
(96067, 'Thyrohyal', 'One of the lower segments in the hyoid arch often consolidated with the body of the hyoid bone and forming one of its great horns as in man'),
(96068, 'Thyrohyoid', 'Of or pertaining to the thyroid cartilage of the larynx and the hyoid arch'),
(96069, 'Thyroid', 'Shaped like an oblong shield shield shaped as the thyroid cartilage'),
(96070, 'Thyroideal', 'Thyroid'),
(96071, 'Thyrotomy', 'The operation of cutting into the thyroid cartilage'),
(96072, 'Thyrse', 'A thyrsus'),
(96073, 'Thyrsoid', 'Having somewhat the form of a thyrsus'),
(96074, 'Thyrsus', 'A staff entwined with ivy and surmounted by a pine cone or by a bunch of vine or ivy leaves with grapes or berries It is an attribute of Bacchus and of the satyrs and others engaging in Bacchic rites'),
(96075, 'Thysanopter', 'One of the Thysanoptera'),
(96076, 'Thysanoptera', 'A division of insects considered by some writers a distinct order but regarded by others as belonging to the Hemiptera They are all of small size and have narrow broadly fringed wings with rudimentary nervures Most of the species feed upon the juices of plants and some as those which attack grain are very injurious to crops Called also Physopoda  See Thrips'),
(96077, 'Thysanopteran', 'One of the Thysanoptera'),
(96078, 'Thysanopterous', 'Of or pertaining to the Thysanoptera'),
(96079, 'Thysanura', 'An order of wingless hexapod insects which have setiform caudal appendages either bent beneath the body to form a spring or projecting as bristles It comprises the Cinura or bristletails and the Collembola or springtails Called also Thysanoura See Lepisma and Podura'),
(96080, 'Thysanuran', 'One of the Thysanura Also used adjectively'),
(96081, 'Thysanurous', 'Of or pertaining to the Thysanura'),
(96082, 'Thysbe', 'A common clearwing moth Hemaris thysbe'),
(96083, 'Thyself', 'An emphasized form of the personal pronoun of the second person    used as a subject commonly with thou as thou thyself shalt go that is thou shalt go and no other It is sometimes used especially in the predicate without thou and in the nominative as well as in the objective case'),
(96084, 'Tiar', 'A tiara'),
(96085, 'Tiara', 'A form of headdress worn by the ancient Persians According to Xenophon the royal tiara was encircled with a diadem and was high and erect while those of the people were flexible or had rims turned over'),
(96086, 'Tiaraed', 'Adorned with or wearing a tiara'),
(96087, 'Tib cat', 'A female cat'),
(96088, 'Tibia', 'The inner or preaxial and usually the larger of the two bones of the leg or hind limb below the knee'),
(96089, 'Tibial', 'Of or pertaining to a tibia'),
(96090, 'Tibiale', 'The bone or cartilage of the tarsus which articulates with the tibia and corresponds to a part of the astragalus in man and most mammals'),
(96091, 'Tibicinate', 'To play on a tibia or pipe'),
(96092, 'Tibio ', 'A combining form used in anatomy to indicate connection with or relation to the tibia as tibiotarsus tibiofibular'),
(96093, 'Tibiotarsal', 'Of or pertaining to both to the tibia and the tarsus as the tibiotarsal articulation'),
(96094, 'Tibiotarsus', 'The large bone between the femur and tarsometatarsus in the leg of a bird It is formed by the union of the proximal part of the tarsus with the tibia'),
(96095, 'Tibrie', 'The pollack'),
(96096, 'Tic', 'A local and habitual convulsive motion of certain muscles especially such a motion of some of the muscles of the face twitching velication    called also spasmodic tic'),
(96097, 'Tical', 'A bean shaped coin of Siam worth about sixty cents also a weight equal to 236 grains troy'),
(96098, 'Tice', 'To entice'),
(96099, 'Ticement', 'Enticement'),
(96100, 'Tichorrhine', 'A fossil rhinoceros with a vertical bony medial septum supporting the nose the hairy rhinoceros'),
(96101, 'Tick', 'Credit trust as to buy on or upon tick'),
(96102, 'Ticken', 'See Ticking'),
(96103, 'Ticker', 'One who or that which ticks or produces a ticking sound as a watch or clock a telegraphic sounder etc'),
(96104, 'ticker tape parade', 'A parade to honor a person or persons held in New York City during which people in the tall buildings of Manhattan throw large quantities of paper confetti paper ribbons or the like onto the parading group  The name comes form the ticker tape originally thrown onto the parade when it passed stockbrokers offices in lower Manhattan before stock tickers became obsolete in subsequent years other types of waste paper were used to serve the honorary function as well as paper tape distributed specifically for the purpose of being thrown in such a parade'),
(96105, 'Ticket', 'A small piece of paper cardboard or the like serving as a notice certificate or distinguishing token of something'),
(96106, 'Ticketing', 'A periodical sale of ore in the English mining districts    so called from the tickets upon which are written the bids of the buyers'),
(96107, 'Ticking', 'A strong closely woven linen or cotton fabric of which ticks for beds are made It is usually twilled and woven in stripes of different colors as white and blue    called also ticken'),
(96108, 'Tickle', 'To touch lightly so as to produce a peculiar thrilling sensation which commonly causes laughter and a kind of spasm which become dangerous if too long protracted'),
(96109, 'Tickle footed', 'Uncertain inconstant slippery'),
(96110, 'Ticklenburg', 'A coarse mixed linen fabric made to be sold in the West Indies'),
(96111, 'Tickleness', 'Unsteadiness'),
(96112, 'Tickler', 'One who or that which tickles'),
(96113, 'Ticklish', 'Sensible to slight touches easily tickled as the sole of the foot is very ticklish the hardened palm of the hand is not ticklish'),
(96114, 'Tickseed', 'A seed or fruit resembling in shape an insect as that of certain plants'),
(96115, 'Ticktack', 'A noise like that made by a clock or a watch'),
(96116, 'Ticpolonga', 'A very venomous viper Daboia Russellii native of Ceylon and India    called also cobra monil'),
(96117, 'Tid', 'Tender soft nice    now only used in tidbit'),
(96118, 'Tidal', 'Of or pertaining to tides caused by tides having tides periodically rising and falling or following and ebbing as tidal waters'),
(96119, 'Tidal wave', 'an unusually high wave from the sea sometimes reaching far inland and causing great destruction and usually caused by some event such as an earthquake far from the shore  In Japan such a wave is called a tsunami'),
(96120, 'Tidbit', 'A delicate or tender piece of anything eatable a delicious morsel'),
