(96239, 'Timbuctoo', 'A city on the southern edge of the Sahara in central Africa some nine miles from the Niger  It is about three miles around and was formerly surrounded by a clay wall  Timbuctoo has a large caravan trade gold dust being the most important export  The people are negroes Tuariks Mandingoes Arabs Foolahs etc  The city was founded in the 12th century but was first seen by a white man in 1826  Timbuctoo now belongs to France and a railroad is proposed to connect Algiers Timbuctoo and Senegambia  Population 13000 1893 greatly increased during the trading season from November to January'),
(96240, 'Timburine', 'A tambourine'),
