(96950, 'Totemism', 'The system of distinguishing families clans etc in a tribe by the totem'),
(96951, 'Totemist', 'One belonging to a clan or tribe having a totem'),
(96952, 'Totem pole', 'A pole or pillar carved and  painted with a series of totemic symbols set up  before the house of certain Indian tribes of the northwest coast of North America esp Indians of the Koluschan stock'),
(96953, 'Toter', 'The stone roller See Stone roller a under Stone'),
(96954, 'Tother', 'A colloquial contraction of the other and formerly a contraction for that other See the Note under That 2'),
(96955, 'Totipalmate', 'Having all four toes united by a web    said of certain sea birds as the pelican and the gannet See Illust under Aves'),
(96956, 'Totipalmi', 'A division of swimming birds including those that have totipalmate feet'),
(96957, 'Totipresence', 'Omnipresence'),
(96958, 'Totipresent', 'Omnipresent'),
(96959, 'Totter', 'To shake so as to threaten a fall to vacillate to be unsteady to stagger as an old man totters with age'),
(96960, 'Totterer', 'One who totters'),
(96961, 'Totteringly', 'In a tottering manner'),
(96962, 'Tottery', 'Trembling or vaccilating as if about to fall unsteady shaking'),
(96963, 'Tottle', 'To walk in a wavering unsteady manner to toddle to topple'),
(96964, 'Tottlish', 'Trembling or tottering as if about to fall unsteady'),
(96965, 'Totty', 'Unsteady dizzy tottery'),
(96966, 'Toty', 'Totty'),
(96967, 'Toucan', 'Any one of numerous species of fruit eating birds of tropical America belonging to Ramphastos Pteroglossus and allied genera of the family Ramphastidae  They have a very large but light and thin beak often nearly as long as the body itself  Most of the species are brilliantly colored with red yellow white and black in striking contrast'),
(96968, 'Toucanet', 'A small toucan'),
(96969, 'Touch', 'To come in contact with to hit or strike lightly against to extend the hand foot or the like so as to reach or rest on'),
(96970, 'Touchable', 'Capable of being touched tangible'),
(96971, 'Touchback', 'The act of touching the football down by a player behind his own goal line when it received its last impulse from an opponent    distinguished from safety touchdown'),
(96972, 'Touch box', 'A box containing lighted tinder formerly carried by soldiers who used matchlocks to kindle the match'),
(96973, 'Touchdown', 'The act of touching the football down behind the opponents goal also the score 6 points resulting from such a play'),
(96974, 'Touchhole', 'The vent of a cannon or other firearm by which fire is communicateed to the powder of the charge'),
(96975, 'Touchily', 'In a touchy manner'),
(96976, 'Touchiness', 'The quality or state of being touchy peevishness irritability irascibility'),
(96977, 'Touching', 'Affecting moving pathetic as a touching tale'),
(96978, 'Touch me not', 'See Impatiens'),
(96979, 'Touch needle', 'A small bar of gold and silver either pure or alloyed in some known proportion with copper for trying the purity of articles of gold or silver by comparison of the streaks made by the article and the bar on a touchstone'),
(96980, 'Touch paper', 'Paper steeped in saltpeter which burns slowly and is used as a match for firing gunpowder and the like'),
(96981, 'Touchstone', 'Lydian stone basanite    so called because used to test the purity of gold and silver by the streak which is left upon the stone when it is rubbed by the metal See Basanite'),
(96982, 'touch on', 'To mention briefly or in passing'),
(96983, 'touch up', 'To make minor improvements in especially in the appearance'),
(96984, 'touch up paint', 'A paint applied to small areas of a painted surface to cover small blemishes especially on an automobile'),
(96985, 'Touchy', 'Peevish irritable irascible techy apt to take fire'),
(96986, 'Tough', 'Having the quality of flexibility without brittleness yielding to force without breaking capable of resisting great strain as the ligaments of animals are remarkably tough'),
(96987, 'Tough cake', 'See Tough pitch b'),
(96988, 'Toughen', 'To grow or make tough or tougher'),
(96989, 'Tough head', 'The ruddy duck'),
(96990, 'Toughish', 'Tough in a slight degree'),
(96991, 'Toughly', 'In a tough manner'),
(96992, 'Toughness', 'The quality or state of being tough'),
(96993, 'Tough pitch', 'The exact state or quality of texture and consistency of well reduced and refined copper'),
(96994, 'Touite', 'The wood warbler'),
(96995, 'Toupettit', 'The crested titmouse'),
(96996, 'Tour', 'A tower'),
(96997, 'Touraco', 'Same as Turacou'),
(96998, 'Tourbillion', 'An ornamental firework which turns round when in the air so as to form a scroll of fire'),
(96999, 'Touring car', 'An automobile designed for touring specif a roomy car not a limousine for five or more passengers'),
(97000, 'Tourist', 'One who makes a tour or performs a journey especially for pleasure'),
(97001, 'Tourmaline', 'A mineral occurring usually in three sided or six sided prisms terminated by rhombohedral or scalenohedral planes Black tourmaline schorl is the most common variety but there are also other varieties as the blue indicolite red rubellite also green brown and white The red and green varieties when transparent are valued as jewels'),
(97002, 'Tourn', 'A spinning wheel'),
(97003, 'Tournament', 'A mock fight or warlike game formerly in great favor in which a number of combatants were engaged as an exhibition of their address and bravery hence figuratively a real battle'),
(97004, 'Tournery', 'Work turned on a lathe turnery'),
(97005, 'Tourney', 'A tournament'),
(97006, 'Tourniquet', 'An instrument for arresting hemorrhage It consists essentially of a pad or compress upon which pressure is made by a band which is tightened by a screw or other means'),
(97007, 'Tournois', 'A former French money of account worth 20 sous or a franc It was thus called in distinction from the Paris livre which contained 25 sous'),
(97008, 'Touse', 'To pull to haul to tear to worry'),
(97009, 'Tousel', 'Same as Tousle'),
(97010, 'Touser', 'One who touses'),
(97011, 'Tousle', 'To put into disorder to tumble to touse'),
(97012, 'Tous les mois', 'A kind of starch with very large oval flattened grains often sold as arrowroot and extensively used for adulterating cocoa It is made from the rootstocks of a species of Canna probably Canna edulis the tubers of which are edible every month in the year'),
(97013, 'Tousy', 'Tousled tangled rough shaggy'),
(97014, 'Tout', 'To act as a tout See 2d Tout'),
(97015, 'Tout ensemble', 'All together hence in costume the fine arts etc the general effect of a work as a whole without regard to the execution of the separate perts'),
(97016, 'Touter', 'One who seeks customers as for an inn a public conveyance shops and the like hence an obtrusive candidate for office'),
(97017, 'Touze', 'See Touse'),
(97018, 'Tow', 'The coarse and broken part of flax or hemp separated from the finer part by the hatchel or swingle'),
(97019, 'Towage', 'The act of towing'),
(97020, 'Towall', 'A towel'),
(97021, 'Toward', 'In the direction of to'),
(97022, 'Towardliness', 'The quality or state of being towardly docility tractableness'),
(97023, 'Towardly', 'Same as Toward a 2'),
(97024, 'Towardness', 'Quality or state of being toward'),
(97025, 'Towards', 'See Toward'),
(97026, 'Towboat', 'A vessel constructed for being towed as a canal boat'),
(97027, 'Towel', 'A cloth used for wiping especially one used for drying anything wet as the person after a bath'),
(97028, 'Toweling', 'Cloth for towels especially such as is woven in long pieces to be cut at will as distinguished from that woven in towel lengths with borders etc'),
(97029, 'Tower', 'A mass of building standing alone and insulated usually higher than its diameter but when of great size not always of that proportion'),
(97030, 'Towered', 'Adorned or defended by towers'),
(97031, 'Towering', 'Very high elevated rising aloft as a towering height'),
(97032, 'Towery', 'Having towers adorned or defended by towers'),
(97033, 'Tow head', 'An urchin who has soft whitish hair'),
(97034, 'Towhee', 'The chewink'),
(97035, 'Towilly', 'The sanderling    so called from its cry'),
(97036, 'Towline', 'A line used to tow vessels a towrope'),
(97037, 'Town', 'Formerly a An inclosure which surrounded the mere homestead or dwelling of the lord of the manor Obs b The whole of the land which constituted the domain Obs c A collection of houses inclosed by fences or walls'),
(97038, 'town and gown', 'Of or pertaining to interactions between a college or university and the residents of the town in which the institution is located as a town and gown dispute'),
(97039, 'Town crier', 'A town officer who makes proclamations to the people the public crier of a town'),
(97040, 'Towned', 'Having towns containing many towns'),
(97041, 'Townhall', 'A public hall or building belonging to a town where the public offices are established the town council meets the people assemble in town meeting etc'),
(97042, 'Townhouse', 'A building devoted to the public used of a town a townhall'),
