(97199, 'Traduction', 'Transmission from one to another'),
(97200, 'Traductive', 'Capable of being deduced derivable'),
(97201, 'Traffic', 'To pass goods and commodities from one person to another for an equivalent in goods or money to buy or sell goods to barter to trade'),
(97202, 'Trafficable', 'Capable of being disposed of in traffic marketable'),
(97203, 'Trafficker', 'One who traffics or carries on commerce a trader a merchant'),
(97204, 'Trafficless', 'Destitute of traffic or trade'),
(97205, 'Traffic mile', 'Any unit of the total obtained by adding the passenger miles and ton miles in a railroads transportation for a given period    a term and practice of restricted or erroneous usage'),
(97206, 'Tragacanth', 'A kind of gum procured from a spiny leguminous shrub Astragalus gummifer of Western Asia and other species of Astragalus It comes in hard whitish or yellowish flakes or filaments and is nearly insoluble in water but slowly swells into a mucilaginous mass which is used as a substitute for gum arabic in medicine and the arts Called also gum tragacanth'),
(97207, 'Trag', 'A woman who plays in tragedy'),
(97208, 'Tragedious', 'Like tragedy tragical'),
(97209, 'Tragic', 'Of or pertaining to tragedy of the nature or character of tragedy as a tragic poem a tragic play or representation'),
(97210, 'Tragi comedy', 'A kind of drama representing some action in which serious and comic scenes are blended a composition partaking of the nature both of tragedy and comedy'),
(97211, 'Tragi comic', 'Of or pertaining to tragi comedy partaking of grave and comic scenes'),
(97212, 'Tragi comi pastoral', 'Partaking of the nature of or combining tragedy comedy and pastoral poetry'),
(97213, 'Tragopan', 'Any one of several species of Asiatic pheasants of the genus Ceriornis They are brilliantly colored with a variety of tints the back and breast are usually covered with white or buff ocelli and the head is ornamented with two bright colored fleshy wattles The crimson tragopan or horned pheasant Ceriornis satyra of India is one of the best known species'),
(97214, 'Tragus', 'The prominence in front of the external opening of the ear See Illust under Ear'),
(97215, 'T rail', 'See under T'),
(97216, 'Trail', 'To be drawn out in length to follow after'),
