(98367, 'Trumpetwood', 'A tropical American tree Cecropia peltata of the Breadfruit family having hollow stems which are used for wind instruments    called also snakewood and trumpet tree'),
(98368, 'Trumpie', 'The Richardsons skua Stercorarius parasiticus'),
(98369, 'Trumplike', 'Resembling a trumpet esp in sound as a trumplike voice'),
(98370, 'Truncal', 'Of or pertaining to the trunk or body'),
(98371, 'Truncate', 'To cut off to lop to maim'),
(98372, 'Truncated', 'Cut off cut short maimed'),
(98373, 'Truncation', 'The act of truncating lopping or cutting off'),
(98374, 'Trunch', 'A stake a small post'),
(98375, 'Truncheon', 'A short staff a club a cudgel a shaft of a spear'),
(98376, 'Truncheoned', 'Having a truncheon'),
(98377, 'Truncheoneer', 'A person armed with a truncheon'),
(98378, 'Truncus', 'The thorax of an insect See Trunk n 5'),
(98379, 'Trundle', 'A round body a little wheel'),
(98380, 'Trundle bed', 'A low bed that is moved on trundles or little wheels so that it can be pushed under a higher bed a truckle bed also sometimes a simiral bed without wheels'),
(98381, 'Trundlehead', 'One of the disks forming the ends of a lantern wheel or pinion'),
(98382, 'Trundletail', 'A round or curled up tail also a dog with such a tail'),
(98383, 'Trunk', 'The stem or body of a tree apart from its limbs and roots the main stem without the branches stock stalk'),
(98384, 'Trunkback', 'The leatherback'),
(98385, 'Trunked', 'Having such a trunk'),
(98386, 'Trunk engine', 'An engine having a trunk piston as most internal combustion engines'),
(98387, 'Trunkfish', 'Any one of several species of plectognath fishes belonging to the genus Ostracion or the family Ostraciontidae having an angular body covered with a rigid integument consisting of bony scales Some of the species are called also coffer fish and boxfish'),
(98388, 'Trunkful', 'As much as a trunk will hold enough to fill a trunk'),
(98389, 'Trunk piston', 'In a single acting engine an elongated hollow piston open at the end in which the end of the connecting rod is pivoted The piston rod crosshead and stuffing box are thus dispensed with'),
(98390, 'Trunk steamer', 'A freight steamer having a high hatch coaming extending almost continuously fore and aft but not of whaleback form at the sides'),
(98391, 'Trunkwork', 'Work or devices suitable to be concealed a secret stratagem'),
(98392, 'Trunnel', 'A trundle'),
(98393, 'Trunnion', 'A cylindrical projection on each side of a piece whether gun mortar or howitzer serving to support it on the cheeks of the carriage See Illust of Cannon'),
(98394, 'Trunnioned', 'Provided with trunnions as the trunnioned cylinder of an oscillating steam engine'),
(98395, 'Trusion', 'The act of pushing or thrusting'),
(98396, 'Truss', 'A bundle a package as a truss of grass'),
(98397, 'Trussing', 'The timbers etc which form a truss taken collectively'),
(98398, 'Trust', 'Assured resting of the mind on the integrity veracity justice friendship or other sound principle of another person confidence reliance reliance'),
(98399, 'Trust company', 'Any corporation formed for the purpose of acting as trustee  Such companies usually do more or less of a banking business'),
(98400, 'Trustee', 'A person to whom property is legally committed in trust to be applied either for the benefit of specified individuals or for public uses one who is intrusted with property for the benefit of another also a person in whose hands the effects of another are attached in a trustee process'),
(98401, 'Trustee process', 'The process of attachment by garnishment'),
(98402, 'Trusteeship', 'The office or duty of a trustee'),
(98403, 'Trustee stock', 'High grade stock in which trust funds may be legally invested'),
(98404, 'Truster', 'One who trusts or credits'),
(98405, 'Trustful', 'Full of trust trusting'),
(98406, 'Trustily', 'In a trusty manner'),
(98407, 'Trustiness', 'The quality or state of being trusty'),
(98408, 'Trusting', 'Having or exercising trust confiding unsuspecting trustful'),
(98409, 'Trustless', 'That may not be trusted not worthy of trust unfaithful'),
(98410, 'Trustworthy', 'Worthy of trust or confidence trusty'),
(98411, 'Trusty', 'Admitting of being safely trusted justly deserving confidence fit to be confided in trustworthy reliable'),
(98412, 'Truth', 'The quality or being true as    a Conformity to fact or reality exact accordance with that which is or has been or shall be'),
(98413, 'Truthful', 'Full of truth veracious reliable'),
(98414, 'Truthless', 'Devoid of truth dishonest dishonest spurious faithless'),
(98415, 'Truth lover', 'One who loves the truth'),
(98416, 'Truthness', 'Truth'),
(98417, 'Truth teller', 'One who tells the truth'),
(98418, 'Truthy', 'Truthful likely probable'),
(98419, 'Trutination', 'The act of weighing'),
(98420, 'Truttaceous', 'Of pertaining to or resembling a trout as fish of the truttaceous kind'),
(98421, 'Try', 'To divide or separate as one sort from another to winnow to sift to pick out    frequently followed by out as to try out the wild corn from the good'),
(98422, 'Try cock', 'A cock for withdrawing a small quantity of liquid as for testing'),
(98423, 'Trygon', 'Any one of several species of large sting rays belonging to Trygon and allied genera'),
(98424, 'Trying', 'Adapted to try or put to severe trial severe afflictive as a trying occasion or position'),
(98425, 'Tryout', 'a test of the performance capability of a person to ascertain fitness for a particular task in sports a test by which the fitness of a player or contestant to remain in a certain class is determined'),
(98426, 'Try out', 'to compete for a postition as on a sports team or in a theatrical presentation'),
(98427, 'Trypsin', 'A proteolytic enzyme present in the pancreatic juice Unlike the pepsin of the gastric juice it acts in a neutral or alkaline fluid and not only converts the albuminous matter of the food into soluble peptones but also in part into leucin and tyrosin'),
(98428, 'Trypsinogen', 'The antecedent of trypsin a substance which is contained in the cells of the pancreas and gives rise to the trypsin'),
(98429, 'Tryptic', 'Relating to trypsin or to its action produced by trypsin as trypsin digestion'),
(98430, 'Tryptone', 'The peptone formed by pancreatic digestion    so called because it is formed through the agency of the ferment trypsin'),
(98431, 'Trysail', 'A fore and aft sail bent to a gaff and hoisted on a lower mast or on a small mast called the trysail mast close abaft a lower mast    used chiefly as a storm sail Called also spencer'),
(98432, 'Try square', 'An instrument used by carpenters joiners etc for laying off right angles off right angles and testing whether work is square'),
(98433, 'Tryst', 'Trust'),
(98434, 'Tryster', 'One who makes an appointment or tryst one who meets with another'),
(98435, 'Trysting', 'An appointment a tryst'),
(98436, 'Tsar', 'The title of the emperor of Russia See Czar'),
(98437, 'Tsarina', 'The title of the empress of Russia See Czarina'),
(98438, 'Tschakmeck', 'The chameck'),
(98439, 'Tschego', 'A West African anthropoid ape allied to the gorilla and chimpanzee and by some considered only a variety of the chimpanzee It is noted for building large umbrella shaped nests in trees Called also tscheigo tschiego nschego nscheigo'),
(98440, 'Tsebe', 'The springbok'),
(98441, 'Tsetse', 'A venomous two winged African fly Glossina morsitans whose bite is very poisonous and even fatal to horses and cattle but harmless to men It renders extensive districts in which it abounds uninhabitable during certain seasons of the year'),
(98442, 'T square', 'See under T'),
(98443, 'Tsung li Yamen', 'The board or department of foreign affairs in the Chinese government See Yamen'),
(98444, 'Tsung tu', 'A viceroy or governor general the highest provincial official in China with civil and military authority over one or more provinces'),
(98445, 'Tuatara', 'A large iguanalike reptile Sphenodon punctatum formerly common in New Zealand but by 1900 confined to certain islets near the coast It reaches a length of two and a half feet is dark olive green with small white or yellowish specks on the sides and has yellow spines along the back except on the neck  It is the only surviving member of the order Rhyncocephala  Also called tuatera and hatteria'),
(98446, 'Tuatera', 'Same as tuatara'),
(98447, 'Tub', 'An open wooden vessel formed with staves bottom and hoops a kind of short cask half barrel or firkin usually with but one head    used for various purposes'),
(98448, 'Tuba', 'An ancient trumpet'),
(98449, 'Tubal', 'Of or pertaining to a tube specifically of or pertaining to one of the Fallopian tubes as tubal pregnancy'),
(98450, 'Tubbing', 'The forming of a tub also collectively materials for tubs'),
(98451, 'Tubby', 'Resembling a tub specifically sounding dull and without resonance like a tub wanting elasticity or freedom of sound as a tubby violin'),
(98452, 'Tube', 'A hollow cylinder of any material used for the conveyance of fluids and for various other purposes a pipe'),
(98453, 'Tubeform', 'In the form of a tube tubular tubiform'),
(98454, 'Tube nosed', 'Having the nostrils prolonged in the form of horny tubes along the sides of the beak    said of certain sea birds'),
(98455, 'Tuber', 'A fleshy rounded stem or root usually containing starchy matter as the potato or arrowroot a thickened root stock See Illust of Tuberous'),
(98456, 'Tube railway', 'an electrically powered railroad with tracks running through a tunnel underground a subway'),
(98457, 'Tubercled', 'Having tubercles affected with tubercles tuberculate as a tubercled lung or stalk'),
(98458, 'Tubercular', 'Having tubercles affected with tubercles tubercled tuberculate'),
(98459, 'Tubercularize', 'To infect with tuberculosis'),
(98460, 'Tuberculate', 'Tubercled tubercular'),
(98461, 'Tuberculin', 'A fluid containing the products formed by the growth of the tubercle bacillus in a suitable culture medium'),
(98462, 'Tuberculin test', 'The hypodermic injection of tuberculin used as a test for past or present infection with tuberculosis  It is used both for humans and cattle'),
(98463, 'Tuberculization', 'The development of tubercles the condition of one who is affected with tubercles'),
(98464, 'Tuberculocidal', 'able to kill Mycobacterum tuberculosis'),
(98465, 'Tuberculocidin', 'A special substance contained in tuberculin supposed to be the active agent of the latter freed from various impurities'),
(98466, 'Tuberculoid', 'Resembling a tubercle'),
(98467, 'Tuberculose', 'Having tubercles affected with or characterized by tubercles tubercular'),
(98468, 'Tuberculosed', 'Affected with tuberculosis'),
(98469, 'Tuberculosis', 'A constitutional disease caused by infection with Mycobacterium tuberculosis also called the Tubercle bacillus characterized by the production of tubercles in the internal organs and especially in the lungs where it constitutes the most common variety of pulmonary phthisis consumption  The Mycobacteria are slow growing and without cell walls and are thus not affected by the beta lactam antibiotics treatment is difficult usually requiring simultaneous administration of multiple antibiotics to effect a cure  Prior to availability of antibiotic treatment the cure required extensive rest for which special sanatoriums were constructed'),
(98470, 'Tuberculous', 'Pertaining to or affected with a tuberculosis'),
(98471, 'Tuberculum', 'A tubercle'),
(98472, 'Tuberiferous', 'Producing or bearing tubers'),
(98473, 'Tuberose', 'A plant Polianthes tuberosa with a tuberous root and a liliaceous flower It is much cultivated for its beautiful and fragrant white blossoms'),
(98474, 'Tuberosity', 'The state of being tuberous'),
(98475, 'Tube shell', 'Any bivalve mollusk which secretes a shelly tube around its siphon as the watering shell'),
(98476, 'Tubeworm', 'Any annelid which constructs a tube one of the Tubicolae'),
(98477, 'Tubfish', 'The sapphirine gurnard Trigla hirundo See Illust under Gurnard'),
(98478, 'Tubful', 'As much as a tub will hold enough to fill a tub'),
(98479, 'Tubicinate', 'To blow a trumpet'),
(98480, 'Tubicolae', 'A division of annelids including those which construct and habitually live in tubes The head or anterior segments usually bear gills and cirri Called also Sedentaria and Capitibranchiata See Serpula and Sabella'),
(98481, 'Tubicolar', 'Tubicolous'),
(98482, 'Tubicole', 'One of the Tubicolae'),
(98483, 'Tubicolous', 'Inhabiting a tube as tubicolous worms'),
(98484, 'Tubicorn', 'Any ruminant having horns composed of a bony axis covered with a horny sheath a hollow horned ruminant'),
(98485, 'Tubicornous', 'Having hollow horns'),
(98486, 'Tubiform', 'Having the form of a tube tubeform'),
(98487, 'Tubinares', 'A tribe of sea birds comprising the petrels shearwaters albatrosses hagdons and allied birds having tubular horny nostrils'),
(98488, 'Tubing', 'The act of making tubes'),
(98489, 'Tubipora', 'A genus of halcyonoids in which the skeleton or coral called organ pipe coral consists of a mass of parallel cylindrical tubes united at intervals by transverse plates These corals are usually red or purple and form large masses They are natives of the tropical parts of the Indian and Pacific Oceans'),
(98490, 'Tubipore', 'Any species of the genus Tubipora'),
(98491, 'Tubiporite', 'Any fossil coral of the genus Syringopora consisting of a cluster of upright tubes united together by small transverse tubules'),
(98492, 'Tubivalve', 'A shell or tube formed by an annelid as a serpula'),
(98493, 'Tubman', 'One of the two most experienced barristers in the Court of Exchequer  Cf Postman 2'),
(98494, 'Tubular', 'Having the form of a tube or pipe consisting of a pipe fistular as a tubular snout a tubular calyx Also containing or provided with tubes'),
(98495, 'Tubularia', 'A genus of hydroids having large naked flowerlike hydranths at the summits of long slender usually simple stems The gonophores are small and form clusters at the bases of the outer tentacles'),
(98496, 'Tubulariae', 'See Tubularida'),
(98497, 'Tubularian', 'Any hydroid belonging to the suborder Tubularida'),
(98498, 'Tubularida', 'An extensive division of Hydroidea the tubularians    called also Athecata Gymnoblastea and Tubulariae'),
(98499, 'Tubulate', 'Tubular tubulated tubulous'),
(98500, 'Tubulated', 'Made in the form of a small tube provided with a tube or elongated opening'),
(98501, 'Tubulation', 'The act of shaping or making a tube or of providing with a tube also a tube or tubulure as the tubulation of a retort'),
(98502, 'Tubulature', 'A tubulure'),
(98503, 'Tubule', 'A small pipe or fistular body a little tube'),
(98504, 'Tubulibranchian', 'One of the Tubulibranchiata'),
(98505, 'Tubulibranchiata', 'A group of gastropod mollusks having a tubular shell Vermetus is an example'),
(98506, 'Tubulicole', 'Any hydroid which has tubular chitinous stems'),
(98507, 'Tubulidentate', 'Having teeth traversed by canals    said of certain edentates'),
(98508, 'Tubuliform', 'Having the form of a small tube'),
(98509, 'Tubulipore', 'Any one of numerous species of Bryozoa belonging to Tubulipora and allied genera having tubular calcareous calicles'),
(98510, 'Tubulose', 'Resembling or in the form of a tube longitudinally hollow specifically Bot having a hollow cylindrical corolla often expanded or toothed at the border as a tubulose flower'),
(98511, 'Tubulure', 'A short tubular opening at the top of a retort or at the top or side of a bottle a tubulation'),
(98512, 'Tucan', 'The Mexican pocket gopher Geomys Mexicanus It resembles the common pocket gopher of the Western United States but is larger Called also tugan and tuza'),
(98513, 'Tucet', 'See Tucket a steak'),
(98514, 'Tuch', 'A dark colored kind of marble touchstone'),
(98515, 'Tuck', 'A long narrow sword a rapier'),
(98516, 'Tuckahoe', 'A curious vegetable production of the Southern Atlantic United States growing under ground like a truffle and often attaining immense size The real nature is unknown Called also Indian bread and Indian loaf'),
(98517, 'Tucker', 'One who or that which tucks specifically an instrument with which tuck are made'),
(98518, 'Tucket', 'A slight flourish on a trumpet a fanfare'),
(98519, 'Tuck net', 'See Tuck n 2'),
(98520, 'Tuck pointing', 'The finishing of joints along the center lines with a narrow ridge of putty or fine lime mortar'),
(98521, 'Tucum', 'A fine strong fiber obtained from the young leaves of a Brazilian palm Astrocaryum vulgare used for cordage bowstrings etc also the plant yielding this fiber Called also tecum and tecum fiber'),
(98522, 'Tucuma', 'A Brazilian palm Astrocaryum Tucuma which furnishes an edible fruit'),
(98523, 'Tudor', 'Of or pertaining to a royal line of England descended from Owen Tudor of Wales who married the widowed queen of Henry V The first reigning Tudor was Henry VII the last Elizabeth'),
(98524, 'Tue', 'The parson bird'),
(98525, 'Tuefall', 'See To fall'),
(98526, 'Tue iron', 'See Tuy'),
(98527, 'Tue irons', 'A pair of blacksmiths tongs'),
(98528, 'Tuesday', 'The third day of the week following Monday and preceding Wednesday'),
(98529, 'Tuet', 'The lapwing'),
(98530, 'Tufa', 'A soft or porous stone formed by depositions from water usually calcareous    called also calcareous tufa'),
(98531, 'Tufaceous', 'Pertaining to tufa consisting of or resembling tufa'),
(98532, 'Tuff', 'Same as Tufa'),
(98533, 'Tuffoon', 'See Typhoon'),
(98534, 'Tuft', 'A collection of small flexible or soft things in a knot or bunch a waving or bending and spreading cluster as a tuft of flowers or feathers'),
(98535, 'Tuf taffeta', 'A silk fabric formerly in use having a nap or pile'),
(98536, 'Tufted', 'Adorned with a tuft as the tufted duck'),
(98537, 'Tufthunter', 'A hanger on to noblemen or persons of quality especially in English universities a toady See 1st Tuft 3'),
(98538, 'Tufthunting', 'The practice of seeking after and hanging on noblemen or persons of quality especially in English universities'),
(98539, 'Tufty', 'Abounding with tufts'),
(98540, 'Tug', 'To pull or draw with great effort to draw along with continued exertion to haul along to tow as to tug a loaded cart to tug a ship into port'),
(98541, 'Tugan', 'Same as Tucan'),
(98542, 'Tugboat', 'See Tug n 3'),
(98543, 'Tugger', 'One who tugs'),
(98544, 'Tuggingly', 'In a tugging manner with laborious pulling'),
(98545, 'Tuille', 'In plate armor a suspended plate in from of the thigh See Illust of Tasses'),
(98546, 'Tuition', 'Superintending care over a young person the particular watch and care of a tutor or guardian over his pupil or ward guardianship'),
(98547, 'Tuitionary', 'Of or pertaining to tuition'),
(98548, 'Tuko tuko', 'A burrowing South American rodent Ctenomys Braziliensis It has small eyes and ears and a short tail It resembles the pocket gopher in size form and habits but is more nearly allied to the porcupines'),
(98549, 'Tula metal', 'An alloy of silver copper and lead made at Tula in Russia'),
(98550, 'Tule', 'A large bulrush Scirpus lacustris and Scirpus Tatora growing abundantly on overflowed land in California and elsewhere'),
(98551, 'Tulip', 'Any plant of the liliaceous genus Tulipa  Many varieties are cultivated for their beautiful often variegated flowers'),
(98552, 'Tulip eared', 'Having erect pointed ears prick eared    said of certain dogs'),
(98553, 'Tulipist', 'A person who is especially devoted to the cultivation of tulips'),
(98554, 'Tulipomania', 'A violent passion for the acquisition or cultivation of tulips    a word said by Beckman to have been coined by Menage'),
(98555, 'Tulipomaniac', 'One who is affected with tulipomania'),
(98556, 'Tulip shell', 'A large handsomely colored marine univalve shell Fasciolaria tulipa native of the Southern United States  The name is sometimes applied also to other species of Fasciolaria'),
(98557, 'Tulipwood', 'The beautiful rose colored striped wood of a Brazilian tree Physocalymna floribunda much used by cabinetmakers for inlaying'),
(98558, 'Tull', 'To allure to tole'),
(98559, 'Tulle', 'A kind of silk lace or light netting used for veils etc'),
(98560, 'Tullian', 'Belonging to or in the style of Tully Marcus Tullius Cicero'),
(98561, 'Tullibee', 'A whitefish Coregonus tullibee found in the Great Lakes of North America    called also mongrel whitefish'),
(98562, 'Tumble', 'To roll over or to and fro to throw ones self about as a person in pain tumbles and tosses'),
(98563, 'Tumblebug', 'See Tumbledung'),
(98564, 'Tumble down', 'Ready to fall dilapidated ruinous as a tumble down house'),
(98565, 'Tumbledung', 'Any one of numerous species of scaraboid beetles belonging to Scarabaeus Copris Phanaeus and allied genera The female lays her eggs in a globular mass of dung which she rolls by means of her hind legs to a burrow excavated in the earth in which she buries it'),
(98566, 'Tumbler', 'One who tumbles one who plays tricks by various motions of the body an acrobat'),
(98567, 'Tumblerful', 'As much as a tumbler will hold enough to fill a tumbler'),
(98568, 'Tumbleweed', 'Any plant which habitually breaks away from its roots in the autumn and is driven by the wind as a light rolling mass over the fields and prairies such as witch grass wild indigo Amarantus albus etc'),
(98569, 'Tumbling', 'a  vb n from Tumble v'),
(98570, 'Tumbrel', 'A cucking stool for the punishment of scolds'),
(98571, 'Tumefacient', 'Producing swelling tumefying'),
(98572, 'Tumefaction', 'The act or process of tumefying swelling or rising into a tumor a tumor a swelling'),
(98573, 'Tumefy', 'To swell to cause to swell or puff up'),
(98574, 'Tumescence', 'The act of becoming tumid the state of being swollen intumescence    applied especially to the state of swelling of the vascular tissue in the male and female sex organs when they have been stimulated to readiness for sexual intercourse'),
(98575, 'Tumescent', 'Slightly tumid swollen as certain moss capsules'),
(98576, 'Tumid', 'Swelled enlarged or distended as a tumid leg tumid flesh'),
(98577, 'Tumidity', 'The quality or state of being tumid'),
(98578, 'Tummals', 'A great quantity or heap'),
(98579, 'Tumor', 'A morbid swelling prominence or growth on any part of the body especially a growth produced by deposition of new tissue a neoplasm'),
(98580, 'Tumored', 'Distended swelled'),
(98581, 'Tumorous', 'Swelling protuberant'),
(98582, 'Tump', 'A little hillock a knoll'),
(98583, 'Tumpline', 'A strap placed across a mans forehead to assist him in carrying a pack on his back'),
(98584, 'Tum tum', 'A dish made in the West Indies by beating boiled plantain quite soft in a wooden mortar'),
(98585, 'Tumular', 'Consisting in a heap formed or being in a heap or hillock'),
(98586, 'Tumulate', 'To cover as a corpse with a mound or tomb to bury'),
(98587, 'Tumulose', 'Tumulous'),
(98588, 'Tumulosity', 'The quality or state of being tumulous hilliness');
INSERT INTO `dictionary` (`id`, `word`, `definition`) VALUES
(98589, 'Tumulous', 'Full of small hills or mounds hilly tumulose'),
(98590, 'Tumult', 'The commotion or agitation of a multitude usually accompanied with great noise uproar and confusion of voices hurly burly noisy confusion'),
(98591, 'Tumulter', 'A maker of tumults'),
(98592, 'Tumultuarily', 'In a tumultuary manner'),
(98593, 'Tumultuariness', 'The quality or state of being tumultuary'),
(98594, 'Tumultuary', 'Attended by or producing a tumult disorderly promiscuous confused tumultuous'),
(98595, 'Tumultuate', 'To make a tumult'),
(98596, 'Tumultuation', 'Irregular or disorderly movement commotion as the tumultuation of the parts of a fluid'),
(98597, 'Tumultuous', 'Full of tumult characterized by tumult disorderly turbulent'),
(98598, 'Tumulus', 'An artificial hillock especially one raised over a grave particularly over the graves of persons buried in ancient times a barrow'),
(98599, 'Tun', 'A large cask an oblong vessel bulging in the middle like a pipe or puncheon and girt with hoops a wine cask'),
(98600, 'Tuna', 'The Opuntia Tuna See Prickly pear under Prickly'),
(98601, 'Tunable', 'Capable of being tuned or made harmonious hence harmonious musical tuneful'),
(98602, 'Tun bellied', 'Having a large protuberant belly or one shaped like a tun pot bellied'),
(98603, 'Tun dish', 'A tunnel'),
(98604, 'Tundra', 'One of the level or undulating treeless plains characteristic of northern arctic regions in both hemispheres the term is most commony associated with the arctic plains of Siberia  The tundras mark the limit of arborescent vegetation they consist of black mucky soil with a permanently frozen subsoil but support a dense growth of mosses and lichens and dwarf herbs and shrubs often showy flowered'),
(98605, 'Tune', 'A sound a note a tone'),
(98606, 'Tuneful', 'Harmonious melodious musical as tuneful notes'),
(98607, 'Tuneless', 'Without tune inharmonious unmusical'),
(98608, 'Tuner', 'One who tunes especially one whose occupation is to tune musical instruments'),
(98609, 'Tun great', 'Having the circumference of a tun'),
(98610, 'Tungstate', 'A salt of tungstic acid a wolframate'),
(98611, 'Tungsten', 'A rare element of the chromium group found in certain minerals as wolfram and scheelite and isolated as a heavy steel gray metal which is very hard and infusible It has both acid and basic properties When alloyed in small quantities with steel it greatly increases its hardness Symbol W Wolframium Atomic weight 1836 Specific gravity 18'),
(98612, 'Tungstenic', 'Of or pertaining to tungsten containing tungsten as tungstenic ores'),
(98613, 'Tungsten lamp', 'An electric glow lamp having filaments of metallic tungsten and contained in a glass bulb which is evacuated or has an inert gas to avoid oxidation of the tungsten a common form of light bulb Such lamps owing to the refractory nature of the metal may be maintained at a very high temperature and require an expenditure of only about 125 watts per candle power depending on the total wattage and the design of the bulb  By mid 20th century tungsten lamps became the most common type of incandescent as contrasted with fluorescent lamp thus the phrase incandescent lamp or incandescent light typically refers to a tungsten lamp'),
(98614, 'Tungsten steel', 'A steel containing a small amount of tungsten noted for tenacity and hardness even under a considerable degree of heat  Magnets made of it are said to be highly permanent  It often contains manganese'),
(98615, 'Tungstic', 'Of or pertaining to tungsten derived from or resembling tungsten wolframic as tungstic oxide'),
(98616, 'Tungstite', 'The oxide of tungsten a yellow mineral occurring in a pulverulent form It is often associated with wolfram'),
(98617, 'Tunguses', 'A group of roving Turanian tribes occupying Eastern Siberia and the Amur valley They resemble the Mongols'),
(98618, 'Tungusic', 'Of or pertaining to the Tunguses as the Tungusic dialects'),
(98619, 'Tunhoof', 'Ground ivy alehoof'),
(98620, 'Tunicary', 'One of the Tunicata'),
(98621, 'Tunicata', 'A grand division of the animal kingdom intermediate in some respects between the invertebrates and vertebrates and in modern classifications considered a subphylum of the vertebrates called also urochordata  They were formerly classed with acephalous mollusks  The body is usually covered with a firm external tunic consisting in part of cellulose and having two openings one for the entrance and one for the exit of water  The pharynx is usually dilated in the form of a sac pierced by several series of ciliated slits and serves as a gill'),
(98622, 'Tunicate', 'Covered with a tunic covered or coated with layers as a tunicated bulb'),
(98623, 'Tunicin', 'Animal cellulose a substance present in the mantle or tunic of the Tunicates which resembles or is identical with the cellulose of the vegetable kingdom'),
(98624, 'Tunicle', 'A slight natural covering an integument'),
