(98991, 'Tympanitis', 'Inflammation of the lining membrane of the middle ear'),
(98992, 'Tympanize', 'To drum'),
(98993, 'Tympano', 'A kettledrum    chiefly used in the plural to denote the kettledrums of an orchestra See Kettledrum'),
(98994, 'Tympanohyal', 'Of or pertaining to the tympanum and the hyoidean arch'),
(98995, 'Tympanum', 'The ear drum or middle ear Sometimes applied incorrectly to the tympanic membrane See Ear'),
(98996, 'Tympany', 'A flatulent distention of the belly tympanites'),
(98997, 'Tynd', 'To shut to close'),
(98998, 'Tyne', 'To lose'),
(98999, 'Tyny', 'Small tiny'),
(99000, 'Typal', 'Relating to a type or types belonging to types serving as a type typical'),
(99001, ' type', 'A combining form signifying impressed form stamp print type typical form representative as in stereotype phototype ferrotype monotype'),
(99002, 'Type', 'The mark or impression of something stamp impressed sign emblem'),
(99003, 'Typesetter', 'One who or that which sets type a compositor a machine for setting type'),
(99004, 'Typesetting', 'The act or art of setting type'),
(99005, 'Typewrite', 'To write with a typewriter'),
(99006, 'Typewriter', 'An instrument for writing by means of type a typewheel or the like in which the operator makes use of a sort of keyboard in order to obtain printed impressions of the characters upon paper'),
(99007, 'Typewriting', 'The act or art of using a typewriter also a print made with a typewriter'),
(99008, 'Typhlitis', 'Inflammation of the caecum'),
(99009, 'Typhlosole', 'A fold of the wall which projects into the cavity of the intestine in bivalve mollusks certain annelids starfishes and some other animals'),
(99010, 'Typhoean', 'Of or pertaining to Typhoeus t'),
(99011, 'Typhoid', 'Of or pertaining to typhus resembling typhus of a low grade like typhus as typhoid symptoms'),
(99012, 'Typhomalarial', 'Pertaining to typhoid fever and malaria as typhomalarial fever a form of fever having symptoms both of malarial and typhoid fever'),
(99013, 'Typhomania', 'A low delirium common in typhus fever'),
(99014, 'Typhon', 'According to Hesiod the son of Typhoeus and father of the winds but later identified with him'),
(99015, 'Typhoon', 'A violent whirlwind specifically a violent whirlwind occurring in the Chinese seas'),
(99016, 'Typhos', 'Typhus'),
(99017, 'Typhotoxin', 'A basic substance C7H17NO2 formed from the growth of the typhoid bacillus on meat pulp It induces in small animals lethargic conditions with liquid dejecta'),
(99018, 'Typhous', 'Of or pertaining to typhus of the nature of typhus'),
(99019, 'Typhus', 'A contagious continued fever lasting from two to three weeks attended with great prostration and cerebral disorder and marked by a copious eruption of red spots upon the body Also called jail fever famine fever putrid fever spottled fever etc See Jail fever under Jail'),
(99020, 'Typic', 'Typical'),
(99021, 'Typical', 'Of the nature of a type representing something by a form model or resemblance emblematic prefigurative'),
(99022, 'Typification', 'The act of typifying or representing by a figure'),
(99023, 'Typifier', 'One who or that which typifies'),
(99024, 'Typify', 'To represent by an image form model or resemblance'),
(99025, 'Typist', 'A person who operates a typewriting machine a typewriter'),
(99026, 'Typo', 'A compositor'),
(99027, 'Typocosmy', 'A representation of the world'),
(99028, 'Typograph', 'A machine for setting type or for casting lines of type and setting them'),
(99029, 'Typographer', 'A printer'),
(99030, 'Typographic', 'Of or pertaining to the act or art of representing by types or symbols emblematic figurative typical'),
(99031, 'Typography', 'The act or art of expressing by means of types or symbols emblematical or hieroglyphic representation'),
(99032, 'Typolite', 'A stone or fossil which has on it impressions or figures of plants and animals'),
(99033, 'Typolithography', 'A branch of lithography in which impressions from printers types are transferred to stone for reproduction'),
(99034, 'Typology', 'A discourse or treatise on types'),
(99035, 'Typothetae', 'Printers    used in the name of an association of the master printers of the United States and Canada called The United Typothetae of America'),
(99036, 'Tyran', 'A tyrant'),
(99037, 'Tyranness', 'A female tyrant'),
(99038, 'Tyrannic', 'Of or pertaining to a tyrant suiting a tyrant unjustly severe in government absolute imperious despotic cruel arbitrary as a tyrannical prince a tyrannical master tyrannical government'),
(99039, 'Tyrannicidal', 'Of or pertaining to tyrannicide or the murder of a tyrant'),
(99040, 'Tyrannicide', 'The act of killing a tyrant'),
(99041, 'Tyrannish', 'Like a tyrant tyrannical'),
(99042, 'Tyrannize', 'To act the tyrant to exercise arbitrary power to rule with unjust and oppressive severity to exercise power over others not permitted by law or required by justice or with a severity not necessary to the ends of justice and government as a prince will often tyrannize over his subjects masters sometimes tyrannize over their servants or apprentices'),
(99043, 'Tyrannous', 'Tyrannical arbitrary unjustly severe despotic'),
(99044, 'Tyranny', 'The government or authority of a tyrant a country governed by an absolute ruler hence arbitrary or despotic exercise of power exercise of power over subjects and others with a rigor not authorized by law or justice or not requisite for the purposes of government'),
(99045, 'Tyrant', 'An absolute ruler a sovereign unrestrained by law or constitution a usurper of sovereignty'),
(99046, 'Tyre', 'Curdled milk'),
(99047, 'Tyrian', 'Of or pertaining to Tyre or its people'),
(99048, 'Tyro', 'A beginner in learning one who is in the rudiments of any branch of study a person imperfectly acquainted with a subject a novice'),
(99049, 'Tyrociny', 'The state of being a tyro or beginner apprenticeship'),
(99050, 'Tyrolite', 'A translucent mineral of a green color and pearly or vitreous luster It is a hydrous arseniate of copper'),
(99051, 'Tyronism', 'The state of being a tyro or beginner'),
(99052, 'Tyrosin', 'A white crystalline nitrogenous substance present in small amount in the pancreas and spleen and formed in large quantity from the decomposition of proteid matter by various means    as by pancreatic digestion by putrefaction as of cheese by the action of boiling acids etc Chemically it consists of oxyphenol and amidopropionic acid and by decomposition yields oxybenzoic acid or some other benzol derivative'),
(99053, 'Tyrotoxicon', 'A ptomaine discovered by Vaughan in putrid cheese and other dairy products and producing symptoms similar to cholera infantum Chemically it appears to be related to or identical with diazobenzol'),
(99054, 'Tyrotoxine', 'Same as Tyrotoxicon'),
(99055, 'Tysonite', 'A fluoride of the cerium metals occurring in hexagonal crystals of a pale yellow color   Cf Fluocerite'),
(99056, 'Tystie', 'The black guillemot'),
(99057, 'Tythe', 'See Tithe'),
(99058, 'Tything', 'See Tithing'),
(99059, 'Tzar', 'The emperor of Russia  See Czar'),
(99060, 'Tzarina', 'The empress of Russia  See Czarina'),
(99061, 'Tzetze', 'Same as Tsetse'),
(99062, 'U', 'the twenty first letter of the English alphabet is a cursive form of the letter V with which it was formerly used interchangeably both letters being then used both as vowels and consonants U and V are now however differentiated U being used only as a vowel or semivowel and V only as a consonant The true primary vowel sound of U in Anglo Saxon was the sound which it still retains in most of the languages of Europe that of long oo as in tool and short oo as in wood answering to the French ou in tour Etymologically U is most closely related to o y vowel w and v as in two duet dyad twice top tuft sop sup auspice aviary See V also O and Y'),
(99063, 'Uakari', 'Same as Ouakari'),
(99064, 'Uberous', 'Fruitful copious abundant plentiful'),
(99065, 'Uberty', 'Fruitfulness copiousness abundance plenty'),
(99066, 'Ubication', 'The quality or state of being in a place local relation position or location whereness'),
(99067, 'Ubiquarian', 'Ubiquitous'),
(99068, 'Ubiquist', 'One of a school of Lutheran divines which held that the body of Christ is present everywhere and especially in the eucharist in virtue of his omnipresence  Called also ubiquitist and ubiquitary'),
(99069, 'Ubiquitariness', 'Quality or state of being ubiquitary or ubiquitous'),
(99070, 'Ubiquitary', 'Ubiquitous'),
(99071, 'Ubiquitist', 'Same as Ubiquist'),
(99072, 'Ubiquitous', 'Existing or being everywhere or in all places at the same time omnipresent'),
(99073, 'Ubiquity', 'Existence everywhere or in all places at the same time omnipresence as the ubiquity of God is not disputed by those who admit his existence'),
(99074, 'Uchees', 'A tribe of North American Indians belonging to the Creek confederation'),
(99075, 'Uckewallist', 'One of a sect of rigid Anabaptists which originated in 1637 and whose tenets were essentially the same as those of the Mennonists  In addition however they held that Judas and the murderers of Christ were saved  So called from the founder of the sect Ucke Wallis a native of Friesland'),
(99076, 'Udal', 'Vars of Odal etc property held by udal or allodial right    Obs exc in Shetland and the Orkney Islands where udal designates a freehold land held in fee simple without any charter and free of any feudal character'),
(99077, 'Udaler', 'In the Shetland and Orkney Islands one who holds property by udal or allodial right'),
(99078, 'Udder', 'The glandular organ in which milk is secreted and stored    popularly called the bag in cows and other quadrupeds See Mamma'),
(99079, 'Uddered', 'Having an udder or udders'),
(99080, 'Udderless', 'Destitute or deprived of an udder'),
(99081, 'Udometer', 'A rain gauge'),
(99082, 'Ugh', 'An exclamation expressive of disgust horror or recoil Its utterance is usually accompanied by a shudder'),
(99083, 'Uglesome', 'Ugly'),
(99084, 'Uglify', 'To disfigure to make ugly'),
(99085, 'Uglily', 'In an ugly manner with deformity'),
(99086, 'Ugliness', 'The quality or state of being ugly');
INSERT INTO `dictionary` (`id`, `word`, `definition`) VALUES
(99087, 'Ugly', 'Offensive to the sight contrary to beauty being of disagreeable or loathsome aspect unsightly repulsive deformed'),
(99088, 'Ugrian', 'A Mongolian race ancestors of the Finns'),
(99089, 'Ugsome', 'Ugly offensive loathsome'),
(99090, 'Uhlan', 'One of a certain description of militia among the Tartars'),
(99091, 'Uintatherium', 'An extinct genus of large Eocene ungulates allied to Dinoceras This name is sometimes used for nearly all the known species of the group See Dinoceras'),
(99092, 'Uitlander', 'A foreigner an outlander'),
(99093, 'Ukase', 'In Russia a published proclamation or imperial order having the force of law'),
(99094, 'Ulan', 'See Uhlan'),
(99095, 'Ularburong', 'A large East Indian nocturnal tree snake Dipsas dendrophila It is not venomous'),
(99096, 'Ulcer', 'A solution of continuity in any of the soft parts of the body discharging purulent matter found on a surface especially one of the natural surfaces of the body and originating generally in a constitutional disorder a sore discharging pus It is distinguished from an abscess which has its beginning at least in the depth of the tissues'),
(99097, 'Ulcerable', 'Capable of ulcerating'),
(99098, 'Ulcerate', 'To be formed into an ulcer to become ulcerous'),
(99099, 'Ulcerated', 'Affected with or as with an ulcer or ulcers as an ulcerated sore throat'),
(99100, 'Ulceration', 'The process of forming an ulcer or of becoming ulcerous the state of being ulcerated also an ulcer'),
(99101, 'Ulcerative', 'Of or pertaining to ulcers as an ulcerative process'),
(99102, 'Ulcered', 'Ulcerous ulcerated'),
(99103, 'Ulcerous', 'Having the nature or character of an ulcer discharging purulent or other matter'),
(99104, 'Ulcuscle', 'A little ulcer'),
(99105, 'Ule', 'A Mexican and Central American tree Castilloa elastica and Castilloa Markhamiana related to the breadfruit tree  Its milky juice contains caoutchouc  Called also ule tree'),
(99106, 'Ulema', 'A college or body composed of the hierarchy the imams or ministers of religion the muftis or doctors of law and the cadis or administrators of justice  That of Turkey alone now has political power its head is the sheik ul Islam'),
(99107, 'Ulexite', 'A mineral occurring in white rounded crystalline masses It is a hydrous borate of lime and soda'),
(99108, 'Uliginose', 'Muddy oozy slimy also growing in muddy places'),
(99109, 'ullage', 'The amount which a vessel as a cask of liquor lacks of being full wantage deficiency'),
(99110, 'ullet', 'A European owl Syrnium aluco of a tawny color    called also uluia'),
(99111, 'Ullmannite', 'A brittle mineral of a steel gray color and metallic luster containing antimony arsenic sulphur and nickel'),
(99112, 'Ulluco', 'See Melluc'),
(99113, 'Ulmaceous', 'Of or pertaining to a suborder of urticaceous plants of which the elm is the type'),
(99114, 'Ulmate', 'A salt of ulmic acid'),
(99115, 'Ulmic', 'Pertaining to ulmin designating an acid obtained from ulmin'),
(99116, 'Ulmin', 'A brown amorphous substance found in decaying vegetation  Cf Humin'),
(99117, 'Ulmus', 'A genus of trees including the elm'),
(99118, 'Ulna', 'The postaxial bone of the forearm or brachium corresponding to the fibula of the hind limb See Radius'),
(99119, 'Ulnage', 'Measurement by the ell alnage'),
(99120, 'Ulnar', 'Of or pertaining to the ulna or the elbow as the ulnar nerve'),
(99121, 'Ulnare', 'One of the bones or cartilages of the carpus which articulates with the ulna and corresponds to the cuneiform in man'),
(99122, 'Ulodendron', 'A genus of fossil trees'),
(99123, 'Uloid', 'Resembling a scar scarlike'),
(99124, 'Ulonata', 'A division of insects nearly equivalent to the true Orthoptera'),
(99125, 'Ulotrichan', 'Of or pertaining to the Ulotrichi'),
(99126, 'Ulotrichi', 'The division of mankind which embraces the races having woolly or crispy hair  Cf Leiotrichi'),
(99127, 'Ulotrichous', 'Having woolly or crispy hair    opposed to leiotrichous'),
(99128, 'Ulster', 'A long loose overcoat worn by men and women originally made of frieze from Ulster Ireland'),
(99129, 'Ulterior', 'Situated beyond or on the farther side thither    correlative with hither'),
(99130, 'Ulteriorly', 'More distantly or remotely'),
(99131, 'Ultima', 'Most remote furthest final last'),
(99132, 'Ultimate', 'Farthest most remote in space or time extreme last final'),
(99133, 'Ultimately', 'As a final consequence at last in the end as afflictions often tend to correct immoral habits and ultimately prove blessings'),
(99134, 'Ultimation', 'State of being ultimate that which is ultimate or final ultimatum'),
(99135, 'Ultimatum', 'A final proposition concession or condition especially the final propositions conditions or terms offered by either of the parties in a diplomatic negotiation the most favorable terms that a negotiator can offer the rejection of which usually puts an end to the hesitation'),
(99136, 'Ultime', 'Ultimate final'),
(99137, 'Ultimity', 'The last stage or consequence finality'),
(99138, 'Ultimo', 'In the month immediately preceding the present as on the 1st ultimo    usually abbreviated to ult  Cf Proximo'),
(99139, 'Ultion', 'The act of taking vengeance revenge'),
(99140, 'Ultra ', 'A prefix from the Latin ultra beyond see Ulterior having in composition the signification beyond on the other side chiefly when joined with words expressing relations of place as ultramarine ultramontane ultramundane ultratropical etc In other relations it has the sense of excessively exceedingly beyond what is common natural right or proper as ultraconservative ultrademocratic ultradespotic ultraliberal ultraradical etc'),
(99141, 'Ultragaseous', 'Having the properties exhibited by gases under very low pressures one millionth of an atmosphere or less Matter under this condition which has been termed the fourth state of matter is sometimes called radiant matter'),
(99142, 'Ultrage', 'Outrage'),
(99143, 'Ultraism', 'The principles of those who advocate extreme measures as radical reform and the like'),
(99144, 'Ultraist', 'One who pushes a principle or measure to extremes an extremist a radical an ultra'),
(99145, 'Ultramarine', 'Situated or being beyond the sea'),
(99146, 'Ultramontane', 'Being beyond the mountains specifically being beyond the Alps in respect to the one who speaks'),
(99147, 'Ultramontanism', 'The principles of those within the Roman Catholic Church who maintain extreme views favoring the popes supremacy    so used by those living north of the Alps in reference to the Italians    rarely used in an opposite sense as referring to the views of those living north of the Alps and opposed to the papal claims  Cf Gallicanism'),
(99148, 'Ultramontanist', 'One who upholds ultramontanism'),
(99149, 'Ultramundane', 'Being beyond the world or beyond the limits of our system'),
(99150, 'Ultrared', 'Situated beyond or below the red rays as the ultrated rays of the spectrum which are less refrangible than the red'),
(99151, 'Ultratropical', 'Situated beyond or outside of the tropics extratropical also having an excessively tropical temperature warmer than the tropics'),
(99152, 'Ultraviolet', 'Lying outside the visible spectrum at its blue violet end    said of light more refrangible i e having a shorter wavelength than the extreme violet rays of the visible spectrum  Electromagnetic radiation with wavelengths shorter than those of ultrviolet light are not usually considered as light waves but are classified differently  The ultraviolet region of the electromagnetic spectrum is generally considered as comprising those electromagnetic emissions with wavelengths lying between those of visible light and those of X rays i e between 4000 Angstroms and 100 Angstroms'),
(99153, 'Ultra vires', 'Beyond power transcending authority    a phrase used frequently in relation to acts or enactments by corporations in excess of their chartered or statutory rights'),
(99154, 'Ultrazodiacal', 'Outside the zodiac being in that part of the heavens that is more than eight degrees from the ecliptic as ultrazodiacal planets that is those planets which in part of their orbits go beyond the zodiac'),
(99155, 'Ultroneous', 'Spontaneous voluntary'),
(99156, 'Ulula', 'A genus of owls including the great gray owl Ulula cinerea of Arctic America and other similar species See Illust of Owl'),
(99157, 'Ululant', 'Howling wailing'),
(99158, 'Ululate', 'To howl as a dog or a wolf to wail as ululating jackals'),
(99159, 'Ululation', 'A howling as of a dog or wolf a wailing'),
(99160, 'Ulva', 'A genus of thin papery bright green seaweeds including the kinds called sea lettuce'),
(99161, 'Umbe', 'About'),
(99162, 'Umbecast', 'To cast about to consider to ponder'),
(99163, 'Umbel', 'A kind of flower cluster in which the flower stalks radiate from a common point as in the carrot and milkweed It is simple or compound in the latter case each peduncle bears another little umbel called umbellet or umbellule'),
(99164, 'Umbellar', 'Of or pertaining to an umbel having the form of an umbel'),
(99165, 'Umbellate', 'Bearing umbels pertaining to an umbel umbel like as umbellate plants or flowers'),
(99166, 'Umbellet', 'A small or partial umbel an umbellule'),
(99167, 'Umbellic', 'Pertaining to or obtained from certain umbelliferous plants as umbellic acid'),
(99168, 'Umbellifer', 'A plant producing an umbel or umbels'),
(99169, 'Umbelliferone', 'A tasteless white crystalline substance C9H6O3 found in the bark of a certain plant Daphne Mezereum and also obtained by the distillation of certain gums from the Umbelliferae as galbanum asafetida etc  It is analogous to coumarin  Called also hydroxy coumarin  Its strong fluorescence under ultraviolet light makes it useful in analytical biochemistry as for example to detect phosphatase activity by hydrolysis of umbelliferyl phosphate'),
(99170, 'Umbelliferous', 'Producing umbels'),
(99171, 'Umbellularia', 'A genus of deep sea alcyonaria consisting of a cluster of large flowerlike polyps situated at the summit of a long slender stem which stands upright in the mud supported by a bulbous base'),
(99172, 'Umbellule', 'An umbellet'),
(99173, 'Umber', 'A brown or reddish pigment used in both oil and water colors obtained from certain natural clays variously colored by the oxides of iron and manganese It is commonly heated or burned before being used and is then called burnt umber when not heated it is called raw umber See Burnt umber below'),
(99174, 'Umbery', 'Of or pertaining to umber like umber as umbery gold'),
(99175, 'Umbilic', 'The navel the center'),
(99176, 'Umbilical', 'Of or pertaining to an umbilicus or umbilical cord umbilic'),
(99177, 'Umbilicate', 'Depressed in the middle like a navel as a flower fruit or leaf navel shaped having an umbilicus as an umbilicated smallpox vesicle'),
(99178, 'Umbilication', 'A slight navel like depression or dimpling of the center of a rounded body as the umbilication of a smallpox vesicle also the condition of being umbilicated'),
(99179, 'Umbilicus', 'The depression or mark in the median line of the abdomen which indicates the point where the umbilical cord separated from the fetus the navel the belly button in humans'),
(99180, 'Umble pie', 'A pie made of umbles See To eat humble pie under Humble'),
(99181, 'Umbles', 'The entrails and coarser parts of a deer hence sometimes entrails in general'),
(99182, 'Umbo', 'The boss of a shield at or near the middle and usually projecting sometimes in a sharp spike'),
(99183, 'Umbonate', 'Having a conical or rounded projection or protuberance like a boss'),
(99184, 'Umbra', 'The conical shadow projected from a planet or satellite on the side opposite to the sun within which a spectator could see no portion of the suns disk    used in contradistinction from penumbra See Penumbra'),
(99185, 'Umbraculiferous', 'Bearing something like an open umbrella'),
(99186, 'Umbraculiform', 'Having the form of anything that serves to shade as a tree top an umbrella and the like specifically Bot having the form of an umbrella umbrella shaped'),
(99187, 'Umbrage', 'Shade shadow obscurity hence that which affords a shade as a screen of trees or foliage'),
(99188, 'umbrageous', 'Forming or affording a shade shady shaded as umbrageous trees or foliage'),
(99189, 'Umbrate', 'To shade to shadow to foreshadow'),
(99190, 'Umbratic', 'Of or pertaining to the shade or darkness shadowy unreal secluded retired'),
(99191, 'Umbratile', 'Umbratic'),
(99192, 'Umbratious', 'Suspicious captious disposed to take umbrage'),
(99193, 'Umbre', 'See Umber'),
(99194, 'Umbrel', 'An umbrella'),
(99195, 'Umbrella', 'A shade screen or guard carried in the hand for sheltering the person from the rays of the sun or from rain or snow  It is formed of silk cotton or other fabric extended on strips of whalebone steel or other elastic material inserted in or fastened to a rod or stick by means of pivots or hinges in such a way as to allow of being opened and closed with ease See Parasol'),
(99196, 'Umbrere', 'In ancient armor a visor or projection like the peak of a cap to which a face guard was sometimes attached This was sometimes fixed and sometimes moved freely upon the helmet and could be raised like the beaver Called also umber and umbril'),
(99197, 'Umbrette', 'See Umber 4'),
(99198, 'Umbriferous', 'Casting or making a shade umbrageous'),
(99199, 'umbril', 'An umbrere'),
(99200, 'umbrine', 'See Umbra 2'),
(99201, 'Umbrose', 'Shady umbrageous'),
(99202, 'Umbrosity', 'The quality or state of being umbrose shadiness'),
(99203, 'umhofo', 'An African two horned rhinoceros Atelodus simus or Rhinoceros simus    called also chukuru and white rhinoceros'),
(99204, 'umlaut', 'The euphonic modification of a root vowel sound by the influence of a u or especially i in the syllable which formerly followed'),
(99205, 'Umlauted', 'Having the umlaut as umlauted vowels'),
(99206, 'Umpirage', 'The office of an umpire the power right or authority of an umpire to decide'),
(99207, 'Umpire', 'To decide as umpire to arbitrate to settle as a dispute'),
(99208, 'Umpireship', 'Umpirage arbitrament'),
(99209, 'Umpress', 'Female umpire'),
(99210, 'umpteen', 'An indefinite number usu more than ten and less than one hundred a lot  Often used hyperbolically and usually expressing the notion of more than the usual number or more than I would like'),
(99211, 'umpteenth', 'Ordinal of umpteen with corresponding signification as for the umpteenth time Im telling you to stop'),
(99212, 'umquhile', 'Some time ago formerly'),
(99213, 'Un ', 'An inseparable verbal prefix or particle It is prefixed a To verbs to express the contrary and not the simple negative of the action of the verb to which it is prefixed as in unbend uncoil undo unfold b To nouns to form verbs expressing privation of the thing quality or state expressed by the noun or separation from it as in unchild unsex Sometimes particles and participial adjectives formed with this prefix coincide in form with compounds of the negative prefix un  see 2d Un  as in undone from undo meaning unfastened ruined and undone from 2d un  and done meaning not done not finished Un  is sometimes used with an intensive force merely as in unloose'),
