(103249, 'Urn shaped', 'Having the shape of an urn as the urn shaped capsules of some mosses'),
(103250, 'Uro ', 'A combining form fr Gr oyron urine'),
(103251, 'Urobilin', 'A yellow pigment identical with hydrobilirubin abundant in the highly colored urine of fever and also present in normal urine See Urochrome'),
(103252, 'Urocele', 'A morbid swelling of the scrotum due to extravasation of urine into it'),
(103253, 'Urocerata', 'A division of boring Hymenoptera including Tremex and allied genera See Illust of Horntail'),
(103254, 'Urochord', 'The central axis or cord in the tail of larval ascidians and of certain adult tunicates'),
(103255, 'Urochorda', 'Same as Tunicata'),
(103256, 'Urochordal', 'Of or pertaining to the Urochorda'),
(103257, 'Urochrome', 'A yellow urinary pigment considered by Thudichum as the only pigment present in normal urine It is regarded by Maly as identical with urobilin'),
(103258, 'Urochs', 'See Aurochs'),
(103259, 'Urocord', 'See Urochord'),
(103260, 'Urocyst', 'The urinary bladder'),
(103261, 'Urodela', 'An order of amphibians having the tail well developed and often long It comprises the salamanders tritons and allied animals'),
(103262, 'Urodele', 'One of the Urodela'),
(103263, 'Urodelian', 'Of or pertaining to the Urodela'),
(103264, 'Uroerythrin', 'A reddish urinary pigment considered as the substance which gives to the urine of rheumatism its characteristic color It also causes the red color often seen in deposits of urates'),
(103265, 'Urogastric', 'Behind the stomach    said of two lobes of the carapace of certain crustaceans'),
(103266, 'Urogenital', 'Same as Urinogenital'),
(103267, 'Uroglaucin', 'A body identical with indigo blue occasionally found in the urine in degeneration of the kidneys It is readily formed by oxidation or decomposition of indican'),
(103268, 'Urohaematin', 'Urinary haematin    applied to the normal coloring matter of the urine on the supposition that it is formed either directly or indirectly through bilirubin from the haematin of the blood See Urochrome and Urobilin'),
(103269, 'Urohyal', 'Of or pertaining to one or more median and posterior elements in the hyoidean arch of fishes'),
(103270, 'Urology', 'See Uronology'),
(103271, 'Uromere', 'Any one of the abdominal segments of an arthropod'),
(103272, 'Uronology', 'That part of medicine which treats of urine'),
(103273, 'Uropod', 'Any one of the abdominal appendages of a crustacean especially one of the posterior ones which are often larger than the rest and different in structure and are used chiefly in locomotion See Illust of Crustacea and Stomapoda'),
(103274, 'Uropodal', 'Of or pertaining to a uropod'),
(103275, 'Uropygial', 'Of or pertaining to the uropygium or prominence at the base of the tail feathers in birds'),
(103276, 'Uropygium', 'The prominence at the posterior extremity of a birds body which supports the feathers of the tail the rump    sometimes called popes nose'),
(103277, 'Urosacral', 'Of or pertaining to both the caudal and sacral parts of the vertebral column as the urosacral vertebrae of birds'),
(103278, 'Uroscopy', 'The diagnosis of diseases by inspection of urine'),
(103279, 'Urosome', 'The abdomen or post abdomen of arthropods'),
(103280, 'Urostege', 'One of the plates on the under side of the tail of a serpent'),
(103281, 'Urosteon', 'A median ossification back of the lophosteon in the sternum of some birds'),
(103282, 'Urosternite', 'The sternal or under piece of any one of the uromeres of insects and other arthropods'),
(103283, 'Urostyle', 'A styliform process forming the posterior extremity of the vertebral column in some fishes and amphibians'),
(103284, 'Urox', 'The aurochs'),
(103285, 'Uroxanate', 'A salt of uroxanic acid'),
(103286, 'Uroxanic', 'Pertaining to or designating an acid C5H8N4O6 which is obtained as a white crystalline substance by the slow oxidation of uric acid in alkaline solution'),
(103287, 'Uroxanthin', 'Same as Indican'),
(103288, 'Urrhodin', 'Indigo red a product of the decomposition or oxidation of indican It is sometimes found in the sediment of pathological urines It is soluble in ether or alcohol giving the solution a beautiful red color Also called indigrubin'),
(103289, 'Urry', 'A sort of blue or black clay lying near a vein of coal'),
(103290, 'Ursa', 'Either one of the Bears See the Phrases below'),
(103291, 'Ursal', 'The ursine seal See the Note under 1st Seal'),
(103292, 'Ursiform', 'Having the shape of a bear'),
(103293, 'Ursine', 'Of or pertaining to a bear resembling a bear'),
(103294, 'Urson', 'The Canada porcupine See Porcupine'),
(103295, 'Ursuk', 'The bearded seal'),
(103296, 'Ursula', 'A beautiful North American butterfly Basilarchia astyanax syn Limenitis astyanax  Its wings are nearly black with red and blue spots and blotches  Called also red spotted purple'),
(103297, 'Ursuline', 'One of an order of nuns founded by St Angela Merici at Brescia in Italy about the year 1537 and so called from St Ursula under whose protection it was placed The order was introduced into Canada as early as 1639 and into the United States in 1727 The members are devoted entirely to education'),
(103298, 'Ursus', 'A genus of Carnivora including the common bears'),
(103299, 'Urtica', 'A genus of plants including the common nettles See Nettle n'),
(103300, 'Urticaceous', 'Of or pertaining to a natural order Urticaceae of plants of which the nettle is the type The order includes also the hop the elm the mulberry the fig and many other plants'),
(103301, 'Urtical', 'Resembling nettles    said of several natural orders allied to urticaceous plants'),
(103302, 'Urticaria', 'The nettle rash a disease characterized by a transient eruption of red pimples and of wheals accompanied with a burning or stinging sensation and with itching uredo'),
(103303, 'Urticate', 'To sting with or as with nettles to irritate to annoy'),
(103304, 'Urtication', 'The act or process of whipping or stinging with nettles    sometimes used in the treatment of paralysis'),
(103305, 'Urubu', 'The black vulture Catharista atrata It ranges from the Southern United States to South America See Vulture'),
(103306, 'Urus', 'A very large powerful and savage extinct bovine animal Bos urus or Bos primigenius anciently abundant in Europe  It appears to have still existed in the time of Julius Caesar  It had very large horns and was hardly capable of domestication  Called also ur ure and tur'),
(103307, 'Urva', 'The crab eating ichneumon Herpestes urva native of India The fur is black annulated with white at the tip of each hair and a white streak extends from the mouth to the shoulder'),
(103308, 'Us', 'The persons speaking regarded as an object ourselves    the objective case of we See We'),
(103309, 'Usable', 'Capable of being used'),
(103310, 'Usager', 'One who has the use of anything in trust for another'),
(103311, 'Usant', 'Using accustomed'),
(103312, 'Usbegs', 'A Turkish tribe which about the close of the 15th century conquered and settled in that part of Asia now called Turkestan'),
(103313, 'Use', 'To be wont or accustomed to be in the habit or practice as he used to ride daily    now disused in the present tense perhaps because of the similarity in sound between '),
(103314, 'Useful', 'Full of use advantage or profit producing or having power to produce good serviceable for any end or object helpful toward advancing any purpose beneficial profitable advantageous as vessels and instruments useful in a family books useful for improvement useful knowledge useful arts'),
(103315, 'Usefully', 'In a useful manner'),
(103316, 'Usefulness', 'The quality or state of being useful utility serviceableness advantage'),
(103317, 'Useless', 'Having or being of no use unserviceable producing no good end answering no valuable purpose not advancing the end proposed unprofitable ineffectual as a useless garment useless pity'),
(103318, 'User', 'One who uses'),
(103319, 'U shaped', 'Having the form of the letter U'),
(103320, 'Usher', 'To introduce or escort as an usher forerunner or harbinger to forerun    sometimes followed by in or forth as to usher in a stranger to usher forth the guests to usher a visitor into the room'),
(103321, 'Usherance', 'The act of ushering or the state of being ushered in'),
(103322, 'Usherdom', 'The office or position of an usher ushership also ushers collectively'),
(103323, 'Usherless', 'Destitute of an usher'),
(103324, 'Ushership', 'The office of an usher usherdom'),
(103325, 'Usitative', 'Denoting usual or customary action'),
(103326, 'Usnea', 'A genus of lichens most of the species of which have long gray pendulous and finely branched fronds Usnea barbata is the common bearded lichen which grows on branches of trees in northern forests'),
(103327, 'Usnic', 'Pertaining to or designating a complex acid obtained as a yellow crystalline substance from certain genera of lichens  Usnea Parmelia etc'),
(103328, 'Usself', 'Ourselves'),
(103329, 'Ustion', 'The act of burning or the state of being burned'),
(103330, 'Ustorious', 'Having the quality of burning'),
(103331, 'Ustulate', 'Blackened as if burned'),
(103332, 'Usual', 'Such as is in common use such as occurs in ordinary practice or in the ordinary course of events customary ordinary habitual common'),
(103333, 'Usucaption', 'The acquisition of the title or right to property by the uninterrupted possession of it for a certain term prescribed by law    the same as prescription in common law'),
(103334, 'Usufruct', 'The right of using and enjoying the profits of an estate or other thing belonging to another without impairing the substance'),
(103335, 'Usufructuary', 'A person who has the use of property and reaps the profits of it'),
(103336, 'Usurarious', 'Usurious'),
(103337, 'Usure', 'To practice usury to charge unlawful interest'),
(103338, 'Usurp', 'To seize and hold in possession by force or without right as to usurp a throne to usurp the prerogatives of the crown to usurp power to usurp the right of a patron is to oust or dispossess him'),
(103339, 'Usurpant', 'Usurping encroaching'),
(103340, 'Usurpatory', 'Marked by usurpation usurping'),
(103341, 'Usurpature', 'Usurpation'),
(103342, 'Usurper', 'One who usurps especially one who seizes illegally on sovereign power as the usurper of a throne of power or of the rights of a patron'),
(103343, 'Usurpingly', 'In a usurping manner'),
(103344, 'Ut', 'The first note in Guidos musical scale now usually superseded by do See Solmization'),
(103345, 'Utensil', 'That which is used an instrument an implement especially an instrument or vessel used in a kitchen or in domestic and farming business'),
(103346, 'Uterogestation', 'Gestation in the womb from conception to birth pregnancy'),
(103347, 'Uterovaginal', 'Pertaining to both the uterus and the vagina'),
(103348, 'Uterus', 'The organ of a female mammal in which the young are developed previous to birth the womb'),
(103349, 'Utes', 'An extensive tribe of North American Indians of the Shoshone stock inhabiting Colorado Utah New Mexico Arizona and adjacent regions They are subdivided into several subordinate tribes some of which are among the most degraded of North American Indians'),
(103350, 'Utia', 'Any species of large West Indian rodents of the genus Capromys or Utia In general appearance and habits they resemble rats but they are as large as rabbits'),
(103351, 'Utica', 'Of pertaining to or designating a subdivision of the Trenton Period of the Lower Silurian characterized in the State of New York by beds of shale'),
(103352, 'Utile', 'Profitable useful'),
(103353, 'Utilitarian', 'One who holds the doctrine of utilitarianism'),
(103354, 'Utilitarianism', 'The doctrine that the greatest happiness of the greatest number should be the end and aim of all social and political institutions'),
(103355, 'Utilizable', 'Capable of being utilized as the utilizable products of the gas works'),
(103356, 'Utilization', 'The act of utilizing or the state of being utilized'),
(103357, 'Utilize', 'To make useful to turn to profitable account or use to make use of as to utilize the whole power of a machine to utilize ones opportunities'),
(103358, 'Utis', 'See Utas'),
(103359, 'Utlary', 'Outlawry'),
(103360, 'Utmost', 'The most that can be the farthest limit the greatest power degree or effort as he has done his utmost try your utmost'),
(103361, 'Utopian', 'Of or pertaining to Utopia resembling Utopia hence ideal chimerical fanciful founded upon or involving imaginary perfections as Utopian projects Utopian happiness'),
(103362, 'Utopianism', 'The ideas views aims etc of a Utopian impracticable schemes of human perfection optimism'),
(103363, 'Utopianist', 'An Utopian an optimist'),
(103364, 'Utopical', 'Utopian ideal'),
(103365, 'Utopist', 'A Utopian'),
(103366, 'Utraquist', 'One who receives the eucharist in both kinds esp one of a body of Hussites who in the 15th century fought for the right to do this Called also Calixtines'),
(103367, 'Utricularia', 'A genus of aquatic flowering plants in which the submersed leaves bear many little utricles or ascidia See Ascidium'),
(103368, 'Utriculate', 'Resembling a bladder swollen like a bladder inflated utricular'),
(103369, 'Utriculoid', 'Resembling a bladder utricular utriculate'),
(103370, 'Utriculus', 'A little sac or bag a utricle especially a part of the membranous labyrinth of the ear See the Note under Ear');
INSERT INTO `dictionary` (`id`, `word`, `definition`) VALUES
(103371, 'Utro ', 'A combining form used in anatomy to indicate connection with or relation to the uterus as in utro ovarian'),
(103372, 'Utterable', 'Capable of being uttered'),
(103373, 'Utterance', 'The act of uttering'),
(103374, 'Utterer', 'One who utters'),
(103375, 'Utterest', 'Uttermost'),
(103376, 'Utterless', 'Incapable of being uttered'),
(103377, 'Utterly', 'In an utter manner to the full extent fully totally as utterly ruined it is utterly vain'),
(103378, 'Uttermore', 'Further outer utter'),
(103379, 'Uttermost', 'Extreme utmost being in the farthest greatest or highest degree as the uttermost extent or end'),
(103380, 'Utterness', 'The quality or state of being utter or extreme extremity utmost uttermost'),
(103381, 'Uva', 'A small pulpy or juicy fruit containing several seeds and having a thin skin as a grape'),
(103382, 'Uvate', 'A conserve made of grapes'),
(103383, 'Uva ursi', 'The bearberry'),
(103384, 'Uvea', 'The posterior pigmented layer of the iris    sometimes applied to the whole iris together with the choroid coat'),
(103385, 'Uveous', 'Resembling a grape'),
(103386, 'Uvic', 'Pertaining to or obtained from grapes specifically designating an organic acid C7H8O3 also called pyrotritartaric acid obtained as a white crystalline substance by the decomposition of tartaric and pyrotartaric acids'),
(103387, 'Uvitic', 'Pertaining to or designating an acid CH3C6H3CO2H2 obtained as a white crystalline substance by the partial oxidation of mesitylene    called also mesitic acid'),
(103388, 'Uvitonic', 'Pertaining to or designating an acid which is obtained as a white crystalline substance by the action of ammonia on pyrotartaric acid'),
(103389, 'Uvrou', 'See Euphroe'),
(103390, 'Uvula', 'The pendent fleshy lobe in the middle of the posterior border of the soft palate'),
(103391, 'Uvular', 'Of or pertaining to a uvula'),
(103392, 'Uvulatome', 'An instrument for removing the uvula'),
(103393, 'Uvulatomy', 'The operation of removing the uvula'),
(103394, 'Uvulitis', 'Inflammation of the uvula'),
(103395, 'Uwarowite', 'Ouvarovite'),
(103396, 'Uxorial', 'Dotingly fond of or servilely submissive to a wife uxorious also becoming a wife pertaining to a wife'),
(103397, 'Uxoricidal', 'Of or pertaining to uxoricide tending to uxoricide'),
(103398, 'Uxorious', 'Excessively fond of or submissive to a wife being a dependent husband'),
(103399, 'Uzema', 'A Burman measure of twelve miles'),
(103400, 'V', 'V the twenty second letter of the English alphabet is a vocal consonant V and U are only varieties of the same character U being the cursive form while V is better adapted for engraving as in stone The two letters were formerly used indiscriminately and till a comparatively recent date words containing them were often classed together in dictionaries and other books of reference see U The letter V is from the Latin alphabet where it was used both as a consonant about like English w and as a vowel The Latin derives it from a form V of the Greek vowel '),
(103401, 'Vaagmer', 'The dealfish'),
(103402, 'Vacantly', 'In a vacant manner inanely'),
(103403, 'Vacate', 'To make vacant to leave empty to cease from filling or occupying as it was resolved by Parliament that James had vacated the throne of England the tenant vacated the house'),
(103404, 'Vacatur', 'An order of court by which a proceeding is set aside or annulled'),
(103405, 'Vaccary', 'A cow house dairy house or cow pasture'),
(103406, 'Vaccina', 'Vaccinia'),
(103407, 'Vaccinal', 'Of or pertaining to vaccinia or vaccination'),
(103408, 'Vaccinate', 'To inoculate with the cowpox by means of a virus called vaccine taken either directly or indirectly from cows now generally to administer by injection or otherwise any vaccine with the objective of rendering the recipient immune to an infectious disease  One who has been thus immunized by vaccination is said to be vaccinated against a particular disease  One may be thus immunized vaccinated also by oral ingestion or inhalation of a vaccine'),
(103409, 'Vaccination', 'The act art or practice of vaccinating or inoculating with the cowpox in order to prevent or mitigate an attack of smallpox  Cf Inoculation'),
(103410, 'Vaccinator', 'One who or that which vaccinates'),
(103411, 'Vaccine', 'Of or pertaining to cows pertaining to derived from or caused by vaccinia as vaccine virus the vaccine disease'),
(103412, 'Vaccine point', 'See Point n 26'),
(103413, 'Vaccinia', 'Cowpox vaccina See Cowpox'),
(103414, 'Vaccinist', 'A vaccinator'),
(103415, 'Vaccinium', 'A genus of ericaceous shrubs including the various kinds of blueberries and the true cranberries'),
(103416, 'Vacher', 'A keeper of stock or cattle a herdsman'),
(103417, 'Vachery', 'An inclosure for cows'),
(103418, 'Vachette clasp', 'A piece of strong steel wire with the ends curved and pointed used on toe or quarter cracks to bind the edges together and prevent motion It is clasped into two notches one on each side of the crack burned into the wall with a cautery iron'),
(103419, 'Vacillancy', 'The quality or state of being vacillant or wavering'),
(103420, 'Vacillant', 'Vacillating wavering fluctuating irresolute'),
(103421, 'Vacillating', 'Inclined to fluctuate wavering'),
(103422, 'Vacillation', 'The act of vacillating a moving one way and the other a wavering'),
(103423, 'Vacillatory', 'Inclined to vacillate wavering irresolute'),
(103424, 'Vacuate', 'To make void or empty'),
(103425, 'Vacuation', 'The act of emptying evacuation'),
(103426, 'Vacuist', 'One who holds the doctrine that the space between the bodies of the universe or the molecules and atoms of matter is a vacuum    opposed to plenist'),
(103427, 'Vacuity', 'The quality or state of being vacuous or not filled emptiness vacancy as vacuity of mind vacuity of countenance'),
(103428, 'Vacuna', 'The goddess of rural leisure to whom the husbandmen sacrificed at the close of the harvest  She was especially honored by the Sabines'),
(103429, 'Vacuolated', 'Full of vacuoles or small air cavities as vacuolated cells'),
(103430, 'Vacuolation', 'Formation into or multiplication of vacuoles'),
(103431, 'Vacuole', 'A small air cell or globular space in the interior of organic cells either containing air or a pellucid watery liquid or some special chemical secretions of the cell protoplasm'),
(103432, 'Vacuometer', 'An instrument for the comparison of barometers'),
(103433, 'Vacuous', 'Empty unfilled void vacant'),
(103434, 'Vacuousness', 'The quality or state of being vacuous emptiness vacuity'),
(103435, 'Vacuum', 'A space entirely devoid of matter called also by way of distinction absolute vacuum hence in a more general sense a space as the interior of a closed vessel which has been exhausted to a high or the highest degree by an air pump or other artificial means as water boils at a reduced temperature in a vacuum'),
(103436, 'Vacuum cleaner', 'A machine for cleaning carpets tapestry upholstered work etc by suction    sometimes called a vacuum'),
(103437, 'Vadantes', 'An extensive artificial group of birds including the wading swimming and cursorial birds'),
(103438, 'Vade', 'To fade hence to vanish'),
(103439, 'Vade mecum', 'A book or other thing that a person carries with him as a constant companion a manual a handbook'),
(103440, 'Vadimony', 'A bond or pledge for appearance before a judge on a certain day'),
(103441, 'Vadium', 'Pledge security bail See Mortgage'),
(103442, 'Vae', 'See Voe'),
(103443, 'Vafrous', 'Crafty cunning sly as vafrous tricks'),
(103444, 'Vagabond', 'Moving from place to place without a settled habitation wandering'),
(103445, 'Vagabondage', 'The condition of a vagabond a state or habit of wandering about in idleness vagrancy'),
(103446, 'Vagabondism', 'Vagabondage'),
(103447, 'Vagabondize', 'To play the vagabond to wander about in idleness'),
(103448, 'Vagabondry', 'Vagabondage'),
(103449, 'Vagal', 'Of or pertaining to the vagus or pneumogastric nerves pneumogastric'),
(103450, 'Vagancy', 'A wandering vagrancy'),
(103451, 'Vagantes', 'A tribe of spiders comprising some of those which take their prey in a web but which also frequently run with agility and chase and seize their prey'),
(103452, 'Vagarious', 'Given to or characterized by vagaries capricious whimsical crochety'),
(103453, 'Vagient', 'Crying like a child'),
(103454, 'Vaginant', 'Serving to invest or sheathe sheathing'),
(103455, 'Vaginate', 'Invested with or as if with a sheath as a vaginate stem or one invested by the tubular base of a leaf'),
(103456, 'Vaginati', 'A tribe of birds comprising the sheathbills'),
(103457, 'Vaginervose', 'Having the nerves or veins placed in apparent disorder'),
(103458, 'Vaginicola', 'A genus of Infusoria which form minute vaselike or tubular cases in which they dwell'),
(103459, 'Vaginismus', 'A painful spasmodic contraction of the vagina often rendering copulation impossible'),
(103460, 'Vaginitis', 'Inflammation of the vagina or the genital canal usually of its mucous lining membrane'),
(103461, 'Vaginopennous', 'Having elytra sheath winged'),
(103462, 'Vaginula', 'A little sheath as that about the base of the pedicel of most mosses'),
(103463, 'Vaginule', 'A vaginula'),
(103464, 'Vagissate', 'To caper or frolic'),
(103465, 'Vagous', 'Wandering unsettled'),
(103466, 'Vagrancy', 'The quality or state of being a vagrant a wandering without a settled home an unsettled condition vagabondism'),
(103467, 'Vagrant', 'One who strolls from place to place one who has no settled habitation an idle wanderer a sturdy beggar an incorrigible rogue a vagabond'),
(103468, 'Vagrantly', 'In a vagrant manner'),
(103469, 'Vagrantness', 'State of being vagrant vagrancy'),
(103470, 'Vague', 'An indefinite expanse'),
(103471, 'Vaguely', 'In a vague manner'),
(103472, 'Vagueness', 'The quality or state of being vague'),
(103473, 'Vagus', 'Wandering    applied especially to the pneumogastric nerve'),
(103474, 'Vail', 'Same as Veil'),
(103475, 'Vailer', 'One who vails'),
(103476, 'Vaimure', 'An outer or exterior wall  See Vauntmure'),
(103477, 'Vain', 'Having no real substance value or importance empty void worthless unsatisfying'),
(103478, 'Vainglorious', 'Feeling or indicating vainglory elated by vanity boastful'),
(103479, 'Vainglory', 'Excessive vanity excited by ones own performances empty pride undue elation of mind vain show boastfulness'),
(103480, 'Vainly', 'In a vain manner in vain'),
(103481, 'Vainness', 'The quality or state of being vain'),
(103482, 'Vair', 'The skin of the squirrel much used in the fourteenth century as fur for garments and frequently mentioned by writers of that period in describing the costly dresses of kings nobles and prelates  It is represented in heraldry by a series of small shields placed close together and alternately white and blue'),
(103483, 'Vairy', 'Charged with vair variegated with shield shaped figures See Vair'),
(103484, 'Vaishnava', 'A worshiper of the god Vishnu in any of his incarnations'),
(103485, 'Vaishnavism', 'The worship of Vishnu'),
(103486, 'Vaisya', 'The third of the four great original castes among the Hindus now either extinct or partially represented by the mercantile class of Banyas See the Note under Caste 1'),
(103487, 'Vaivode', 'See Waywode'),
(103488, 'Vakeel', 'A native attorney or agent also an ambassador'),
(103489, 'Valance', 'To furnish with a valance to decorate with hangings or drapery'),
(103490, 'Vale', 'A tract of low ground or of land between hills a valley'),
(103491, 'Valediction', 'A farewell a bidding farewell'),
(103492, 'Valedictorian', 'One who pronounces a valedictory address especially in American colleges the student who pronounces the valedictory of the graduating class at the annual commencement usually the student who ranks first in scholarship'),
(103493, 'Valedictory', 'Bidding farewell suitable or designed for an occasion of leave taking as a valedictory oration'),
(103494, 'Valence', 'The degree of combining power of an atom or radical as shown by the number of atoms of hydrogen or of other monads as chlorine sodium etc with which it will combine or for which it can be substituted or with which it can be compared thus an atom of hydrogen is a monad and has a valence of one the atoms of oxygen nitrogen and carbon are respectively dyads triads and tetrads and have a valence respectively of two three and four'),
(103495, 'Valencia', 'A kind of woven fabric for waistcoats having the weft of wool and the warp of silk or cotton'),
(103496, 'Valenciennes lace', 'A rich kind of lace made at Valenciennes in France Each piece is made throughout ground and pattern by the same person and with the same thread the pattern being worked in the net'),
(103497, 'Valency', 'See Valence'),
(103498, 'Valentia', 'See Valencia'),
(103499, 'Valentine', 'A sweetheart chosen on St Valentines Day'),
(103500, 'Valentinian', 'One of a school of Judaizing Gnostics in the second century    so called from Valentinus the founder'),
(103501, 'Valeramide', 'The acid amide derivative of valeric acid obtained as a white crystalline substance'),
(103502, 'Valerate', 'A salt of valeric acid'),
(103503, 'Valerian', 'Any plant of the genus Valeriana The root of the officinal valerian Valeriana officinalis has a strong smell and is much used in medicine as an antispasmodic'),
(103504, 'Valerianaceous', 'Of pertaining to or resembling plants of a natural order Valerianaceae of which the valerian is the type The order includes also the corn salads and the oriental spikenard'),
(103505, 'Valerianate', 'A valerate'),
(103506, 'Valerianic', 'Pertaining to or obtained from valerian root specifically designating an acid which is usually called valeric acid'),
(103507, 'Valeric', 'Valerianic specifically designating any one of three metameric acids of which the typical one called also inactive valeric acid C4H9CO2H is obtained from valerian root and other sources as a corrosive mobile oily liquid having a strong acid taste and an odor of old cheese'),
(103508, 'Valeridine', 'A base C10H19N produced by heating valeric aldehyde with ammonia  It is probably related to the conine alkaloids'),
