(104100, 'Verners law', 'A statement propounded by the Danish philologist Karl Verner in 1875 which explains certain apparent exceptions to Grimms law by the original position of the accent  Primitive Indo European k t p became first in Teutonic h th f and appear without further change in old Teutonic if the accent rested on the preceding syllable but these sounds became voiced and produced g d b if the accent was originally on a different syllable Similarly s either remained unchanged or it became z and later r Example Skt sapt'),
