(105432, 'Waterage', 'Money paid for transportation of goods etc by water'),
(105433, 'Water agrimony', 'A kind of bur marigold Bidens tripartita found in wet places in Europe'),
(105434, 'Water aloe', 'See Water soldier'),
(105435, 'Water antelope', 'See Water buck'),
(105436, 'Water arum', 'An aroid herb Calla palustris having a white spathe It is an inhabitant of the north temperate zone'),
(105437, 'Water back', 'See under 1st Back'),
(105438, 'Water bailiff', 'An officer of the customs whose duty it is to search vessels'),
(105439, 'Water ballast', 'Water confined in specially constructed compartments in a vessels hold to serve as ballast'),
(105440, 'Water barometer', 'A barometer in which the changes of atmospheric pressure are indicated by the motion of a column of water instead of mercury It requires a column of water about thirty three feet in height'),
(105441, 'Water bath', 'A device for regulating the temperature of anything subjected to heat by surrounding the vessel containing it with another vessel containing water which can be kept at a desired temperature also a vessel designed for this purpose'),
(105442, 'Water battery', 'A voltaic battery in which the exciting fluid is water'),
(105443, 'Water bear', 'Any species of Tardigrada 2  See Illust of Tardigrada'),
(105444, 'Water bearer', 'The constellation Aquarius'),
(105445, 'Water bed', 'A kind of mattress made of or covered with waterproof fabric and filled with water thus providing uniform support to the body lying on it    also a bed constructed so as to contain such a mattress It is used in hospitals for bedridden patients as well as in some private homes'),
(105446, 'Water beech', 'The American hornbeam  See Hornbeam'),
(105447, 'Water beetle', 'Any one of numerous species of aquatic beetles belonging to Dytiscus and allied genera of the family Dytiscidae and to various genera of the family Hydrophilidae  These beetles swim with great agility the fringed hind legs acting together like oars'),
(105448, 'Water bellows', 'Same as Tromp'),
(105449, 'Water bird', 'Any aquatic bird a water fowl'),
(105450, 'Water blackbird', 'The European water ousel or dipper'),
(105451, 'Waterboard', 'A board set up to windward in a boat to keep out water'),
(105452, 'Water boatman', 'A boat bug'),
(105453, 'Waterbok', 'A water buck'),
(105454, 'Water bound', 'Prevented by a flood from proceeding'),
(105455, 'Water brain', 'A disease of sheep gid'),
(105456, 'Water brash', 'See under Brash'),
(105457, 'Water breather', 'Any arthropod that breathes by means of gills'),
(105458, 'Water bridge', 'See Water table'),
(105459, 'Water buck', 'A large heavy antelope Kobus ellipsiprymnus native of Central Africa It frequents the banks of rivers and is a good swimmer It has a white ring around the rump Called also photomok water antelope and waterbok'),
(105460, 'Water buffalo', 'The European buffalo'),
(105461, 'Water bug', 'The Croton bug'),
(105462, 'Water butt', 'A large open headed cask set up on end to contain water'),
(105463, 'Water caltrop', 'The water chestnut'),
(105464, 'Water can', 'Any one of several species of Nuphar the yellow frog lily    so called from the shape of the seed vessel  See Nuphar and cf Candock'),
(105465, 'Water canker', 'See Canker n 1'),
(105466, 'Water carriage', 'Transportation or conveyance by water means of transporting by water'),
(105467, 'Water cart', 'A cart carrying water esp one carrying water for sale or for sprinkling streets gardens etc'),
(105468, 'Water cavy', 'The capybara'),
(105469, 'Water celery', 'A very acrid herb Ranunculus sceleratus growing in ditches and wet places    called also cursed crowfoot'),
(105470, 'Water cell', 'A cell containing water specifically Zool one of the cells or chambers in which water is stored up in the stomach of a camel'),
(105471, 'Water cement', 'Hydraulic cement'),
(105472, 'Water chestnut', 'The fruit of Trapa natans and Trapa bicornis Old World water plants bearing edible nutlike fruits armed with several hard and sharp points also the plant itself    called also water caltrop'),
(105473, 'Water chevrotain', 'A large West African chevrotain Hyaemoschus aquaticus It has a larger body and shorter legs than the other allied species Called also water deerlet'),
(105474, 'Water chicken', 'The common American gallinule'),
(105475, 'Water chickweed', 'A small annual plant Montia fontana growing in wet places in southern regions'),
(105476, 'Water chinquapin', 'The American lotus and its edible seeds which somewhat resemble chinquapins   Cf Yoncopin'),
(105477, 'Water clock', 'An instrument or machine serving to measure time by the fall or flow of a certain quantity of water a clepsydra'),
(105478, 'Water closet', 'A privy especially a privy furnished with a contrivance for introducing a stream of water to cleanse it'),
(105479, 'Water cock', 'A large gallinule Gallicrex cristatus native of Australia India and the East Indies In the breeding season the male is black and has a fleshy red caruncle or horn on the top of its head Called also kora'),
(105480, 'Water color', 'A color ground with water and gum or other glutinous medium a color the vehicle of which is water    so called in distinction from oil color'),
(105481, 'Water colorist', 'One who paints in water colors'),
(105482, 'Watercourse', 'One of the holes in floor or other plates to permit water to flow through'),
(105483, 'Water course', 'A stream of water a river or brook'),
(105484, 'Water craft', 'Any vessel or boat plying on water vessels and boats collectively'),
(105485, 'Water crake', 'The dipper'),
(105486, 'Water crane', 'A goose neck apparatus for supplying water from an elevated tank as to the tender of a locomotive'),
(105487, 'Water cress', 'A perennial cruciferous herb Nasturtium officinale growing usually in clear running or spring water The leaves are pungent and used for salad and as an antiscorbutic'),
(105488, 'Water crow', 'The dipper'),
(105489, 'Water crowfoot', 'An aquatic kind of buttercup Ranunculus aquatilis used as food for cattle in parts of England'),
(105490, 'Water cure', 'Hydropathy'),
(105491, 'Water deck', 'A covering of painting canvas for the equipments of a dragoons horse'),
(105492, 'Water deer', 'A small Chinese deer Hydropotes inermis Both sexes are destitute of antlers but the male has large descending canine tusks'),
(105493, 'Water deerlet', 'See Water chevrotain'),
(105494, 'Water devil', 'The rapacious larva of a large water beetle Hydrophilus piceus and of other similar species  See Illust of Water beetle'),
(105495, 'Water dock', 'A tall coarse dock growing in wet places The American water dock is Rumex orbiculatus the European is Rumex Hydrolapathum'),
(105496, 'Water doctor', 'One who professes to be able to divine diseases by inspection of the urine'),
(105497, 'Water dog', 'A dog accustomed to the water or trained to retrieve waterfowl Retrievers waters spaniels and Newfoundland dogs are so trained'),
(105498, 'Water drain', 'A drain or channel for draining off water'),
(105499, 'Water drainage', 'The draining off of water'),
(105500, 'Water dressing', 'The treatment of wounds or ulcers by the application of water also a dressing saturated with water only for application to a wound or an ulcer'),
(105501, 'Water dropwort', 'A European poisonous umbelliferous plant Enanthe fistulosa with large hollow stems and finely divided leaves'),
(105502, 'Water eagle', 'The osprey'),
(105503, 'Water elder', 'The guelder rose'),
(105504, 'Water elephant', 'The hippopotamus'),
(105505, 'Water engine', 'An engine to raise water or an engine moved by water also an engine or machine for extinguishing fires a fire engine'),
(105506, 'Waterer', 'One who or that which waters'),
(105507, 'Waterfall', 'A fall or perpendicular descent of the water of a river or stream or a descent nearly perpendicular a cascade a cataract'),
(105508, 'Water feather', 'The water violet Hottonia palustris also the less showy American plant Hottonia inflata'),
(105509, 'Water flag', 'A European species of Iris Iris Pseudacorus having bright yellow flowers'),
(105510, 'Water flannel', 'A floating mass formed in pools by the entangled filaments of a European fresh water alga Cladophora crispata'),
(105511, 'Water flea', 'Any one of numerous species of small aquatic Entomostraca belonging to the genera Cyclops Daphnia etc    so called because they swim with sudden leaps or starts'),
(105512, 'Waterflood', 'A flood of water an inundation'),
(105513, 'Water flounder', 'The windowpane Pleuronectes maculatus'),
(105514, 'Waterfowl', 'Any bird that frequents the water or lives about rivers lakes etc or on or near the sea an aquatic fowl    used also collectively'),
(105515, 'Water fox', 'The carp    so called on account of its cunning'),
(105516, 'Water frame', 'A name given to the first power spinning machine because driven by water power'),
(105517, 'Water furrow', 'A deep furrow for conducting water from the ground and keeping the surface soil dry'),
(105518, 'Water gage', 'See Water gauge'),
(105519, 'Water gall', 'A cavity made in the earth by a torrent of water a washout'),
(105520, 'Water gang', 'A passage for water such as was usually made in a sea wall to drain water out of marshes'),
(105521, 'Water gas', 'See under Gas'),
(105522, 'Water gate', 'A gate or valve by which a flow of water is permitted prevented or regulated'),
(105523, 'Water gavel', 'A gavel or rent paid for a privilege as of fishing in some river or water'),
(105524, 'Water germander', 'A labiate plant Teucrium Scordium found in marshy places in Europe'),
(105525, 'Water gilding', 'The act or the process of gilding metallic surfaces by covering them with a thin coating of amalgam of gold and then volatilizing the mercury by heat    called also wash gilding'),
(105526, 'Water glass', 'See Soluble glass under Glass'),
(105527, 'Water god', 'A fabulous deity supposed to dwell in and preside over some body of water'),
(105528, 'Water grass', 'A tall march perennial grass Paspalum dilatatum of the southern United States and the American tropics'),
(105529, 'Water gruel', 'A liquid food composed of water and a small portion of meal or other farinaceous substance boiled and seasoned'),
(105530, 'Water hammer', 'A vessel partly filled with water exhausted of air and hermetically sealed When reversed or shaken the water being unimpeded by air strikes the sides in solid mass with a sound like that of a hammer'),
(105531, 'Water hare', 'A small American hare or rabbit Lepus aquaticus found on or near the southern coasts of the United States    called also water rabbit and swamp hare'),
(105532, 'Water hemlock', 'A poisonous umbelliferous plant Cicuta virosa of Europe also any one of several plants of that genus'),
(105533, 'Water hemp', 'See under Hemp'),
(105534, 'Water hen', 'Any gallinule'),
(105535, 'Water hog', 'The capybara'),
(105536, 'Water horehound', 'Bugleweed'),
(105537, 'Waterhorse', 'A pile of salted fish heaped up to drain'),
(105538, 'Water hyacinth', 'Either of several tropical aquatic plants of the genus Eichhornia related to the pickerel weed'),
(105539, 'Water ice', 'Water flavored sweetened and frozen to be eaten as a confection'),
(105540, 'Waterie', 'The pied wagtail    so called because it frequents ponds'),
(105541, 'Water inch', 'Same as Inch of water under Water'),
(105542, 'Wateriness', 'The quality or state of being watery moisture humidity'),
(105543, 'Watering', 'a  n from Water v'),
(105544, 'Waterish', 'Resembling water thin watery'),
(105545, 'Waterishness', 'The quality of being waterish'),
(105546, 'Water joint', 'A joint in a stone pavement where the stones are left slightly higher than elsewhere the rest of the surface being sunken or dished The raised surface is intended to prevent the settling of water in the joints'),
(105547, 'Water junket', 'The common sandpiper'),
(105548, 'Water laid', 'Having a left hand twist    said of cordage as a water laid or left hand rope'),
(105549, 'Waterlander', 'One of a body of Dutch Anabaptists who separated from the Mennonites in the sixteenth century    so called from a district in North Holland denominated Waterland'),
(105550, 'Water laverock', 'The common sandpiper'),
(105551, 'Waterleaf', 'Any plant of the American genus Hydrophyllum herbs having white or pale blue bell shaped flowers'),
(105552, 'Water leg', 'See Leg 7'),
(105553, 'Water lemon', 'The edible fruit of two species of passion flower Passiflora laurifolia and Passiflora maliformis    so called in the West Indies'),
(105554, 'Waterless', 'Destitute of water dry'),
(105555, 'Water lettuce', 'A plant Pistia stratiotes which floats on tropical waters and forms a rosette of spongy wedge shaped leaves'),
(105556, 'Water level', 'The level formed by the surface of still water'),
(105557, 'Water lily', 'A blossom or plant of any species of the genus Nymphaea distinguished for its large floating leaves and beautiful flowers  See Nymphaea'),
(105558, 'Water lime', 'Hydraulic lime'),
(105559, 'Water line', 'Any one of certain lines of a vessel model or plan parallel with the surface of the water at various heights from the keel'),
(105560, 'Water lizard', 'Any aquatic lizard of the genus Varanus as the monitor of the Nile  See Monitor n 3'),
(105561, 'Water locust', 'A thorny leguminous tree Gleditschia monosperma which grows in the swamps of the Mississippi valley'),
(105562, 'Water logged', 'Filled or saturated with water so as to be heavy unmanageable or loglike    said of a vessel when by receiving a great quantity of water into her hold she has become so heavy as not to be manageable by the helm'),
(105563, 'Watermanship', 'The business or skill of a waterman'),
(105564, 'Watermark', 'A mark indicating the height to which water has risen or at which it has stood the usual limit of high or low water'),
(105565, 'Water meadow', 'A meadow or piece of low flat land capable of being kept in a state of fertility by being overflowed with water from some adjoining river or stream'),
(105566, 'Water measure', 'A measure formerly used for articles brought by water as coals oysters etc The water measure bushel was three gallons larger than the Winchester bushel'),
(105567, 'Water measurer', 'Any one of numerous species of water the skater  See Skater n 2'),
(105568, 'Watermelon', 'The very large ovoid or roundish fruit of a cucurbitaceous plant Citrullus vulgaris of many varieties also the plant itself The fruit sometimes weighs many pounds its pulp is usually pink in color and full of a sweet watery juice It is a native of tropical Africa but is now cultivated in many countries  See Illust of Melon'),
(105569, 'Water meter', 'A contrivance for measuring a supply of water delivered or received for any purpose as from a street main'),
(105570, 'Water milfoil', 'Any plant of the genus Myriophyllum aquatic herbs with whorled leaves the submersed ones pinnately parted into capillary divisions'),
(105571, 'Water mill', 'A mill whose machinery is moved by water    distinguished from a windmill and a steam mill'),
(105572, 'Water mint', 'A kind of mint Mentha aquatica growing in wet places and sometimes having a perfume resembling bergamot'),
(105573, 'Water mite', 'Any of numerous species of aquatic mites belonging to Hydrachna and allied genera of the family Hydrachnidae usually having the legs fringed and adapted for swimming They are often red or red and black in color and while young are parasites of fresh water insects and mussels  Called also water tick and water spider'),
(105574, 'Water moccasin', 'A venomous North American snake Ancistrodon piscivorus allied to the rattlesnake but destitute of a rattle It lives in or about pools and ponds and feeds largely of fishes Called also water snake water adder water viper'),
(105575, 'Water mole', 'The shrew mole  See under Shrew'),
(105576, 'Water monitor', 'A very large lizard Varanaus salvator native of India It frequents the borders of streams and swims actively It becomes five or six feet long Called also two banded monitor and kabaragoya The name is also applied to other aquatic monitors'),
(105577, 'Water monkey', 'A jar or bottle as of porous earthenware in which water is cooled by evaporation'),
(105578, 'Water motor', 'A water engine'),
(105579, 'Water mouse', 'Any one of several species of mice belonging to the genus Hydromys native of Australia and Tasmania Their hind legs are strong and their toes partially webbed They live on the borders of streams and swim well They are remarkable as being the only rodents found in Australia'),
(105580, 'Water murrain', 'A kind of murrain affecting cattle'),
(105581, 'Water newt', 'Any one of numerous species of aquatic salamanders a triton'),
(105582, 'Water nymph', 'A goddess of any stream or other body of water whether one of the Naiads Nereids or Oceanides'),
(105583, 'Water oat', 'Indian rice  See under Rice'),
(105584, 'Water opossum', 'See Yapock and the Note under Opossum'),
(105585, 'Water ordeal', 'Same as Ordeal by water  See the Note under Ordeal n 1'),
(105586, 'Water ousel', 'Any one of several species of small insessorial birds of the genus Cinclus or Hydrobates especially the European water ousel Cinclus aquaticus and the American water ousel Cinclus Mexicanus These birds live about the water and are in the habit of walking on the bottom of streams beneath the water in search of food'),
(105587, 'Water parsnip', 'Any plant of the aquatic umbelliferous genus Sium poisonous herbs with pinnate or dissected leaves and small white flowers'),
(105588, 'Water parting', 'A summit from the opposite sides of which rain waters flow to different streams a line separating the drainage districts of two streams or coasts a divide a watershed'),
(105589, 'Water partridge', 'The ruddy duck'),
(105590, 'Water pennywort', 'Marsh pennywort  See under Marsh'),
(105591, 'Water pepper', 'Smartweed'),
(105592, 'Water pheasant', 'The pintail  See Pintail n 1'),
(105593, 'Water piet', 'The water ousel'),
(105594, 'Water pig', 'The capybara'),
(105595, 'Water pillar', 'A waterspout'),
(105596, 'Water pimpernel', 'A small white flowered shrub brookweed'),
(105597, 'Water pipe', 'A pipe for conveying water'),
(105598, 'Water pitcher', 'A pitcher for water'),
(105599, 'Water plant', 'A plant that grows in water an aquatic plant'),
(105600, 'Water plantain', 'A kind of plant with acrid leaves  See under 2d Plantain'),
(105601, 'Water plate', 'A plate heated by hot water contained in a double bottom or jacket'),
(105602, 'Water poa', 'Meadow reed grass  See under Reed'),
(105603, 'Water pocket', 'A water hole in the bed of an intermittent stream esp the bowl at the foot of a cliff over which the stream leaps when in the flood stage'),
(105604, 'Water poise', 'A hydrometer'),
(105605, 'Water pore', 'A pore by which the water tubes of various invertebrates open externally'),
(105606, 'Waterpot', 'A vessel for holding or conveying water or for sprinkling water on cloth plants etc'),
(105607, 'Water power', 'The power of water employed to move machinery etc'),
(105608, 'Water pox', 'A variety of chicken pox or varicella'),
(105609, 'Water privilege', 'The advantage of using water as a mechanical power also the place where water is or may be so used  See under Privilege'),
(105610, 'Waterproof', 'Proof against penetration or permeation by water impervious to water as a waterproof garment a waterproof roof'),
(105611, 'Waterproofing', 'The act or process of making waterproof'),
(105612, 'Water purslane', 'See under Purslane'),
(105613, 'Water qualm', 'See Water brash under Brash'),
(105614, 'Water rabbit', 'See Water hare'),
(105615, 'Water radish', 'A coarse yellow flowered plant Nasturtium amphibium related to the water cress and to the horse radish'),
(105616, 'Water rail', 'Any one of numerous species of rails of the genus Rallus as the common European species Rallus aquaticus  See Illust of Rail'),
(105617, 'Water ram', 'An hydraulic ram'),
(105618, 'Water rat', 'The water vole  See under Vole'),
(105619, 'Water rate', 'A rate or tax for a supply of water'),
(105620, 'Water rattle', 'The diamond rattlesnake Crotalus adamanteus    so called from its preference for damp places near water'),
(105621, 'Water ret', 'To ret or rot in water as flax to water rot'),
(105622, 'Water rice', 'Indian rice  See under Rice'),
(105623, 'Water rocket', 'A cruciferous plant Nasturtium sylvestre with small yellow flowers'),
(105624, 'Water rot', 'To rot by steeping in water to water ret as to water rot hemp or flax'),
(105625, 'Water sail', 'A small sail sometimes set under a studding sail or under a driver boom and reaching nearly to the water'),
(105626, 'Water sapphire', 'A deep blue variety of iolite sometimes used as a gem    called also saphir deau'),
(105627, 'Waterscape', 'A sea view    distinguished from landscape'),
(105628, 'Water scorpion', 'See Nepa'),
(105629, 'Water screw', 'A screw propeller'),
(105630, 'Water shield', 'An aquatic American plant Brasenia peltata having floating oval leaves and the covered with a clear jelly'),
(105631, 'Watershoot', 'A sprig or shoot from the root or stock of a tree'),
(105632, 'Water shrew', 'Any one of several species of shrews having fringed feet and capable of swimming actively The two common European species Crossopus fodiens and Crossopus ciliatus are the best known The most common American water shrew or marsh shrew Neosorex palustris is rarely seen owing to its nocturnal habits'),
(105633, 'Water snail', 'Any aquatic pulmonate gastropod belonging to Planorbis Limnaea and allied genera a pond snail'),
(105634, 'Water snake', 'A common North American colubrine snake Tropidonotus sipedon which lives chiefly in the water'),
(105635, 'Water soak', 'To soak water to fill the interstices of with water'),
(105636, 'Water soldier', 'An aquatic European plant Stratiotes aloides with bayonet shaped leaves'),
(105637, 'Water souchy', 'A dish consisting of small fish stewed and served in a little water'),
(105638, 'Water spaniel', 'A curly haired breed of spaniels naturally very fond of the water'),
(105639, 'Water sparrow', 'The reed warbler'),
(105640, 'Water speedwell', 'A kind of speedwell Veronica Anagallis found in wet places in Europe and America'),
(105641, 'Water spider', 'An aquatic European spider Argyoneta aquatica which constructs its web beneath the surface of the water on water plants It lives in a bell shaped structure of silk open beneath like a diving bell and filled with air which the spider carries down in the form of small bubbles attached one at a time to the spinnerets and hind feet Called also diving spider'),
(105642, 'Water spinner', 'The water spider'),
(105643, 'Waterspout', 'A remarkable meteorological phenomenon of the nature of a tornado or whirlwind usually observed over the sea but sometimes over the land'),
(105644, 'Water sprite', 'A sprite or spirit imagined as inhabiting the water'),
(105645, 'Water standing', 'Tear filled'),
(105646, 'Water star grass', 'An aquatic plant Schollera graminea with grassy leaves and yellow star shaped blossoms'),
(105647, 'Water starwort', 'See under Starwort'),
(105648, 'Water supply', 'A supply of water specifically water collected as in reservoirs and conveyed as by pipes for use in a city mill or the like'),
(105649, 'Water tabby', 'A kind of waved or watered tabby  See Tabby n 1'),
(105650, 'Water table', 'A molding or other projection in the wall of a building to throw off the water    generally used in the United States for the first table above the surface of the ground see Table n 9 that is for the table at the top of the foundation and the beginning of the upper wall'),
(105651, 'Watertath', 'A kind of coarse grass growing in wet grounds and supposed to be injurious to sheep'),
(105652, 'Water telescope', 'A telescope in which the medium between the objective and the eye piece is water instead of air used in some experiments in aberration'),
(105653, 'Water tender', 'In the United States navy a first class petty officer in charge in a fireroom He '),
(105654, 'Water thermometer', 'A thermometer filled with water instead of mercury for ascertaining the precise temperature at which water attains its maximum density This is about 39'),
(105655, 'Water thief', 'A pirate'),
(105656, 'Water thrush', 'A North American bird of the genus Seiurus belonging to the Warbler family especially the common species Seiurus Noveboracensis'),
(105657, 'Water thyme', 'See Anacharis'),
(105658, 'Water tick', 'Same as Water mite'),
(105659, 'Water tiger', 'A diving or water beetle especially the larva of a water beetle  See Illust b of Water beetle'),
(105660, 'Water tight', 'So tight as to retain or not to admit water not leaky'),
(105661, 'Water torch', 'The common cat tail Typha latifolia the spike of which makes a good torch soaked in oil'),
(105662, 'Water tower', 'A large metal pipe made to be extended vertically by sections and used for discharging water upon burning buildings'),
(105663, 'Water tree', 'A climbing shrub Tetracera alnifolia syn Tetracera potatoria of Western Africa which pours out a watery sap from the freshly cut stems'),
(105664, 'Water trefoil', 'The buck bean'),
(105665, 'Water tube', 'One of a system of tubular excretory organs having external openings found in many invertebrates  They are believed to be analogous in function to the kidneys of vertebrates  See Illust under Trematodea and Sporocyst'),
(105666, 'Water tupelo', 'A species of large tupelo Nyssa aquatica growing in swamps in the southern of the United States  See Ogeechee lime'),
(105667, 'Water turkey', 'The American snakebird  See Snakebird'),
(105668, 'Water tuy', 'A tuy'),
(105669, 'Water twist', 'Yarn made by the throstle or water frame'),
(105670, 'Water vine', 'Any plant of the genus Phytocrene climbing shrubs of Asia and Africa the stems of which are singularly porous and when cut stream with a limpid potable juice'),
(105671, 'Water violet', 'See under Violet'),
(105672, 'Water viper', 'See Water moccasin'),
(105673, 'Water vole', 'See under Vole'),
(105674, 'Water wagtail', 'See under Wagtail'),
(105675, 'Waterway', 'Heavy plank or timber extending fore and aft the whole length of a vessels deck at the line of junction with the sides forming a channel to the scuppers which are cut through it In iron vessels the waterway is variously constructed'),
(105676, 'Water way', 'Same as Water course'),
(105677, 'Waterweed', 'See Anacharis'),
(105678, 'Water wheel', 'Any wheel for propelling machinery or for other purposes that is made to rotate by the direct action of water    called an overshot wheel when the water is applied at the top an undershot wheel when at the bottom a breast wheel when at an intermediate point other forms are called reaction wheel vortex wheel turbine wheel etc'),
(105679, 'Water willow', 'An American aquatic plant Dianthera Americana with long willowlike leaves and spikes of small purplish flowers'),
(105680, 'Water wing', 'One of two walls built on either side of the junction of a bridge with the bank of a river to protect the abutment of the bridge and the bank from the action of the current'),
(105681, 'Water witch', 'The dabchick'),
(105682, 'Water withe', 'A vinelike plant Vitis Caribaea growing in parched districts in the West Indies and containing a great amount of sap which is sometimes used for quenching thirst'),
(105683, 'Waterwork', 'Painting executed in size or distemper on canvas or walls    formerly frequently taking the place of tapestry'),
(105684, 'Waterworn', 'Worn smoothed or polished by the action of water as waterworn stones'),
(105685, 'Waterwort', 'Any plant of the natural order Elatineae consisting of two genera Elatine and Bergia mostly small annual herbs growing in the edges of ponds Some have a peppery or acrid taste'),
(105686, 'Watt', 'A unit of power or activity equal to 107 CGS units of power or to work done at the rate of one joule a second  An English horse power is approximately equal to 746 watts'),
(105687, 'Watteau', 'Having the appearance of that which is seen in pictures by Antoine Watteau a French painter of the eighteenth century    said esp of womens garments as a Watteau bodice'),
(105688, 'Watteau back', 'The back of a womans gown in which one or more very broad folds are carried from the neck to the floor without being held in at the waist while the front and sides of the gown are shaped to the person and have a belt or its equivalent'),
(105689, 'Wattmeter', 'An instrument for measuring power in watts    much used in measuring the energy of an electric current'),
(105690, 'Wattlebird', 'Any one of several species of honey eaters belonging to Anthochaera and allied genera of the family Meliphagidae  These birds usually have a large and conspicuous wattle of naked skin hanging down below each ear They are natives of Australia and adjacent islands'),
(105691, 'Wattled', 'Furnished with wattles or pendent fleshy processes at the chin or throat'),
(105692, 'Wattless', 'Without any power cf Watt    said of an alternating current or component of current when it differs in phase by ninety degrees from the electromotive force which produces it or of an electromotive force or component thereof when the current it produces differs from it in phase by 90 degrees'),
(105693, 'Wattling', 'The act or process of binding or platting with twigs also the network so formed'),
(105694, 'Waucht', 'A large draught of any liquid'),
(105695, 'Waul', 'To cry as a cat to squall to wail'),
(105696, 'Waur', 'Worse'),
(105697, 'Wave', 'See Waive'),
(105698, 'Waved', 'Exhibiting a wavelike form or outline undulating intended wavy as waved edge'),
