(105858, 'Wedge tailed', 'Having a tail which has the middle pair of feathers longest the rest successively and decidedly shorter and all more or less attenuate    said of certain birds  See Illust of Wood hoopoe under Wood'),
(105859, 'Wedgewise', 'In the manner of a wedge'),
(105860, 'Wedgwood ware', 'A kind of fine pottery the most remarkable being what is called jasper either white or colored throughout the body and capable of being molded into the most delicate forms so that fine and minute bas reliefs like cameos were made of it fit even for being set as jewels'),
